{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011WLTwG1De9qigzGcr6ade8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Routing helps but the margin over rank-48 universal is modest\n2\t  (~6pp). Advantage is clear on Visual and Spatial but Physical shows limited routing\n3\t  benefit. Headline: \"~72% with ~12M params, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal.\"\n4\t  Reframe as \"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types requiring qualitatively\n5\t  different representation-level corrections.\" Physical perturbation is the weak link\n6\t  — LoRA-P underperforms because physical perturbations require action-space correction\n7\t  beyond representation-level adaptation. Rank sweep shows routing benefit depends\n8\t  on expert capacity (inflection at r≥32). Clean LIBERO shows minor degradation (-2pp).\n9\t  Oracle gap (5pp) suggests PAR router leaves room for improvement — discuss as future\n10\t  work. Paper is viable at CoRL with sharpened per-type framing and honest capacity\n11\t  analysis.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\n16\t  plan_id: plan_002\n17\t  description: 'Train LoRA-V/S/P (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers) on\n18\t    type-specific data plus universal LoRA (rank 16) on mixed data. Evaluate each\n19\t    expert in-type and cross-type. Visual experts trained with online augmentation\n20\t    on LIBERO demos; Spatial/Physical experts trained on oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus\n21\t    demos.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    lora_v_on_visual: 75.5\n26\t    lora_v_on_spatial: 63.1\n27\t    lora_v_on_physical: 59.8\n28\t    lora_s_on_visual: 62.4\n29\t    lora_s_on_spatial: 69.2\n30\t    lora_s_on_physical: 58.3\n31\t    lora_p_on_visual: 57.6\n32\t    lora_p_on_spatial: 57.2\n33\t    lora_p_on_physical: 61.0\n34\t    universal_r16_on_visual: 65.8\n35\t    universal_r16_on_spatial: 60.2\n36\t    universal_r16_on_physical: 55.4\n37\t    universal_r16_overall: 62.1\n38\t    avg_cross_type_mismatch_drop_pp: 10.8\n39\t    lora_v_cross_drop: 13.1\n40\t    lora_s_cross_drop: 9.0\n41\t    lora_p_cross_drop: 3.6\n42\t    training_converged_epochs_v: 15\n43\t    training_converged_epochs_s: 22\n44\t    training_converged_epochs_p: 30\n45\t    action_mse_val_lora_v: 0.0031\n46\t    action_mse_val_lora_s: 0.0039\n47\t    action_mse_val_lora_p: 0.0054\n48\t    action_mse_val_universal: 0.0042\n49\t  result_rationale: 'LoRA experts learn partially distinct corrections — cross-type\n50\t    drop exists but is asymmetric across types. LoRA-V shows strongest specialization\n51\t    (cross-drop ~13pp) because visual augmentation training creates a tight distribution\n52\t    match: the expert learns to invert specific visual corruptions (brightness shifts,\n53\t    background swaps) that are absent in spatial/physical data, so applying LoRA-V\n54\t    to non-visual inputs actively degrades representations by \"correcting\" features\n55\t    that weren''t corrupted. LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (~9pp cross-drop):\n56\t    layout perturbations alter mid-level spatial relationships that require qualitatively\n57\t    different corrections from visual corruptions, but there is partial overlap because\n58\t    spatial changes also affect visual appearance. LoRA-P is the weak link: only ~3.6pp\n59\t    cross-drop because physical perturbations (robot initial state) primarily affect\n60\t    action trajectories, not visual representations. LoRA-P''s correction at the representation\n61\t    level is modest (61% on-type, barely above universal''s 55.4%) — the signal for\n62\t    physical correction is weak at PaliGemma layer 6 where representations encode\n63\t    visual/spatial features better than proprioceptive state. Training convergence\n64\t    reflects this: LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong, clean signal), LoRA-P takes\n65\t    30 epochs with higher validation MSE (noisy, weak signal). The average cross-drop\n66\t    of ~10.8pp is in the median range — specialization exists but is less dramatic\n67\t    than best-case, driven mostly by Visual/Spatial divergence while Physical contributes\n68\t    little.\n69\t\n70\t    '\n71\t  tables:\n72\t  - title: Per-expert success rates (rows=expert, cols=evaluation perturbation type)\n73\t    content: '| Expert | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Avg Cross-type | Cross Δ |\n74\t\n75\t      |--------|--------|---------|----------|----------------|---------|\n76\t\n77\t      | LoRA-V | **75.5** | 63.1 | 59.8 | 61.5 | −13.1 |\n78\t\n79\t      | LoRA-S | 62.4 | **69.2** | 58.3 | 60.4 | −9.0 |\n80\t\n81\t      | LoRA-P | 57.6 | 57.2 | **61.0** | 57.4 | −3.6 |\n82\t\n83\t      | Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | — | — |\n84\t\n85\t      | Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | — | — |\n86\t\n87\t      '\n88\t  figures_description:\n89\t  - '3×3 heatmap of expert success rates: diagonal (matched) cells are brightest.\n90\t    LoRA-P row is nearly flat — minimal on-type advantage. LoRA-V row shows sharp\n91\t    contrast between on-type (bright) and off-type (dim).'\n92\t  - 'Training loss curves (4 subplots): LoRA-V converges fastest/smoothest; LoRA-P\n93\t    shows noisy plateau; LoRA-S intermediate; Universal converges to moderate loss.'\n94\t  analysis: 'Experts DO specialize, but unevenly. The asymmetry is the interesting\n95\t    finding: Visual and Spatial LoRA show strong type-specific corrections (cross-drops\n96\t    13pp and 9pp), while Physical LoRA shows minimal specialization (cross-drop 3.6pp).\n97\t    This maps to the mechanistic story: PaliGemma''s representation space strongly\n98\t    encodes visual and spatial information (it''s a vision-language model), but physical\n99\t    state (robot joint positions, force dynamics) is weakly represented. LoRA-P''s\n100\t    modest 61% on-type and low cross-drop suggest that rank-16 representation-level\n101\t    correction is insufficient for physical perturbations — this needs honest discussion\n102\t    in the paper. Universal r16 at 62.1% sets the parameter-unmatched baseline for\n103\t    ablation.\n104\t\n105\t    '\n106\t- name: Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\n107\t  plan_id: plan_013\n108\t  description: 'Train single universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params) on mixed all-perturbation\n109\t    data. Parameter-matched to Meta-Brain''s total budget. THE existential test for\n110\t    Claim 2.\n111\t\n112\t    '\n113\t  expected_results:\n114\t    rank48_universal_overall: 66.4\n115\t    rank48_on_visual: 70.1\n116\t    rank48_on_spatial: 64.3\n117\t    rank48_on_physical: 58.6\n118\t    rank48_vs_rank16_gain_pp: 4.3\n119\t    rank48_vs_meta_brain_gap_pp: -5.6\n120\t    claim2_survival: true\n121\t    training_epochs: 18\n122\t    action_mse_val: 0.0036\n123\t  result_rationale: 'Rank-48 universal captures most of the low-hanging correction\n124\t    accessible via parameter scaling alone. The +4.3pp gain over rank-16 (62.1→66.4)\n125\t    shows that extra capacity helps uniformly — but with sharply diminishing returns\n126\t    (3.2× the parameters for only 1.4× the gain-over-frozen). This diminishing return\n127\t    is because a single adapter learning mixed-perturbation data must find a compromise\n128\t    correction that partially works for all types but optimally corrects none. The\n129\t    rank-48 adapter effectively learns the average correction direction across perturbation\n130\t    types; it boosts visual most (+4.3pp) because visual perturbations are most common\n131\t    in training data and share the most overlap, while physical gains least (+3.2pp)\n132\t    because physical correction requires a qualitatively different direction. The\n133\t    66.4% vs Meta-Brain''s 72.0% leaves a 5.6pp gap — enough to validate Claim 2 but\n134\t    not dramatic. This gap is the routing premium: what you gain by allocating the\n135\t    same 12M parameters to three specialized experts with informed routing vs one\n136\t    universal adapter. The gap is largest on visual (70.1 vs 75.5, ~5.4pp) where specialization\n137\t    matters most and smallest on physical (58.6 vs 61.0, ~2.4pp) where even specialized\n138\t    LoRA-P barely outperforms.\n139\t\n140\t    '\n141\t  tables:\n142\t  - title: Rank-48 universal vs Meta-Brain per-type comparison\n143\t    content: '| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n144\t\n145\t      |--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n146\t\n147\t      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | 62.1 |\n148\t\n149\t      | Universal r48 | 12M | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 | 66.4 |\n150\t\n151\t      | Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n152\t\n153\t      | Δ (MB − r48) | 0 | +5.4 | +4.9 | +2.4 | +5.6 |\n154\t\n155\t      '\n156\t  figures_description:\n157\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: rank-48 vs Meta-Brain across 3 perturbation categories. Gap\n158\t    is clearly visible on Visual/Spatial, narrow on Physical.'\n159\t  analysis: 'Claim 2 survives — routing adds 5.6pp over parameter-matched universal.\n160\t    But the per-type decomposition tells the real story: routing earns its keep on\n161\t    Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp) where experts learn genuinely distinct corrections,\n162\t    but adds only +2.4pp on Physical where the expert itself is limited. Paper should\n163\t    frame: \"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types that require qualitatively\n164\t    different representation-level corrections; for perturbation types that primarily\n165\t    affect action-space dynamics (Physical), the routing advantage is modest.\"\n166\t\n167\t    '\n168\t- name: Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\n169\t  plan_id: plan_003\n170\t  description: 'Assemble full Meta-Brain system: PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, first-token,\n171\t    2-layer MLP) produces soft routing weights p(type_k). Corrected hidden state h_corrected\n172\t    = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning\n173\t    with frozen LoRA expert parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n174\t\n175\t    '\n176\t  expected_results:\n177\t    meta_brain_overall: 72.0\n178\t    meta_brain_visual: 75.5\n179\t    meta_brain_spatial: 69.2\n180\t    meta_brain_physical: 61.0\n181\t    meta_brain_camera: 78.0\n182\t    meta_brain_light: 79.2\n183\t    meta_brain_background: 71.0\n184\t    meta_brain_noise: 73.6\n185\t    meta_brain_layout: 69.2\n186\t    meta_brain_robot: 61.0\n187\t    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_v: 0.82\n188\t    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_s: 0.11\n189\t    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_p: 0.07\n190\t    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_s: 0.76\n191\t    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_v: 0.14\n192\t    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_p: 0.1\n193\t    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_p: 0.67\n194\t    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_v: 0.14\n195\t    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_s: 0.19\n196\t    routing_entropy_visual: 0.41\n197\t    routing_entropy_spatial: 0.52\n198\t    routing_entropy_physical: 0.72\n199\t    gain_over_frozen: 18.4\n200\t    gain_over_universal_r16: 9.9\n201\t    gain_over_universal_r48: 5.6\n202\t  result_rationale: 'Soft routing via PAR is accurate but introduces blending that\n203\t    partially dilutes expert specialization — this is the core median-tier mechanism.\n204\t    For visual perturbations, the router confidently assigns ~82% weight to LoRA-V\n205\t    (entropy 0.41) because Phase 0 showed visual perturbations create the most distinctive\n206\t    activation signatures at layer 6. The resulting 75.5% visual accuracy is strong\n207\t    but below the oracle ceiling because 18% of the correction comes from mismatched\n208\t    experts. For spatial, routing is slightly less decisive (76% to LoRA-S, entropy\n209\t    0.52) because some spatial perturbations (e.g., layout changes that also alter\n210\t    visual appearance) overlap with visual signatures, producing moderate blending\n211\t    and 69.2%. For physical, the router is least confident (67% to LoRA-P, entropy\n212\t    0.72) — physical perturbation signatures are weakest at PaliGemma layer 6 because\n213\t    this layer primarily encodes visual-spatial features, not proprioceptive state.\n214\t    The high entropy means ~33% of correction comes from LoRA-V/S, which applies visual/spatial\n215\t    corrections to physical inputs — not harmful but not helpful either. Physical\n216\t    at 61.0% is only +2.4pp above rank-48 universal (58.6%), making it the weak link.\n217\t    The overall 72.0% is a solid +18.4pp over frozen pi0 but the per-type gradient\n218\t    (75.5→69.2→61.0) is the interesting story. Routing confidence (entropy) directly\n219\t    predicts per-type success — this correlation supports confidence-gated routing\n220\t    as a practical recommendation.\n221\t\n222\t    '\n223\t  tables:\n224\t  - title: Meta-Brain per-perturbation success rates\n225\t    content: '| Perturbation | Category | Success (%) | Frozen pi0 (%) | Δ |\n226\t\n227\t      |-------------|----------|-------------|----------------|------|\n228\t\n229\t      | Camera jitter | Visual | 78.0 | 55.2 | +22.8 |\n230\t\n231\t      | Light change | Visual | 79.2 | 60.8 | +18.4 |\n232\t\n233\t      | Background swap | Visual | 71.0 | 54.0 | +17.0 |\n234\t\n235\t      | Sensor noise | Visual | 73.6 | 56.4 | +17.2 |\n236\t\n237\t      | Layout random | Spatial | 69.2 | 51.6 | +17.6 |\n238\t\n239\t      | Robot state | Physical | 61.0 | 47.2 | +13.8 |\n240\t\n241\t      | **Overall** | **—** | **72.0** | **53.6** | **+18.4** |\n242\t\n243\t      '\n244\t  - title: PAR routing weight distribution (per input category)\n245\t    content: '| Input Category | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\n246\t\n247\t      |---------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n248\t\n249\t      | Visual | **0.82** | 0.11 | 0.07 | 0.41 |\n250\t\n251\t      | Spatial | 0.14 | **0.76** | 0.10 | 0.52 |\n252\t\n253\t      | Physical | 0.14 | 0.19 | **0.67** | 0.72 |\n254\t\n255\t      '\n256\t  figures_description:\n257\t  - '3×3 routing weight heatmap: near-diagonal but imperfect. Visual row is sharpest\n258\t    (brightest diagonal). Physical row is most diffuse (weakest diagonal, visible\n259\t    off-diagonal mass).'\n260\t  - Per-perturbation bar chart with frozen baseline overlay. Visual subtypes cluster\n261\t    at 71-79%, spatial at 69%, physical at 61%. Clear gradient from left to right.\n262\t  - 'Scatter plot: routing entropy (x) vs per-category success rate (y). Three points\n263\t    form a tight negative correlation (r ≈ −0.98), supporting confidence-gated routing.'\n264\t  analysis: 'The routing weight heatmap is the key diagnostic figure. The entropy\n265\t    gradient (0.41→0.52→0.72) perfectly anti-correlates with success rate (75.5→69.2→61.0),\n266\t    suggesting routing confidence is a reliable proxy for expected correction quality.\n267\t    This motivates two practical recommendations: (1) confidence-gated routing — fall\n268\t    back to frozen when entropy exceeds a threshold (addresses clean LIBERO degradation),\n269\t    and (2) hard routing at deployment — if soft routing blending dilutes specialization,\n270\t    temperature-sharpened or argmax routing may close part of the oracle gap. The\n271\t    physical category''s high entropy and low success rate is the honest limitation:\n272\t    Meta-Brain''s representation-level correction has diminishing returns for perturbation\n273\t    types that primarily require action-space adaptation.\n274\t\n275\t    '\n276\t- name: Oracle routing ablation — upper bound\n277\t  plan_id: plan_004\n278\t  description: 'Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard\n279\t    one-hot routing). Measures the gap between learned soft routing and perfect hard\n280\t    routing — upper bound for routing quality.\n281\t\n282\t    '\n283\t  expected_results:\n284\t    oracle_overall: 77.0\n285\t    oracle_visual: 81.8\n286\t    oracle_spatial: 74.6\n287\t    oracle_physical: 66.4\n288\t    oracle_minus_meta_brain_overall: 5.0\n289\t    oracle_minus_meta_brain_visual: 6.3\n290\t    oracle_minus_meta_brain_spatial: 5.4\n291\t    oracle_minus_meta_brain_physical: 5.4\n292\t  result_rationale: 'Oracle routing eliminates soft routing blending — each input\n293\t    gets 100% weight on the matched expert. The 5.0pp overall gap over Meta-Brain\n294\t    comes from two compounding effects: (1) eliminating dilution from mismatched expert\n295\t    blending (accounts for ~3pp — the 18-33% off-diagonal mass in PAR routing now\n296\t    goes to zero), and (2) perfect type assignment on ambiguous boundary cases where\n297\t    PAR misroutes (accounts for ~2pp — PAR''s 0.996 F1 still makes errors on a few\n298\t    percent of timesteps, and these compound over 44-step episodes). The gap is roughly\n299\t    uniform across types (5.0-6.3pp) rather than concentrated on physical, suggesting\n300\t    the PAR router''s limitation is general blending behavior, not systematic failure\n301\t    on any specific type. Oracle physical at 66.4% confirms that even perfect routing\n302\t    cannot push physical above the expert''s ceiling — LoRA-P itself is the bottleneck,\n303\t    not the routing. The 77.0% oracle sets a meaningful upper bound: the 3-expert\n304\t    architecture has 5pp more headroom, achievable through sharper routing (temperature\n305\t    annealing, hard routing, or better router architectures).\n306\t\n307\t    '\n308\t  tables:\n309\t  - title: Oracle vs PAR routing comparison\n310\t    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n311\t\n312\t      |--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n313\t\n314\t      | Meta-Brain (PAR) | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n315\t\n316\t      | Oracle routing | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 | 77.0 |\n317\t\n318\t      | Δ | +6.3 | +5.4 | +5.4 | +5.0 |\n319\t\n320\t      '\n321\t  figures_description:\n322\t  - 'Paired bar chart: Meta-Brain vs Oracle per category, gap annotated. Uniform ~5pp\n323\t    gap across all types.'\n324\t  analysis: 'The oracle gap of 5.0pp is moderate — PAR captures most of the routing\n325\t    value (72/77 = 93.5% of oracle performance) but leaves room for improvement. The\n326\t    uniformity of the gap across types suggests that improving routing sharpness (e.g.,\n327\t    lower-temperature softmax, curriculum-based routing training) would benefit all\n328\t    categories equally. Present as concrete future work: \"hard routing or temperature-annealed\n329\t    routing could recover an estimated 2-3pp of the 5pp oracle gap.\" The oracle at\n330\t    77% also contextualizes the absolute performance ceiling of the 3-expert architecture\n331\t    — further gains require stronger individual experts (higher rank) or additional\n332\t    experts.\n333\t\n334\t    '\n335\t- name: Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n336\t  plan_id: plan_005\n337\t  description: 'Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal\n338\t    r16, Random routing (3×r16 experts), Universal r48 (parameter-matched), Meta-Brain,\n339\t    Oracle routing. Report overall + per-perturbation breakdown with standard deviations\n340\t    and statistical significance.\n341\t\n342\t    '\n343\t  expected_results:\n344\t    frozen_overall: 53.6\n345\t    frozen_std: 2.1\n346\t    universal_r16_overall: 62.1\n347\t    universal_r16_std: 1.8\n348\t    universal_r48_overall: 66.4\n349\t    universal_r48_std: 1.6\n350\t    random_routing_overall: 64.2\n351\t    random_routing_std: 2.5\n352\t    meta_brain_overall: 72.0\n353\t    meta_brain_std: 1.9\n354\t    oracle_overall: 77.0\n355\t    oracle_std: 1.4\n356\t    meta_brain_vs_random_gap: 7.8\n357\t    meta_brain_vs_r48_gap: 5.6\n358\t    meta_brain_vs_frozen_gain: 18.4\n359\t    random_vs_r16_gap: 2.1\n360\t    random_vs_r48_gap: -2.2\n361\t    p_value_mb_vs_random: 0.003\n362\t    p_value_mb_vs_r48: 0.008\n363\t    p_value_mb_vs_r16: <0.001\n364\t  result_rationale: 'The ablation ladder forms a clean monotonic ordering: Frozen\n365\t    (53.6) < r16 (62.1) < Random (64.2) < r48 (66.4) < Meta-Brain (72.0) < Oracle\n366\t    (77.0). Each step isolates a specific factor:\n367\t\n368\t    Frozen→r16 (+8.5pp): LoRA adaptation on perturbed data helps, even with limited\n369\t    capacity and no specialization. This is the \"any correction is better than none\"\n370\t    baseline gain.\n371\t\n372\t    r16→Random (+2.1pp): Random routing gives 1/3 chance of the right expert, plus\n373\t    the ensemble effect of having 3× the parameters (12M vs 3.7M). The modest +2.1pp\n374\t    shows the ensemble effect alone is minor — randomly applying the wrong expert\n375\t    often hurts more than the extra capacity helps.\n376\t\n377\t    Random→r48 (−2.2pp): Random routing actually underperforms the parameter-matched\n378\t    universal r48. This is important: 12M parameters concentrated in one coherent\n379\t    adapter (r48) outperform 12M parameters split across 3 randomly-assigned experts.\n380\t    Random routing''s 2/3 mismatch penalty exceeds its 1/3 specialization benefit.\n381\t    This directly supports the claim that INFORMED routing is necessary — mere expert\n382\t    diversity without correct assignment is worse than a single universal adapter.\n383\t\n384\t    r48→Meta-Brain (+5.6pp): The routing premium. Informed routing turns the 3-expert\n385\t    architecture from a liability (random: −2.2pp vs r48) into a significant advantage\n386\t    (+5.6pp vs r48). This is the core evidence for Claim 2.\n387\t\n388\t    Meta-Brain→Oracle (+5.0pp): Room for routing improvement. The PAR router captures\n389\t    most of the value but soft blending leaves 5pp on the table.\n390\t\n391\t    Statistical significance: Meta-Brain vs random (p=0.003) and vs r48 (p=0.008)\n392\t    are significant at α=0.01 across 5 seeds. Random routing has the highest variance\n393\t    (std=2.5) due to stochastic expert assignment; oracle has the lowest (std=1.4)\n394\t    due to deterministic correct routing.\n395\t\n396\t    '\n397\t  tables:\n398\t  - title: Ablation ladder — LIBERO-Plus overall success rate (5 seeds)\n399\t    content: '| Method | Params | Overall (%) | Std | Δ vs Frozen | Δ vs r48 |\n400\t\n401\t      |--------|--------|------------|-----|-------------|----------|\n402\t\n403\t      | Frozen pi0 | 0 | 53.6 | 2.1 | — | −12.8 |\n404\t\n405\t      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | 62.1 | 1.8 | +8.5 | −4.3 |\n406\t\n407\t      | Random routing | 12M | 64.2 | 2.5 | +10.6 | −2.2 |\n408\t\n409\t      | Universal r48 | 12M | 66.4 | 1.6 | +12.8 | — |\n410\t\n411\t      | **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **72.0** | **1.9** | **+18.4** | **+5.6** |\n412\t\n413\t      | Oracle routing | 12M | 77.0 | 1.4 | +23.4 | +10.6 |\n414\t\n415\t      '\n416\t  - title: Per-perturbation-category breakdown\n417\t    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\n418\t\n419\t      |--------|--------|---------|----------|\n420\t\n421\t      | Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 |\n422\t\n423\t      | Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 |\n424\t\n425\t      | Random routing | 67.8 | 62.4 | 57.0 |\n426\t\n427\t      | Universal r48 | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 |\n428\t\n429\t      | Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 |\n430\t\n431\t      | Oracle | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 |\n432\t\n433\t      '\n434\t  - title: Statistical significance (pairwise p-values, two-sided t-test, 5 seeds)\n435\t    content: '| Comparison | Δ (pp) | p-value | Significant (α=0.01) |\n436\t\n437\t      |-----------|--------|---------|---------------------|\n438\t\n439\t      | MB vs Frozen | +18.4 | <0.001 | ✓ |\n440\t\n441\t      | MB vs r16 | +9.9 | <0.001 | ✓ |\n442\t\n443\t      | MB vs Random | +7.8 | 0.003 | ✓ |\n444\t\n445\t      | MB vs r48 | +5.6 | 0.008 | ✓ |\n446\t\n447\t      | Oracle vs MB | +5.0 | 0.011 | ✗ (marginal) |\n448\t\n449\t      | Random vs r16 | +2.1 | 0.14 | ✗ |\n450\t\n451\t      | Random vs r48 | −2.2 | 0.18 | ✗ |\n452\t\n453\t      '\n454\t  figures_description:\n455\t  - 'Ablation ladder bar chart (ascending): 6 bars from Frozen to Oracle with error\n456\t    bars (±1 std). Monotonic increase. The r48↔Random ordering is the subtle but important\n457\t    finding — Random slightly below r48.'\n458\t  - 'Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 6 methods × 3 categories. The Physical column\n459\t    shows compressed spacing between methods — limited routing benefit. Visual column\n460\t    shows the widest spread.'\n461\t  - 'Waterfall/decomposition chart: each step from Frozen→Oracle annotated with the\n462\t    factor it isolates (adaptation, ensemble, parameter scaling, informed routing,\n463\t    perfect routing).'\n464\t  analysis: 'The ablation ladder is the paper''s core figure. Critical narrative points:\n465\t    (1) Random < r48 is the surprise result — it proves that routing QUALITY matters,\n466\t    not just expert diversity. Without this, a reviewer could argue Meta-Brain''s\n467\t    gains are just ensemble effects. (2) Meta-Brain vs r48 (+5.6pp, p=0.008) is statistically\n468\t    significant but requires careful framing: \"modest but reliable routing premium.\"\n469\t    (3) The per-type breakdown shows Physical compresses the ladder — all methods\n470\t    are closer together on Physical than on Visual/Spatial. This is the honest story:\n471\t    routing helps most where representation-level corrections differ qualitatively\n472\t    by type. (4) Oracle−MB significance is marginal (p=0.011) — the paper can discuss\n473\t    the oracle gap as suggestive rather than definitive.\n474\t\n475\t    '\n476\t- name: FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\n477\t  plan_id: plan_006\n478\t  description: 'Reproduce FTM on pi0 backbone for visual perturbation types. Compare\n479\t    Meta-Brain''s visual performance vs FTM. Establish that Meta-Brain matches visual-only\n480\t    methods on visual while additionally covering spatial/physical.\n481\t\n482\t    '\n483\t  expected_results:\n484\t    ftm_visual_overall: 73.8\n485\t    ftm_camera: 76.4\n486\t    ftm_light: 78.2\n487\t    ftm_background: 67.8\n488\t    ftm_noise: 72.6\n489\t    ftm_spatial: 52.4\n490\t    ftm_physical: 47.8\n491\t    ftm_overall: 60.8\n492\t    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n493\t    meta_brain_visual_overall: 75.5\n494\t    meta_brain_vs_ftm_visual_delta: 1.7\n495\t    meta_brain_vs_ftm_spatial_delta: 16.8\n496\t    meta_brain_vs_ftm_physical_delta: 13.2\n497\t  result_rationale: 'FTM is purpose-built for visual robustness with extreme parameter\n498\t    efficiency (~4K params), so it achieves strong 73.8% on visual perturbations —\n499\t    only 1.7pp below Meta-Brain''s 75.5%. This is expected: FTM''s fine-tuned visual\n500\t    transforms directly target the same representation corruption that LoRA-V corrects,\n501\t    and 4K purpose-designed parameters can be more efficient than 3.7M generic LoRA\n502\t    parameters for a narrow task. However, FTM on spatial (52.4%) and physical (47.8%)\n503\t    is at or below frozen pi0 level — its visual-only training provides zero spatial/physical\n504\t    correction and occasionally interferes (physical 47.8% vs frozen 47.2% suggests\n505\t    slight degradation from applying visual transforms to non-visual inputs). FTM''s\n506\t    background swap result (67.8%) is notably lower than its camera/light/noise results\n507\t    because background swaps introduce novel textures that FTM''s limited capacity\n508\t    cannot fully absorb. Meta-Brain''s value proposition is clear: comparable visual\n509\t    performance (+1.7pp) plus +16.8pp on spatial and +13.2pp on physical that FTM\n510\t    cannot touch.\n511\t\n512\t    '\n513\t  tables:\n514\t  - title: FTM vs Meta-Brain comparison\n515\t    content: '| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n516\t\n517\t      |--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n518\t\n519\t      | Frozen pi0 | 0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | 53.6 |\n520\t\n521\t      | FTM | 4K | 73.8 | 52.4 | 47.8 | 60.8 |\n522\t\n523\t      | Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n524\t\n525\t      | Δ (MB − FTM) | — | +1.7 | +16.8 | +13.2 | +11.2 |\n526\t\n527\t      '\n528\t  figures_description:\n529\t  - 'Radar/spider chart: FTM vs Meta-Brain across 6 perturbation types. FTM is competitive\n530\t    on the 4 visual axes but collapses to frozen-level on spatial/physical.'\n531\t  analysis: 'FTM comparison reinforces the multi-type robustness narrative. Do NOT\n532\t    claim Meta-Brain beats FTM on visual — the 1.7pp gap is not significant and FTM\n533\t    achieves this with 3000× fewer parameters. Instead frame: \"Meta-Brain matches\n534\t    purpose-built visual methods while providing broad perturbation coverage that\n535\t    single-modality approaches cannot. The parameter cost (12M vs 4K) is the price\n536\t    of multi-type generality.\"\n537\t\n538\t    '\n539\t- name: Parameter efficiency analysis\n540\t  plan_id: plan_007\n541\t  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters.\n542\t    Generate scatter plot comparing Meta-Brain, Universal variants, FTM, VLA-GSE.\n543\t\n544\t    '\n545\t  expected_results:\n546\t    meta_brain_gain_pp: 18.4\n547\t    meta_brain_params_M: 12\n548\t    meta_brain_gain_per_Mparam: 1.53\n549\t    universal_r16_gain_pp: 8.5\n550\t    universal_r16_params_M: 3.7\n551\t    universal_r16_gain_per_Mparam: 2.3\n552\t    universal_r48_gain_pp: 12.8\n553\t    universal_r48_params_M: 12\n554\t    universal_r48_gain_per_Mparam: 1.07\n555\t    ftm_gain_pp_visual_only: 16.0\n556\t    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n557\t    ftm_gain_per_Mparam: 4000.0\n558\t    ftm_gain_pp_overall: 7.2\n559\t    ftm_gain_per_Mparam_overall: 1800.0\n560\t    vla_gse_gain_pp: 27.6\n561\t    vla_gse_params_M: 114\n562\t    vla_gse_gain_per_Mparam: 0.24\n563\t    vla_gse_note: different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), indirect comparison only\n564\t  result_rationale: 'Parameter efficiency depends on what you measure. FTM dominates\n565\t    gain-per-param on visual-only (4000 gain/Mparam) because it was designed for exactly\n566\t    this — but its overall gain-per-param (1800) still leads because overall averages\n567\t    include the visual types where FTM excels. Universal r16 has better gain-per-param\n568\t    (2.30) than Meta-Brain (1.53) because smaller models have an efficiency advantage\n569\t    on the low-hanging-fruit gains. The critical comparison is r48 (1.07) vs Meta-Brain\n570\t    (1.53): at the same parameter budget, routing gives 43% better efficiency than\n571\t    uniform adaptation. VLA-GSE at 0.24 gain/Mparam (on a different backbone, hence\n572\t    indirect) shows that Meta-Brain is ~6.4× more parameter-efficient in gain-per-param\n573\t    terms. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain above the \"universal LoRA scaling\n574\t    line\" (connecting r16 and r48 points), visually demonstrating that routing adds\n575\t    value beyond what parameter scaling alone provides.\n576\t\n577\t    '\n578\t  tables:\n579\t  - title: Parameter efficiency comparison\n580\t    content: '| Method | Params | Gain over frozen | Gain/Mparam | Coverage |\n581\t\n582\t      |--------|--------|-----------------|-------------|----------|\n583\t\n584\t      | FTM | 4K | +16.0pp (visual) | 4000.0 | Visual only |\n585\t\n586\t      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | +8.5pp | 2.30 | All (uniform) |\n587\t\n588\t      | **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **+18.4pp** | **1.53** | **All (specialized)**\n589\t      |\n590\t\n591\t      | Universal r48 | 12M | +12.8pp | 1.07 | All (uniform) |\n592\t\n593\t      | VLA-GSE* | 114M | +27.6pp* | 0.24* | All (capacity) |\n594\t\n595\t      '\n596\t  figures_description:\n597\t  - 'Log-scale scatter: x=trainable params (log), y=gain-over-frozen (%). Meta-Brain\n598\t    sits above the r16→r48 scaling line. FTM is a high-efficiency outlier (visual\n599\t    only, flagged). VLA-GSE is marked with asterisk (different backbone). Dashed lines\n600\t    connect parameter-matched pairs (r48, Meta-Brain).'\n601\t  analysis: 'The scatter plot is the Claim 3 figure. Meta-Brain''s position above\n602\t    the universal LoRA scaling line visually demonstrates the routing premium. However,\n603\t    FTM''s dominance on visual-only efficiency needs honest treatment — acknowledge\n604\t    that for deployments facing only visual perturbations, FTM is more efficient.\n605\t    Meta-Brain''s value is multi-type coverage with routing specialization. VLA-GSE\n606\t    comparison must carry the \"different backbone\" caveat prominently — apples-to-oranges\n607\t    with asterisk, not a direct parameter efficiency claim.\n608\t\n609\t    '\n610\t- name: Correction Memory ablation\n611\t  plan_id: plan_008\n612\t  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain ± Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing\n613\t    weights based on FAISS-retrieved exemplars. Quantify marginal contribution.\n614\t\n615\t    '\n616\t  expected_results:\n617\t    meta_brain_no_cm_overall: 72.0\n618\t    meta_brain_with_cm_overall: 73.2\n619\t    cm_delta_overall: 1.2\n620\t    cm_delta_visual: 1.4\n621\t    cm_delta_spatial: 1.2\n622\t    cm_delta_physical: 0.8\n623\t    cm_p_value: 0.12\n624\t    cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 2.3\n625\t    cm_alpha_learned: 0.15\n626\t  result_rationale: 'CM provides a marginal +1.2pp improvement that is not statistically\n627\t    significant (p=0.12 across 5 seeds). The learned alpha=0.15 means CM modulates\n628\t    routing weights by only 15% at most — the base PAR router already does most of\n629\t    the work. The per-type pattern follows the same gradient as all other results:\n630\t    visual benefits slightly more (+1.4pp) because visual perturbation exemplars are\n631\t    most distinctive in representation space and FAISS retrieval is most accurate.\n632\t    Physical benefits least (+0.8pp) because physical perturbation representations\n633\t    are less separable, so retrieved exemplars are noisier. The 2.3ms retrieval latency\n634\t    is negligible but the architectural complexity (FAISS index, exemplar storage,\n635\t    alpha parameter) is not justified by 1.2pp. Dream reviewers unanimously flagged\n636\t    CM as padding — this ablation confirms that assessment.\n637\t\n638\t    '\n639\t  tables:\n640\t  - title: Correction Memory ablation\n641\t    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | p-value |\n642\t\n643\t      |--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|---------|\n644\t\n645\t      | Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 | — |\n646\t\n647\t      | Meta-Brain + CM | 76.9 | 70.4 | 61.8 | 73.2 | 0.12 |\n648\t\n649\t      | Δ CM | +1.4 | +1.2 | +0.8 | +1.2 | n.s. |\n650\t\n651\t      '\n652\t  figures_description:\n653\t  - Minimal paired bar chart showing CM adds small, non-significant improvement. Error\n654\t    bars overlap.\n655\t  analysis: 'CM is confirmed as ablation footnote material. Present as: \"An optional\n656\t    Correction Memory module provides +1.2pp (n.s.) by modulating routing weights\n657\t    with retrieved exemplars. We include this result for completeness but recommend\n658\t    the CM-free architecture for deployment simplicity.\" Do NOT elevate CM in the\n659\t    architecture diagram or contribution list.\n660\t\n661\t    '\n662\t- name: Router architecture comparison\n663\t  plan_id: plan_009\n664\t  description: 'Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP (default), linear classifier,\n665\t    attention- based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6, first-token pooling.\n666\t\n667\t    '\n668\t  expected_results:\n669\t    linear_routing_f1: 0.988\n670\t    linear_downstream_success: 70.6\n671\t    linear_params_M: 0.1\n672\t    mlp_routing_f1: 0.996\n673\t    mlp_downstream_success: 72.0\n674\t    mlp_params_M: 0.5\n675\t    attention_routing_f1: 0.997\n676\t    attention_downstream_success: 72.4\n677\t    attention_params_M: 0.8\n678\t  result_rationale: 'The routing classification task is nearly saturated (Phase 0\n679\t    F1=0.993), so router architecture differences are small. Linear classifier''s\n680\t    slight accuracy gap (F1 0.988 vs 0.996) translates to ~1.4pp downstream loss:\n681\t    the 0.8% additional routing errors occur on ambiguous boundary cases (e.g., background\n682\t    swaps that also affect spatial layout), and these errors compound over multi-step\n683\t    rollouts (a single misroute early in an episode can cascade). Attention-based\n684\t    router marginally improves (F1 0.997, +0.4pp downstream) by better handling these\n685\t    boundary cases through cross-dimensional attention on the 2048-dim PaliGemma features,\n686\t    but the gain doesn''t justify 60% more parameters. The 2-layer MLP is Pareto-optimal:\n687\t    near-perfect routing at minimal overhead.\n688\t\n689\t    '\n690\t  tables:\n691\t  - title: Router architecture comparison\n692\t    content: '| Router | Params | Routing F1 | Downstream (%) | Δ vs MLP |\n693\t\n694\t      |--------|--------|-----------|----------------|----------|\n695\t\n696\t      | Linear | 0.1M | 0.988 | 70.6 | −1.4 |\n697\t\n698\t      | 2-layer MLP | 0.5M | 0.996 | 72.0 | — |\n699\t\n700\t      | Attention | 0.8M | 0.997 | 72.4 | +0.4 |\n701\t\n702\t      '\n703\t  figures_description:\n704\t  - 'Small 3-point scatter: x=router params, y=downstream success. Diminishing returns\n705\t    beyond MLP.'\n706\t  analysis: 'Router architecture is not a bottleneck. Present as a single sentence\n707\t    in the ablation section: \"We compared linear, MLP, and attention-based routers;\n708\t    the 2-layer MLP provides the best complexity-performance tradeoff (Table N).\"\n709\t\n710\t    '\n711\t- name: 6-class vs 3-class routing\n712\t  plan_id: plan_010\n713\t  description: 'Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot,\n714\t    6 LoRA experts) vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts).\n715\t\n716\t    '\n717\t  expected_results:\n718\t    class3_overall: 72.0\n719\t    class3_params_M: 12\n720\t    class6_overall: 72.6\n721\t    class6_params_M: 24\n722\t    marginal_gain_per_expert: 0.2\n723\t    class6_camera: 79.3\n724\t    class6_light: 80.0\n725\t    class6_background: 72.8\n726\t    class6_noise: 75.0\n727\t    class6_layout: 69.6\n728\t    class6_robot: 61.4\n729\t  result_rationale: '6-class routing with 6 experts provides only +0.6pp over 3-class\n730\t    at 2× the parameters. This is expected from Phase 0: intra-Visual subtypes cluster\n731\t    tightly in activation space (6-class accuracy 99.82% shows they''re separable,\n732\t    but 3-class accuracy 99.46% shows the grouping is already natural). The marginal\n733\t    gain per additional expert (0.2pp) is negligible. Within visual subtypes, background\n734\t    swap benefits most from its own expert (+1.8pp: 72.8 vs 71.0) because background\n735\t    textures are the most visually distinct subtype, but camera/light/noise show <1pp\n736\t    gains over being grouped. Spatial and physical are already singleton categories\n737\t    so gain is only from having dedicated visual subtype experts freeing up minor\n738\t    capacity. The 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA\n739\t    processing (early/mid/late layers) and is the right granularity.\n740\t\n741\t    '\n742\t  tables:\n743\t  - title: 6-class vs 3-class routing\n744\t    content: '| Routing | Experts | Params | Overall | Δ vs 3-class |\n745\t\n746\t      |---------|---------|--------|---------|-------------|\n747\t\n748\t      | 3-class | 3 | 12M | 72.0 | — |\n749\t\n750\t      | 6-class | 6 | 24M | 72.6 | +0.6 |\n751\t\n752\t      '\n753\t  figures_description:\n754\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3-class vs 6-class per perturbation subtype. Nearly identical\n755\t    bars except background swap shows a small 6-class advantage.'\n756\t  analysis: 'Directly addresses reviewer concern C5. 3-class is sufficient — 6-class\n757\t    doubles parameters for 0.6pp. Background swap is the only subtype that meaningfully\n758\t    benefits from its own expert, but not enough to justify the complexity. If a reviewer\n759\t    pushes, point to Phase 0 intra-Visual clustering as the mechanistic justification\n760\t    for grouping.\n761\t\n762\t    '\n763\t- name: LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity-routing interaction\n764\t  plan_id: plan_014\n765\t  description: 'Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity. At each rank\n766\t    R, compare 3 type-specific rank-R experts with PAR routing vs parameter-matched\n767\t    universal LoRA (rank 3R). Measures capacity-routing interaction.\n768\t\n769\t    '\n770\t  expected_results:\n771\t    r16_routed_overall: 72.0\n772\t    r16_universal_matched_overall: 66.4\n773\t    r16_routing_benefit: 5.6\n774\t    r32_routed_overall: 75.4\n775\t    r32_universal_matched_overall: 68.6\n776\t    r32_routing_benefit: 6.8\n777\t    r64_routed_overall: 78.2\n778\t    r64_universal_matched_overall: 70.4\n779\t    r64_routing_benefit: 7.8\n780\t    capacity_threshold: r≥32\n781\t    r32_total_params_M: 24\n782\t    r64_total_params_M: 48\n783\t  result_rationale: 'Routing benefit is capacity-dependent, growing from 5.6pp (r16)\n784\t    to 6.8pp (r32) to 7.8pp (r64). The mechanism: at low rank (r16), each expert has\n785\t    limited capacity to specialize — the rank-16 LoRA subspace is constrained enough\n786\t    that specialized and universal corrections partially overlap (both learn \"general\n787\t    robustness\" corrections that consume most of the limited rank budget, leaving\n788\t    little room for type-specific corrections). At rank 32, experts have enough capacity\n789\t    to learn the general correction AND type-specific refinements — this is where\n790\t    specialization becomes meaningfully different from universality, producing the\n791\t    inflection from 5.6→6.8pp. At rank 64, further capacity allows even finer specialization\n792\t    but with diminishing marginal routing benefit (6.8→7.8pp, only +1.0pp more). The\n793\t    universal LoRA scaling line (66.4→68.6→70.4) shows steady but decelerating gains\n794\t    from rank alone, while the routed line (72.0→75.4→78.2) scales faster because\n795\t    additional rank is leveraged differently by each expert. At r64, the routed system\n796\t    (78.2%) approaches the rank-16 oracle level (77.0%), suggesting that higher-capacity\n797\t    experts can partially compensate for imperfect routing. The capacity threshold\n798\t    of r≥32 is a concrete, actionable finding: \"routing requires a minimum expert\n799\t    capacity to be beneficial beyond parameter scaling.\"\n800\t\n801\t    '\n802\t  tables:\n803\t  - title: 'Rank sweep: routing benefit vs expert capacity'\n804\t    content: '| Expert Rank | 3×Expert (Routed) | Universal (Matched) | Routing Δ\n805\t      | Total Params |\n806\t\n807\t      |-------------|-------------------|--------------------|-----------|----|\n808\t\n809\t      | r=16 | 72.0% | 66.4% | +5.6pp | 12M |\n810\t\n811\t      | r=32 | 75.4% | 68.6% | +6.8pp | 24M |\n812\t\n813\t      | r=64 | 78.2% | 70.4% | +7.8pp | 48M |\n814\t\n815\t      '\n816\t  figures_description:\n817\t  - 'Dual-line plot: x=expert rank, y=overall success rate. ''Routed'' line (blue)\n818\t    and ''Universal matched'' line (orange). Gap widens from r16 to r32 (inflection),\n819\t    then continues at reduced slope to r64. Shaded area between lines = routing benefit.'\n820\t  - 'Single-line plot: x=expert rank, y=routing benefit (Δ pp). Shows the inflection\n821\t    at r32 and plateau toward r64.'\n822\t  analysis: 'The rank sweep adds a concrete finding to the paper: \"routing benefit\n823\t    scales with expert capacity, with an inflection at r≥32.\" At our operating point\n824\t    (r16), routing benefit is 5.6pp — present as the minimum viable routing advantage.\n825\t    The implication: practitioners with larger compute budgets should increase expert\n826\t    rank to maximize the routing premium. The r64 routed result (78.2%) exceeding\n827\t    the r16 oracle (77.0%) is a nice finding — higher-capacity experts + learned routing\n828\t    can surpass perfect-routing of lower-capacity experts. Frame as: \"capacity and\n829\t    routing are complementary dimensions of adaptation.\"\n830\t\n831\t    '\n832\t- name: Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\n833\t  plan_id: plan_015\n834\t  description: 'Evaluate Meta-Brain on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO benchmark. Verify\n835\t    that routing does not significantly degrade nominal performance.\n836\t\n837\t    '\n838\t  expected_results:\n839\t    frozen_pi0_clean_libero: 92.4\n840\t    meta_brain_clean_libero: 90.4\n841\t    clean_delta_pp: -2.0\n842\t    universal_r16_clean_libero: 91.6\n843\t    universal_r48_clean_libero: 91.2\n844\t    oracle_clean_libero: 91.0\n845\t    par_entropy_on_clean: 0.88\n846\t    par_max_weight_on_clean: 0.48\n847\t    par_routing_distribution_clean: roughly uniform (0.35, 0.31, 0.34)\n848\t  result_rationale: 'Meta-Brain shows -2.0pp degradation on clean LIBERO (90.4% vs\n849\t    frozen 92.4%). The mechanism: on clean inputs, no perturbation correction is needed,\n850\t    but the PAR router still activates with non-trivial confidence. PAR entropy on\n851\t    clean inputs is high (0.88 — near maximum entropy of ln(3)=1.10) and the routing\n852\t    distribution is roughly uniform (0.35/0.31/0.34), meaning the router is uncertain\n853\t    about which type to route to (correct behavior — there IS no perturbation type).\n854\t    However, the near-uniform routing still applies a weighted average of all three\n855\t    expert corrections (each contributing ~33%), introducing small representation\n856\t    shifts that are not correcting any actual perturbation. These spurious corrections\n857\t    occasionally push the policy off its optimal trajectory, causing -2.0pp degradation.\n858\t    Universal LoRA variants show smaller degradation (-0.8pp for r16, -1.2pp for r48)\n859\t    because their single correction direction is less disruptive when unnecessary.\n860\t    Oracle routing on clean inputs (assigning a \"clean\" label that applies no correction)\n861\t    would be 92.4% by definition, but since oracle routing in practice assigns perturbation\n862\t    types (not a \"clean\" class), oracle on clean inputs must force-assign a type,\n863\t    yielding 91.0% (-1.4pp). This motivates confidence-gated routing: if PAR entropy\n864\t    exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.8), suppress expert corrections and fall back to\n865\t    frozen representations.\n866\t\n867\t    '\n868\t  tables:\n869\t  - title: Clean LIBERO performance\n870\t    content: '| Method | Clean LIBERO (%) | Δ vs Frozen |\n871\t\n872\t      |--------|-----------------|-------------|\n873\t\n874\t      | Frozen pi0 | 92.4 | — |\n875\t\n876\t      | Universal r16 | 91.6 | −0.8 |\n877\t\n878\t      | Universal r48 | 91.2 | −1.2 |\n879\t\n880\t      | Oracle routing | 91.0 | −1.4 |\n881\t\n882\t      | Meta-Brain | 90.4 | −2.0 |\n883\t\n884\t      '\n885\t  - title: PAR router behavior on clean inputs\n886\t    content: '| Metric | Value |\n887\t\n888\t      |--------|-------|\n889\t\n890\t      | Avg entropy | 0.88 |\n891\t\n892\t      | Max expert weight | 0.48 |\n893\t\n894\t      | w(LoRA-V) | 0.35 |\n895\t\n896\t      | w(LoRA-S) | 0.31 |\n897\t\n898\t      | w(LoRA-P) | 0.34 |\n899\t\n900\t      '\n901\t  figures_description:\n902\t  - 'Routing entropy histogram: bimodal — perturbed inputs cluster at low entropy\n903\t    (0.3-0.7), clean inputs cluster at high entropy (0.8-1.0). Clear separation supports\n904\t    entropy-based gating.'\n905\t  analysis: 'The -2.0pp clean degradation is notable but manageable. The entropy analysis\n906\t    provides a clean solution: confidence-gated routing. Add to the paper as a practical\n907\t    recommendation: \"For deployment with mixed clean/perturbed inputs, we recommend\n908\t    entropy-gated routing — suppress corrections when PAR entropy exceeds 0.8 (indicating\n909\t    no clear perturbation type). This recovers frozen performance on clean inputs\n910\t    while preserving routing benefits on perturbed inputs.\" Include the entropy histogram\n911\t    as evidence that the gating threshold is well-defined (bimodal distribution with\n912\t    clear separation).\n913\t\n914\t    '\n915\t- name: Cross-backbone validation — OpenVLA\n916\t  plan_id: plan_011\n917\t  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 (perturbation classification) + core Phase 1 pipeline\n918\t    on OpenVLA to test generalizability of perturbation-diagnostic routing across\n919\t    architectures.\n920\t\n921\t    '\n922\t  expected_results:\n923\t    openvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.94\n924\t    openvla_phase0_best_layer: layer_8\n925\t    openvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.86\n926\t    openvla_frozen_overall: 48.2\n927\t    openvla_universal_r16_overall: 55.8\n928\t    openvla_meta_brain_overall: 61.4\n929\t    openvla_routing_benefit: 5.6\n930\t    openvla_gain_over_frozen: 13.2\n931\t  result_rationale: 'Phase 0 partially replicates on OpenVLA: 3-class F1=0.94 is lower\n932\t    than pi0''s 0.993 because OpenVLA''s Llama backbone produces less cleanly separable\n933\t    perturbation representations than PaliGemma (Llama processes visual tokens through\n934\t    more language-oriented layers, partially mixing perturbation signals). Best layer\n935\t    shifts from layer 6 (pi0/PaliGemma) to layer 8 (OpenVLA/Llama), consistent with\n936\t    different architecture depths. K=1 F1 drops more sharply (0.86 vs 0.898) because\n937\t    single-step features are noisier in OpenVLA. Phase 1 routing benefit on OpenVLA\n938\t    (~5.6pp over universal r16) is comparable to pi0''s 5.6pp over r48, suggesting\n939\t    the routing principle generalizes even if absolute performance differs (OpenVLA''s\n940\t    frozen baseline is lower at 48.2%). The similar routing benefit across backbones\n941\t    is the key generalizability result — perturbation- diagnostic routing is not pi0-specific.\n942\t\n943\t    '\n944\t  tables:\n945\t  - title: Cross-backbone comparison\n946\t    content: '| Metric | pi0 (PaliGemma) | OpenVLA (Llama) |\n947\t\n948\t      |--------|-----------------|-----------------|\n949\t\n950\t      | Phase 0 3-class F1 | 0.993 | 0.94 |\n951\t\n952\t      | Phase 0 K=1 F1 | 0.898 | 0.86 |\n953\t\n954\t      | Best classification layer | 6 | 8 |\n955\t\n956\t      | Frozen baseline | 53.6% | 48.2% |\n957\t\n958\t      | Universal r16 | 62.1% | 55.8% |\n959\t\n960\t      | Meta-Brain | 72.0% | 61.4% |\n961\t\n962\t      | Routing Δ (vs universal r16) | +9.9pp | +5.6pp |\n963\t\n964\t      '\n965\t  figures_description:\n966\t  - 'Side-by-side t-SNE plots: pi0 layer 6 activations (tight clusters) vs OpenVLA\n967\t    layer 8 activations (looser clusters, partial overlap). Both show 3-class structure.'\n968\t  analysis: 'Cross-backbone results support generalizability with caveats. The routing\n969\t    principle works on OpenVLA but with lower absolute numbers and less clean separation.\n970\t    Present as: \"perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes across VLA architectures\n971\t    — the routing benefit (5-6pp over universal LoRA) is consistent despite different\n972\t    backbone feature characteristics.\" Honestly note that OpenVLA''s weaker Phase\n973\t    0 classification (F1=0.94) sets a lower ceiling for routing quality. This is a\n974\t    P2 experiment — if time is short, Phase 0 replication alone is sufficient for\n975\t    a generalizability claim.\n976\t\n977\t    '\n978\t- name: LoRA layer placement ablation\n979\t  plan_id: plan_012\n980\t  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid\n981\t    (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers (default). Test per-expert-type layer\n982\t    placement hypothesis.\n983\t\n984\t    '\n985\t  expected_results:\n986\t    lora_v_early_visual: 76.8\n987\t    lora_v_mid_visual: 73.2\n988\t    lora_v_late_visual: 70.4\n989\t    lora_v_all_visual: 75.5\n990\t    lora_s_early_spatial: 64.6\n991\t    lora_s_mid_spatial: 70.8\n992\t    lora_s_late_spatial: 66.2\n993\t    lora_s_all_spatial: 69.2\n994\t    lora_p_early_physical: 57.4\n995\t    lora_p_mid_physical: 59.2\n996\t    lora_p_late_physical: 60.8\n997\t    lora_p_all_physical: 61.0\n998\t    optimal_v: early (2-6)\n999\t    optimal_s: mid (6-12)\n1000\t    optimal_p: late (12-16) ≈ all layers\n1001\t  result_rationale: 'Layer placement shows a partial match to the V→early, S→mid,\n1002\t    P→late hypothesis, but with important nuances. LoRA-V is best at early layers\n1003\t    (76.8%) because visual perturbations corrupt early representations most — intervening\n1004\t    at layers 2-6 corrects corrupted visual features before they propagate to higher\n1005\t    layers. LoRA-V at all layers (75.5%) is slightly worse because later LoRA modules\n1006\t    introduce unnecessary corrections at layers where visual features are already\n1007\t    clean. LoRA-S is best at mid layers (70.8%) because spatial perturbations (layout\n1008\t    changes) affect mid-level spatial reasoning where object relationships are encoded\n1009\t    — early layers still process raw visual features correctly, and late layers have\n1010\t    already committed to action plans. LoRA-P shows the weakest layer preference:\n1011\t    late layers (60.8%) are marginally best but barely exceed all layers (61.0%),\n1012\t    confirming that physical perturbation correction is weak throughout the representation\n1013\t    hierarchy. The lack of strong layer preference for LoRA-P reinforces the median-tier\n1014\t    narrative that physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation\n1015\t    level. The layer placement analysis provides a concrete practical insight: type-specific\n1016\t    layer assignment (V→early, S→mid) could improve parameter efficiency by 40% (5\n1017\t    layers × 3 experts = 15 LoRA modules vs 18 layers × 3 experts = 54 modules in\n1018\t    the default config).\n1019\t\n1020\t    '\n1021\t  tables:\n1022\t  - title: Layer placement ablation (per-type success rate with matched expert)\n1023\t    content: '| Expert | Early (2-6) | Mid (6-12) | Late (12-16) | All (default) |\n1024\t      Best |\n1025\t\n1026\t      |--------|-------------|------------|-------------|---------------|------|\n1027\t\n1028\t      | LoRA-V on Visual | **76.8** | 73.2 | 70.4 | 75.5 | Early |\n1029\t\n1030\t      | LoRA-S on Spatial | 64.6 | **70.8** | 66.2 | 69.2 | Mid |\n1031\t\n1032\t      | LoRA-P on Physical | 57.4 | 59.2 | 60.8 | **61.0** | All ≈ Late |\n1033\t\n1034\t      '\n1035\t  figures_description:\n1036\t  - '3-panel line plot (one per expert type): x=layer range, y=success rate. LoRA-V\n1037\t    peaks early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P is nearly flat. Clear mechanistic alignment\n1038\t    for V and S; P shows no useful structure.'\n1039\t  analysis: 'Layer placement provides a practical insight for the paper''s \"layer-perturbation\n1040\t    mapping\" narrative. V→early and S→mid are clear wins that align with VLA processing\n1041\t    stages. P→late/all is ambiguous, reinforcing that physical perturbation correction\n1042\t    at the representation level is fundamentally limited. Present as: \"perturbation-type-to-layer\n1043\t    mapping reveals that visual perturbations are best corrected at early layers (pre-spatial-reasoning)\n1044\t    and spatial perturbations at mid layers (spatial-reasoning stage), suggesting\n1045\t    type-aware layer assignment as a parameter efficiency optimization.\" Note: all-layer\n1046\t    (default) is competitive for all types, so the default config is a reasonable\n1047\t    choice if practitioners don''t want to tune layer assignment.'\n1048\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"be50b614-6a9d-479f-a5de-ac9a1b369f66","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:16:54.172Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Routing helps but the margin over rank-48 universal is modest\n  (~6pp). Advantage is clear on Visual and Spatial but Physical shows limited routing\n  benefit. Headline: \"~72% with ~12M params, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal.\"\n  Reframe as \"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types requiring qualitatively\n  different representation-level corrections.\" Physical perturbation is the weak link\n  — LoRA-P underperforms because physical perturbations require action-space correction\n  beyond representation-level adaptation. Rank sweep shows routing benefit depends\n  on expert capacity (inflection at r≥32). Clean LIBERO shows minor degradation (-2pp).\n  Oracle gap (5pp) suggests PAR router leaves room for improvement — discuss as future\n  work. Paper is viable at CoRL with sharpened per-type framing and honest capacity\n  analysis.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Train LoRA-V/S/P (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers) on\n    type-specific data plus universal LoRA (rank 16) on mixed data. Evaluate each\n    expert in-type and cross-type. Visual experts trained with online augmentation\n    on LIBERO demos; Spatial/Physical experts trained on oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus\n    demos.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    lora_v_on_visual: 75.5\n    lora_v_on_spatial: 63.1\n    lora_v_on_physical: 59.8\n    lora_s_on_visual: 62.4\n    lora_s_on_spatial: 69.2\n    lora_s_on_physical: 58.3\n    lora_p_on_visual: 57.6\n    lora_p_on_spatial: 57.2\n    lora_p_on_physical: 61.0\n    universal_r16_on_visual: 65.8\n    universal_r16_on_spatial: 60.2\n    universal_r16_on_physical: 55.4\n    universal_r16_overall: 62.1\n    avg_cross_type_mismatch_drop_pp: 10.8\n    lora_v_cross_drop: 13.1\n    lora_s_cross_drop: 9.0\n    lora_p_cross_drop: 3.6\n    training_converged_epochs_v: 15\n    training_converged_epochs_s: 22\n    training_converged_epochs_p: 30\n    action_mse_val_lora_v: 0.0031\n    action_mse_val_lora_s: 0.0039\n    action_mse_val_lora_p: 0.0054\n    action_mse_val_universal: 0.0042\n  result_rationale: 'LoRA experts learn partially distinct corrections — cross-type\n    drop exists but is asymmetric across types. LoRA-V shows strongest specialization\n    (cross-drop ~13pp) because visual augmentation training creates a tight distribution\n    match: the expert learns to invert specific visual corruptions (brightness shifts,\n    background swaps) that are absent in spatial/physical data, so applying LoRA-V\n    to non-visual inputs actively degrades representations by \"correcting\" features\n    that weren''t corrupted. LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (~9pp cross-drop):\n    layout perturbations alter mid-level spatial relationships that require qualitatively\n    different corrections from visual corruptions, but there is partial overlap because\n    spatial changes also affect visual appearance. LoRA-P is the weak link: only ~3.6pp\n    cross-drop because physical perturbations (robot initial state) primarily affect\n    action trajectories, not visual representations. LoRA-P''s correction at the representation\n    level is modest (61% on-type, barely above universal''s 55.4%) — the signal for\n    physical correction is weak at PaliGemma layer 6 where representations encode\n    visual/spatial features better than proprioceptive state. Training convergence\n    reflects this: LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong, clean signal), LoRA-P takes\n    30 epochs with higher validation MSE (noisy, weak signal). The average cross-drop\n    of ~10.8pp is in the median range — specialization exists but is less dramatic\n    than best-case, driven mostly by Visual/Spatial divergence while Physical contributes\n    little.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Per-expert success rates (rows=expert, cols=evaluation perturbation type)\n    content: '| Expert | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Avg Cross-type | Cross Δ |\n\n      |--------|--------|---------|----------|----------------|---------|\n\n      | LoRA-V | **75.5** | 63.1 | 59.8 | 61.5 | −13.1 |\n\n      | LoRA-S | 62.4 | **69.2** | 58.3 | 60.4 | −9.0 |\n\n      | LoRA-P | 57.6 | 57.2 | **61.0** | 57.4 | −3.6 |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | — | — |\n\n      | Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | — | — |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - '3×3 heatmap of expert success rates: diagonal (matched) cells are brightest.\n    LoRA-P row is nearly flat — minimal on-type advantage. LoRA-V row shows sharp\n    contrast between on-type (bright) and off-type (dim).'\n  - 'Training loss curves (4 subplots): LoRA-V converges fastest/smoothest; LoRA-P\n    shows noisy plateau; LoRA-S intermediate; Universal converges to moderate loss.'\n  analysis: 'Experts DO specialize, but unevenly. The asymmetry is the interesting\n    finding: Visual and Spatial LoRA show strong type-specific corrections (cross-drops\n    13pp and 9pp), while Physical LoRA shows minimal specialization (cross-drop 3.6pp).\n    This maps to the mechanistic story: PaliGemma''s representation space strongly\n    encodes visual and spatial information (it''s a vision-language model), but physical\n    state (robot joint positions, force dynamics) is weakly represented. LoRA-P''s\n    modest 61% on-type and low cross-drop suggest that rank-16 representation-level\n    correction is insufficient for physical perturbations — this needs honest discussion\n    in the paper. Universal r16 at 62.1% sets the parameter-unmatched baseline for\n    ablation.\n\n    '\n- name: Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Train single universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params) on mixed all-perturbation\n    data. Parameter-matched to Meta-Brain''s total budget. THE existential test for\n    Claim 2.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rank48_universal_overall: 66.4\n    rank48_on_visual: 70.1\n    rank48_on_spatial: 64.3\n    rank48_on_physical: 58.6\n    rank48_vs_rank16_gain_pp: 4.3\n    rank48_vs_meta_brain_gap_pp: -5.6\n    claim2_survival: true\n    training_epochs: 18\n    action_mse_val: 0.0036\n  result_rationale: 'Rank-48 universal captures most of the low-hanging correction\n    accessible via parameter scaling alone. The +4.3pp gain over rank-16 (62.1→66.4)\n    shows that extra capacity helps uniformly — but with sharply diminishing returns\n    (3.2× the parameters for only 1.4× the gain-over-frozen). This diminishing return\n    is because a single adapter learning mixed-perturbation data must find a compromise\n    correction that partially works for all types but optimally corrects none. The\n    rank-48 adapter effectively learns the average correction direction across perturbation\n    types; it boosts visual most (+4.3pp) because visual perturbations are most common\n    in training data and share the most overlap, while physical gains least (+3.2pp)\n    because physical correction requires a qualitatively different direction. The\n    66.4% vs Meta-Brain''s 72.0% leaves a 5.6pp gap — enough to validate Claim 2 but\n    not dramatic. This gap is the routing premium: what you gain by allocating the\n    same 12M parameters to three specialized experts with informed routing vs one\n    universal adapter. The gap is largest on visual (70.1 vs 75.5, ~5.4pp) where specialization\n    matters most and smallest on physical (58.6 vs 61.0, ~2.4pp) where even specialized\n    LoRA-P barely outperforms.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Rank-48 universal vs Meta-Brain per-type comparison\n    content: '| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n\n      |--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n\n      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | 62.1 |\n\n      | Universal r48 | 12M | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 | 66.4 |\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n\n      | Δ (MB − r48) | 0 | +5.4 | +4.9 | +2.4 | +5.6 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: rank-48 vs Meta-Brain across 3 perturbation categories. Gap\n    is clearly visible on Visual/Spatial, narrow on Physical.'\n  analysis: 'Claim 2 survives — routing adds 5.6pp over parameter-matched universal.\n    But the per-type decomposition tells the real story: routing earns its keep on\n    Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp) where experts learn genuinely distinct corrections,\n    but adds only +2.4pp on Physical where the expert itself is limited. Paper should\n    frame: \"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types that require qualitatively\n    different representation-level corrections; for perturbation types that primarily\n    affect action-space dynamics (Physical), the routing advantage is modest.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Assemble full Meta-Brain system: PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, first-token,\n    2-layer MLP) produces soft routing weights p(type_k). Corrected hidden state h_corrected\n    = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning\n    with frozen LoRA expert parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    meta_brain_overall: 72.0\n    meta_brain_visual: 75.5\n    meta_brain_spatial: 69.2\n    meta_brain_physical: 61.0\n    meta_brain_camera: 78.0\n    meta_brain_light: 79.2\n    meta_brain_background: 71.0\n    meta_brain_noise: 73.6\n    meta_brain_layout: 69.2\n    meta_brain_robot: 61.0\n    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_v: 0.82\n    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_s: 0.11\n    routing_weight_visual_to_lora_p: 0.07\n    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_s: 0.76\n    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_v: 0.14\n    routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_p: 0.1\n    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_p: 0.67\n    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_v: 0.14\n    routing_weight_physical_to_lora_s: 0.19\n    routing_entropy_visual: 0.41\n    routing_entropy_spatial: 0.52\n    routing_entropy_physical: 0.72\n    gain_over_frozen: 18.4\n    gain_over_universal_r16: 9.9\n    gain_over_universal_r48: 5.6\n  result_rationale: 'Soft routing via PAR is accurate but introduces blending that\n    partially dilutes expert specialization — this is the core median-tier mechanism.\n    For visual perturbations, the router confidently assigns ~82% weight to LoRA-V\n    (entropy 0.41) because Phase 0 showed visual perturbations create the most distinctive\n    activation signatures at layer 6. The resulting 75.5% visual accuracy is strong\n    but below the oracle ceiling because 18% of the correction comes from mismatched\n    experts. For spatial, routing is slightly less decisive (76% to LoRA-S, entropy\n    0.52) because some spatial perturbations (e.g., layout changes that also alter\n    visual appearance) overlap with visual signatures, producing moderate blending\n    and 69.2%. For physical, the router is least confident (67% to LoRA-P, entropy\n    0.72) — physical perturbation signatures are weakest at PaliGemma layer 6 because\n    this layer primarily encodes visual-spatial features, not proprioceptive state.\n    The high entropy means ~33% of correction comes from LoRA-V/S, which applies visual/spatial\n    corrections to physical inputs — not harmful but not helpful either. Physical\n    at 61.0% is only +2.4pp above rank-48 universal (58.6%), making it the weak link.\n    The overall 72.0% is a solid +18.4pp over frozen pi0 but the per-type gradient\n    (75.5→69.2→61.0) is the interesting story. Routing confidence (entropy) directly\n    predicts per-type success — this correlation supports confidence-gated routing\n    as a practical recommendation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Meta-Brain per-perturbation success rates\n    content: '| Perturbation | Category | Success (%) | Frozen pi0 (%) | Δ |\n\n      |-------------|----------|-------------|----------------|------|\n\n      | Camera jitter | Visual | 78.0 | 55.2 | +22.8 |\n\n      | Light change | Visual | 79.2 | 60.8 | +18.4 |\n\n      | Background swap | Visual | 71.0 | 54.0 | +17.0 |\n\n      | Sensor noise | Visual | 73.6 | 56.4 | +17.2 |\n\n      | Layout random | Spatial | 69.2 | 51.6 | +17.6 |\n\n      | Robot state | Physical | 61.0 | 47.2 | +13.8 |\n\n      | **Overall** | **—** | **72.0** | **53.6** | **+18.4** |\n\n      '\n  - title: PAR routing weight distribution (per input category)\n    content: '| Input Category | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\n\n      |---------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n\n      | Visual | **0.82** | 0.11 | 0.07 | 0.41 |\n\n      | Spatial | 0.14 | **0.76** | 0.10 | 0.52 |\n\n      | Physical | 0.14 | 0.19 | **0.67** | 0.72 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - '3×3 routing weight heatmap: near-diagonal but imperfect. Visual row is sharpest\n    (brightest diagonal). Physical row is most diffuse (weakest diagonal, visible\n    off-diagonal mass).'\n  - Per-perturbation bar chart with frozen baseline overlay. Visual subtypes cluster\n    at 71-79%, spatial at 69%, physical at 61%. Clear gradient from left to right.\n  - 'Scatter plot: routing entropy (x) vs per-category success rate (y). Three points\n    form a tight negative correlation (r ≈ −0.98), supporting confidence-gated routing.'\n  analysis: 'The routing weight heatmap is the key diagnostic figure. The entropy\n    gradient (0.41→0.52→0.72) perfectly anti-correlates with success rate (75.5→69.2→61.0),\n    suggesting routing confidence is a reliable proxy for expected correction quality.\n    This motivates two practical recommendations: (1) confidence-gated routing — fall\n    back to frozen when entropy exceeds a threshold (addresses clean LIBERO degradation),\n    and (2) hard routing at deployment — if soft routing blending dilutes specialization,\n    temperature-sharpened or argmax routing may close part of the oracle gap. The\n    physical category''s high entropy and low success rate is the honest limitation:\n    Meta-Brain''s representation-level correction has diminishing returns for perturbation\n    types that primarily require action-space adaptation.\n\n    '\n- name: Oracle routing ablation — upper bound\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard\n    one-hot routing). Measures the gap between learned soft routing and perfect hard\n    routing — upper bound for routing quality.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    oracle_overall: 77.0\n    oracle_visual: 81.8\n    oracle_spatial: 74.6\n    oracle_physical: 66.4\n    oracle_minus_meta_brain_overall: 5.0\n    oracle_minus_meta_brain_visual: 6.3\n    oracle_minus_meta_brain_spatial: 5.4\n    oracle_minus_meta_brain_physical: 5.4\n  result_rationale: 'Oracle routing eliminates soft routing blending — each input\n    gets 100% weight on the matched expert. The 5.0pp overall gap over Meta-Brain\n    comes from two compounding effects: (1) eliminating dilution from mismatched expert\n    blending (accounts for ~3pp — the 18-33% off-diagonal mass in PAR routing now\n    goes to zero), and (2) perfect type assignment on ambiguous boundary cases where\n    PAR misroutes (accounts for ~2pp — PAR''s 0.996 F1 still makes errors on a few\n    percent of timesteps, and these compound over 44-step episodes). The gap is roughly\n    uniform across types (5.0-6.3pp) rather than concentrated on physical, suggesting\n    the PAR router''s limitation is general blending behavior, not systematic failure\n    on any specific type. Oracle physical at 66.4% confirms that even perfect routing\n    cannot push physical above the expert''s ceiling — LoRA-P itself is the bottleneck,\n    not the routing. The 77.0% oracle sets a meaningful upper bound: the 3-expert\n    architecture has 5pp more headroom, achievable through sharper routing (temperature\n    annealing, hard routing, or better router architectures).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Oracle vs PAR routing comparison\n    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n\n      |--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n\n      | Meta-Brain (PAR) | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n\n      | Oracle routing | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 | 77.0 |\n\n      | Δ | +6.3 | +5.4 | +5.4 | +5.0 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired bar chart: Meta-Brain vs Oracle per category, gap annotated. Uniform ~5pp\n    gap across all types.'\n  analysis: 'The oracle gap of 5.0pp is moderate — PAR captures most of the routing\n    value (72/77 = 93.5% of oracle performance) but leaves room for improvement. The\n    uniformity of the gap across types suggests that improving routing sharpness (e.g.,\n    lower-temperature softmax, curriculum-based routing training) would benefit all\n    categories equally. Present as concrete future work: \"hard routing or temperature-annealed\n    routing could recover an estimated 2-3pp of the 5pp oracle gap.\" The oracle at\n    77% also contextualizes the absolute performance ceiling of the 3-expert architecture\n    — further gains require stronger individual experts (higher rank) or additional\n    experts.\n\n    '\n- name: Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal\n    r16, Random routing (3×r16 experts), Universal r48 (parameter-matched), Meta-Brain,\n    Oracle routing. Report overall + per-perturbation breakdown with standard deviations\n    and statistical significance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    frozen_overall: 53.6\n    frozen_std: 2.1\n    universal_r16_overall: 62.1\n    universal_r16_std: 1.8\n    universal_r48_overall: 66.4\n    universal_r48_std: 1.6\n    random_routing_overall: 64.2\n    random_routing_std: 2.5\n    meta_brain_overall: 72.0\n    meta_brain_std: 1.9\n    oracle_overall: 77.0\n    oracle_std: 1.4\n    meta_brain_vs_random_gap: 7.8\n    meta_brain_vs_r48_gap: 5.6\n    meta_brain_vs_frozen_gain: 18.4\n    random_vs_r16_gap: 2.1\n    random_vs_r48_gap: -2.2\n    p_value_mb_vs_random: 0.003\n    p_value_mb_vs_r48: 0.008\n    p_value_mb_vs_r16: <0.001\n  result_rationale: 'The ablation ladder forms a clean monotonic ordering: Frozen\n    (53.6) < r16 (62.1) < Random (64.2) < r48 (66.4) < Meta-Brain (72.0) < Oracle\n    (77.0). Each step isolates a specific factor:\n\n    Frozen→r16 (+8.5pp): LoRA adaptation on perturbed data helps, even with limited\n    capacity and no specialization. This is the \"any correction is better than none\"\n    baseline gain.\n\n    r16→Random (+2.1pp): Random routing gives 1/3 chance of the right expert, plus\n    the ensemble effect of having 3× the parameters (12M vs 3.7M). The modest +2.1pp\n    shows the ensemble effect alone is minor — randomly applying the wrong expert\n    often hurts more than the extra capacity helps.\n\n    Random→r48 (−2.2pp): Random routing actually underperforms the parameter-matched\n    universal r48. This is important: 12M parameters concentrated in one coherent\n    adapter (r48) outperform 12M parameters split across 3 randomly-assigned experts.\n    Random routing''s 2/3 mismatch penalty exceeds its 1/3 specialization benefit.\n    This directly supports the claim that INFORMED routing is necessary — mere expert\n    diversity without correct assignment is worse than a single universal adapter.\n\n    r48→Meta-Brain (+5.6pp): The routing premium. Informed routing turns the 3-expert\n    architecture from a liability (random: −2.2pp vs r48) into a significant advantage\n    (+5.6pp vs r48). This is the core evidence for Claim 2.\n\n    Meta-Brain→Oracle (+5.0pp): Room for routing improvement. The PAR router captures\n    most of the value but soft blending leaves 5pp on the table.\n\n    Statistical significance: Meta-Brain vs random (p=0.003) and vs r48 (p=0.008)\n    are significant at α=0.01 across 5 seeds. Random routing has the highest variance\n    (std=2.5) due to stochastic expert assignment; oracle has the lowest (std=1.4)\n    due to deterministic correct routing.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Ablation ladder — LIBERO-Plus overall success rate (5 seeds)\n    content: '| Method | Params | Overall (%) | Std | Δ vs Frozen | Δ vs r48 |\n\n      |--------|--------|------------|-----|-------------|----------|\n\n      | Frozen pi0 | 0 | 53.6 | 2.1 | — | −12.8 |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | 62.1 | 1.8 | +8.5 | −4.3 |\n\n      | Random routing | 12M | 64.2 | 2.5 | +10.6 | −2.2 |\n\n      | Universal r48 | 12M | 66.4 | 1.6 | +12.8 | — |\n\n      | **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **72.0** | **1.9** | **+18.4** | **+5.6** |\n\n      | Oracle routing | 12M | 77.0 | 1.4 | +23.4 | +10.6 |\n\n      '\n  - title: Per-perturbation-category breakdown\n    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\n\n      |--------|--------|---------|----------|\n\n      | Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 |\n\n      | Random routing | 67.8 | 62.4 | 57.0 |\n\n      | Universal r48 | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 |\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 |\n\n      | Oracle | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 |\n\n      '\n  - title: Statistical significance (pairwise p-values, two-sided t-test, 5 seeds)\n    content: '| Comparison | Δ (pp) | p-value | Significant (α=0.01) |\n\n      |-----------|--------|---------|---------------------|\n\n      | MB vs Frozen | +18.4 | <0.001 | ✓ |\n\n      | MB vs r16 | +9.9 | <0.001 | ✓ |\n\n      | MB vs Random | +7.8 | 0.003 | ✓ |\n\n      | MB vs r48 | +5.6 | 0.008 | ✓ |\n\n      | Oracle vs MB | +5.0 | 0.011 | ✗ (marginal) |\n\n      | Random vs r16 | +2.1 | 0.14 | ✗ |\n\n      | Random vs r48 | −2.2 | 0.18 | ✗ |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Ablation ladder bar chart (ascending): 6 bars from Frozen to Oracle with error\n    bars (±1 std). Monotonic increase. The r48↔Random ordering is the subtle but important\n    finding — Random slightly below r48.'\n  - 'Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 6 methods × 3 categories. The Physical column\n    shows compressed spacing between methods — limited routing benefit. Visual column\n    shows the widest spread.'\n  - 'Waterfall/decomposition chart: each step from Frozen→Oracle annotated with the\n    factor it isolates (adaptation, ensemble, parameter scaling, informed routing,\n    perfect routing).'\n  analysis: 'The ablation ladder is the paper''s core figure. Critical narrative points:\n    (1) Random < r48 is the surprise result — it proves that routing QUALITY matters,\n    not just expert diversity. Without this, a reviewer could argue Meta-Brain''s\n    gains are just ensemble effects. (2) Meta-Brain vs r48 (+5.6pp, p=0.008) is statistically\n    significant but requires careful framing: \"modest but reliable routing premium.\"\n    (3) The per-type breakdown shows Physical compresses the ladder — all methods\n    are closer together on Physical than on Visual/Spatial. This is the honest story:\n    routing helps most where representation-level corrections differ qualitatively\n    by type. (4) Oracle−MB significance is marginal (p=0.011) — the paper can discuss\n    the oracle gap as suggestive rather than definitive.\n\n    '\n- name: FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Reproduce FTM on pi0 backbone for visual perturbation types. Compare\n    Meta-Brain''s visual performance vs FTM. Establish that Meta-Brain matches visual-only\n    methods on visual while additionally covering spatial/physical.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ftm_visual_overall: 73.8\n    ftm_camera: 76.4\n    ftm_light: 78.2\n    ftm_background: 67.8\n    ftm_noise: 72.6\n    ftm_spatial: 52.4\n    ftm_physical: 47.8\n    ftm_overall: 60.8\n    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n    meta_brain_visual_overall: 75.5\n    meta_brain_vs_ftm_visual_delta: 1.7\n    meta_brain_vs_ftm_spatial_delta: 16.8\n    meta_brain_vs_ftm_physical_delta: 13.2\n  result_rationale: 'FTM is purpose-built for visual robustness with extreme parameter\n    efficiency (~4K params), so it achieves strong 73.8% on visual perturbations —\n    only 1.7pp below Meta-Brain''s 75.5%. This is expected: FTM''s fine-tuned visual\n    transforms directly target the same representation corruption that LoRA-V corrects,\n    and 4K purpose-designed parameters can be more efficient than 3.7M generic LoRA\n    parameters for a narrow task. However, FTM on spatial (52.4%) and physical (47.8%)\n    is at or below frozen pi0 level — its visual-only training provides zero spatial/physical\n    correction and occasionally interferes (physical 47.8% vs frozen 47.2% suggests\n    slight degradation from applying visual transforms to non-visual inputs). FTM''s\n    background swap result (67.8%) is notably lower than its camera/light/noise results\n    because background swaps introduce novel textures that FTM''s limited capacity\n    cannot fully absorb. Meta-Brain''s value proposition is clear: comparable visual\n    performance (+1.7pp) plus +16.8pp on spatial and +13.2pp on physical that FTM\n    cannot touch.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: FTM vs Meta-Brain comparison\n    content: '| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\n\n      |--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\n\n      | Frozen pi0 | 0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | 53.6 |\n\n      | FTM | 4K | 73.8 | 52.4 | 47.8 | 60.8 |\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\n\n      | Δ (MB − FTM) | — | +1.7 | +16.8 | +13.2 | +11.2 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Radar/spider chart: FTM vs Meta-Brain across 6 perturbation types. FTM is competitive\n    on the 4 visual axes but collapses to frozen-level on spatial/physical.'\n  analysis: 'FTM comparison reinforces the multi-type robustness narrative. Do NOT\n    claim Meta-Brain beats FTM on visual — the 1.7pp gap is not significant and FTM\n    achieves this with 3000× fewer parameters. Instead frame: \"Meta-Brain matches\n    purpose-built visual methods while providing broad perturbation coverage that\n    single-modality approaches cannot. The parameter cost (12M vs 4K) is the price\n    of multi-type generality.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Parameter efficiency analysis\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters.\n    Generate scatter plot comparing Meta-Brain, Universal variants, FTM, VLA-GSE.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    meta_brain_gain_pp: 18.4\n    meta_brain_params_M: 12\n    meta_brain_gain_per_Mparam: 1.53\n    universal_r16_gain_pp: 8.5\n    universal_r16_params_M: 3.7\n    universal_r16_gain_per_Mparam: 2.3\n    universal_r48_gain_pp: 12.8\n    universal_r48_params_M: 12\n    universal_r48_gain_per_Mparam: 1.07\n    ftm_gain_pp_visual_only: 16.0\n    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n    ftm_gain_per_Mparam: 4000.0\n    ftm_gain_pp_overall: 7.2\n    ftm_gain_per_Mparam_overall: 1800.0\n    vla_gse_gain_pp: 27.6\n    vla_gse_params_M: 114\n    vla_gse_gain_per_Mparam: 0.24\n    vla_gse_note: different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), indirect comparison only\n  result_rationale: 'Parameter efficiency depends on what you measure. FTM dominates\n    gain-per-param on visual-only (4000 gain/Mparam) because it was designed for exactly\n    this — but its overall gain-per-param (1800) still leads because overall averages\n    include the visual types where FTM excels. Universal r16 has better gain-per-param\n    (2.30) than Meta-Brain (1.53) because smaller models have an efficiency advantage\n    on the low-hanging-fruit gains. The critical comparison is r48 (1.07) vs Meta-Brain\n    (1.53): at the same parameter budget, routing gives 43% better efficiency than\n    uniform adaptation. VLA-GSE at 0.24 gain/Mparam (on a different backbone, hence\n    indirect) shows that Meta-Brain is ~6.4× more parameter-efficient in gain-per-param\n    terms. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain above the \"universal LoRA scaling\n    line\" (connecting r16 and r48 points), visually demonstrating that routing adds\n    value beyond what parameter scaling alone provides.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Parameter efficiency comparison\n    content: '| Method | Params | Gain over frozen | Gain/Mparam | Coverage |\n\n      |--------|--------|-----------------|-------------|----------|\n\n      | FTM | 4K | +16.0pp (visual) | 4000.0 | Visual only |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 3.7M | +8.5pp | 2.30 | All (uniform) |\n\n      | **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **+18.4pp** | **1.53** | **All (specialized)**\n      |\n\n      | Universal r48 | 12M | +12.8pp | 1.07 | All (uniform) |\n\n      | VLA-GSE* | 114M | +27.6pp* | 0.24* | All (capacity) |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Log-scale scatter: x=trainable params (log), y=gain-over-frozen (%). Meta-Brain\n    sits above the r16→r48 scaling line. FTM is a high-efficiency outlier (visual\n    only, flagged). VLA-GSE is marked with asterisk (different backbone). Dashed lines\n    connect parameter-matched pairs (r48, Meta-Brain).'\n  analysis: 'The scatter plot is the Claim 3 figure. Meta-Brain''s position above\n    the universal LoRA scaling line visually demonstrates the routing premium. However,\n    FTM''s dominance on visual-only efficiency needs honest treatment — acknowledge\n    that for deployments facing only visual perturbations, FTM is more efficient.\n    Meta-Brain''s value is multi-type coverage with routing specialization. VLA-GSE\n    comparison must carry the \"different backbone\" caveat prominently — apples-to-oranges\n    with asterisk, not a direct parameter efficiency claim.\n\n    '\n- name: Correction Memory ablation\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain ± Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing\n    weights based on FAISS-retrieved exemplars. Quantify marginal contribution.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    meta_brain_no_cm_overall: 72.0\n    meta_brain_with_cm_overall: 73.2\n    cm_delta_overall: 1.2\n    cm_delta_visual: 1.4\n    cm_delta_spatial: 1.2\n    cm_delta_physical: 0.8\n    cm_p_value: 0.12\n    cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 2.3\n    cm_alpha_learned: 0.15\n  result_rationale: 'CM provides a marginal +1.2pp improvement that is not statistically\n    significant (p=0.12 across 5 seeds). The learned alpha=0.15 means CM modulates\n    routing weights by only 15% at most — the base PAR router already does most of\n    the work. The per-type pattern follows the same gradient as all other results:\n    visual benefits slightly more (+1.4pp) because visual perturbation exemplars are\n    most distinctive in representation space and FAISS retrieval is most accurate.\n    Physical benefits least (+0.8pp) because physical perturbation representations\n    are less separable, so retrieved exemplars are noisier. The 2.3ms retrieval latency\n    is negligible but the architectural complexity (FAISS index, exemplar storage,\n    alpha parameter) is not justified by 1.2pp. Dream reviewers unanimously flagged\n    CM as padding — this ablation confirms that assessment.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Correction Memory ablation\n    content: '| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | p-value |\n\n      |--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|---------|\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 | — |\n\n      | Meta-Brain + CM | 76.9 | 70.4 | 61.8 | 73.2 | 0.12 |\n\n      | Δ CM | +1.4 | +1.2 | +0.8 | +1.2 | n.s. |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Minimal paired bar chart showing CM adds small, non-significant improvement. Error\n    bars overlap.\n  analysis: 'CM is confirmed as ablation footnote material. Present as: \"An optional\n    Correction Memory module provides +1.2pp (n.s.) by modulating routing weights\n    with retrieved exemplars. We include this result for completeness but recommend\n    the CM-free architecture for deployment simplicity.\" Do NOT elevate CM in the\n    architecture diagram or contribution list.\n\n    '\n- name: Router architecture comparison\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP (default), linear classifier,\n    attention- based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6, first-token pooling.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    linear_routing_f1: 0.988\n    linear_downstream_success: 70.6\n    linear_params_M: 0.1\n    mlp_routing_f1: 0.996\n    mlp_downstream_success: 72.0\n    mlp_params_M: 0.5\n    attention_routing_f1: 0.997\n    attention_downstream_success: 72.4\n    attention_params_M: 0.8\n  result_rationale: 'The routing classification task is nearly saturated (Phase 0\n    F1=0.993), so router architecture differences are small. Linear classifier''s\n    slight accuracy gap (F1 0.988 vs 0.996) translates to ~1.4pp downstream loss:\n    the 0.8% additional routing errors occur on ambiguous boundary cases (e.g., background\n    swaps that also affect spatial layout), and these errors compound over multi-step\n    rollouts (a single misroute early in an episode can cascade). Attention-based\n    router marginally improves (F1 0.997, +0.4pp downstream) by better handling these\n    boundary cases through cross-dimensional attention on the 2048-dim PaliGemma features,\n    but the gain doesn''t justify 60% more parameters. The 2-layer MLP is Pareto-optimal:\n    near-perfect routing at minimal overhead.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Router architecture comparison\n    content: '| Router | Params | Routing F1 | Downstream (%) | Δ vs MLP |\n\n      |--------|--------|-----------|----------------|----------|\n\n      | Linear | 0.1M | 0.988 | 70.6 | −1.4 |\n\n      | 2-layer MLP | 0.5M | 0.996 | 72.0 | — |\n\n      | Attention | 0.8M | 0.997 | 72.4 | +0.4 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Small 3-point scatter: x=router params, y=downstream success. Diminishing returns\n    beyond MLP.'\n  analysis: 'Router architecture is not a bottleneck. Present as a single sentence\n    in the ablation section: \"We compared linear, MLP, and attention-based routers;\n    the 2-layer MLP provides the best complexity-performance tradeoff (Table N).\"\n\n    '\n- name: 6-class vs 3-class routing\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot,\n    6 LoRA experts) vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    class3_overall: 72.0\n    class3_params_M: 12\n    class6_overall: 72.6\n    class6_params_M: 24\n    marginal_gain_per_expert: 0.2\n    class6_camera: 79.3\n    class6_light: 80.0\n    class6_background: 72.8\n    class6_noise: 75.0\n    class6_layout: 69.6\n    class6_robot: 61.4\n  result_rationale: '6-class routing with 6 experts provides only +0.6pp over 3-class\n    at 2× the parameters. This is expected from Phase 0: intra-Visual subtypes cluster\n    tightly in activation space (6-class accuracy 99.82% shows they''re separable,\n    but 3-class accuracy 99.46% shows the grouping is already natural). The marginal\n    gain per additional expert (0.2pp) is negligible. Within visual subtypes, background\n    swap benefits most from its own expert (+1.8pp: 72.8 vs 71.0) because background\n    textures are the most visually distinct subtype, but camera/light/noise show <1pp\n    gains over being grouped. Spatial and physical are already singleton categories\n    so gain is only from having dedicated visual subtype experts freeing up minor\n    capacity. The 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA\n    processing (early/mid/late layers) and is the right granularity.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 6-class vs 3-class routing\n    content: '| Routing | Experts | Params | Overall | Δ vs 3-class |\n\n      |---------|---------|--------|---------|-------------|\n\n      | 3-class | 3 | 12M | 72.0 | — |\n\n      | 6-class | 6 | 24M | 72.6 | +0.6 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3-class vs 6-class per perturbation subtype. Nearly identical\n    bars except background swap shows a small 6-class advantage.'\n  analysis: 'Directly addresses reviewer concern C5. 3-class is sufficient — 6-class\n    doubles parameters for 0.6pp. Background swap is the only subtype that meaningfully\n    benefits from its own expert, but not enough to justify the complexity. If a reviewer\n    pushes, point to Phase 0 intra-Visual clustering as the mechanistic justification\n    for grouping.\n\n    '\n- name: LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity-routing interaction\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: 'Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity. At each rank\n    R, compare 3 type-specific rank-R experts with PAR routing vs parameter-matched\n    universal LoRA (rank 3R). Measures capacity-routing interaction.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r16_routed_overall: 72.0\n    r16_universal_matched_overall: 66.4\n    r16_routing_benefit: 5.6\n    r32_routed_overall: 75.4\n    r32_universal_matched_overall: 68.6\n    r32_routing_benefit: 6.8\n    r64_routed_overall: 78.2\n    r64_universal_matched_overall: 70.4\n    r64_routing_benefit: 7.8\n    capacity_threshold: r≥32\n    r32_total_params_M: 24\n    r64_total_params_M: 48\n  result_rationale: 'Routing benefit is capacity-dependent, growing from 5.6pp (r16)\n    to 6.8pp (r32) to 7.8pp (r64). The mechanism: at low rank (r16), each expert has\n    limited capacity to specialize — the rank-16 LoRA subspace is constrained enough\n    that specialized and universal corrections partially overlap (both learn \"general\n    robustness\" corrections that consume most of the limited rank budget, leaving\n    little room for type-specific corrections). At rank 32, experts have enough capacity\n    to learn the general correction AND type-specific refinements — this is where\n    specialization becomes meaningfully different from universality, producing the\n    inflection from 5.6→6.8pp. At rank 64, further capacity allows even finer specialization\n    but with diminishing marginal routing benefit (6.8→7.8pp, only +1.0pp more). The\n    universal LoRA scaling line (66.4→68.6→70.4) shows steady but decelerating gains\n    from rank alone, while the routed line (72.0→75.4→78.2) scales faster because\n    additional rank is leveraged differently by each expert. At r64, the routed system\n    (78.2%) approaches the rank-16 oracle level (77.0%), suggesting that higher-capacity\n    experts can partially compensate for imperfect routing. The capacity threshold\n    of r≥32 is a concrete, actionable finding: \"routing requires a minimum expert\n    capacity to be beneficial beyond parameter scaling.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Rank sweep: routing benefit vs expert capacity'\n    content: '| Expert Rank | 3×Expert (Routed) | Universal (Matched) | Routing Δ\n      | Total Params |\n\n      |-------------|-------------------|--------------------|-----------|----|\n\n      | r=16 | 72.0% | 66.4% | +5.6pp | 12M |\n\n      | r=32 | 75.4% | 68.6% | +6.8pp | 24M |\n\n      | r=64 | 78.2% | 70.4% | +7.8pp | 48M |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Dual-line plot: x=expert rank, y=overall success rate. ''Routed'' line (blue)\n    and ''Universal matched'' line (orange). Gap widens from r16 to r32 (inflection),\n    then continues at reduced slope to r64. Shaded area between lines = routing benefit.'\n  - 'Single-line plot: x=expert rank, y=routing benefit (Δ pp). Shows the inflection\n    at r32 and plateau toward r64.'\n  analysis: 'The rank sweep adds a concrete finding to the paper: \"routing benefit\n    scales with expert capacity, with an inflection at r≥32.\" At our operating point\n    (r16), routing benefit is 5.6pp — present as the minimum viable routing advantage.\n    The implication: practitioners with larger compute budgets should increase expert\n    rank to maximize the routing premium. The r64 routed result (78.2%) exceeding\n    the r16 oracle (77.0%) is a nice finding — higher-capacity experts + learned routing\n    can surpass perfect-routing of lower-capacity experts. Frame as: \"capacity and\n    routing are complementary dimensions of adaptation.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\n  plan_id: plan_015\n  description: 'Evaluate Meta-Brain on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO benchmark. Verify\n    that routing does not significantly degrade nominal performance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    frozen_pi0_clean_libero: 92.4\n    meta_brain_clean_libero: 90.4\n    clean_delta_pp: -2.0\n    universal_r16_clean_libero: 91.6\n    universal_r48_clean_libero: 91.2\n    oracle_clean_libero: 91.0\n    par_entropy_on_clean: 0.88\n    par_max_weight_on_clean: 0.48\n    par_routing_distribution_clean: roughly uniform (0.35, 0.31, 0.34)\n  result_rationale: 'Meta-Brain shows -2.0pp degradation on clean LIBERO (90.4% vs\n    frozen 92.4%). The mechanism: on clean inputs, no perturbation correction is needed,\n    but the PAR router still activates with non-trivial confidence. PAR entropy on\n    clean inputs is high (0.88 — near maximum entropy of ln(3)=1.10) and the routing\n    distribution is roughly uniform (0.35/0.31/0.34), meaning the router is uncertain\n    about which type to route to (correct behavior — there IS no perturbation type).\n    However, the near-uniform routing still applies a weighted average of all three\n    expert corrections (each contributing ~33%), introducing small representation\n    shifts that are not correcting any actual perturbation. These spurious corrections\n    occasionally push the policy off its optimal trajectory, causing -2.0pp degradation.\n    Universal LoRA variants show smaller degradation (-0.8pp for r16, -1.2pp for r48)\n    because their single correction direction is less disruptive when unnecessary.\n    Oracle routing on clean inputs (assigning a \"clean\" label that applies no correction)\n    would be 92.4% by definition, but since oracle routing in practice assigns perturbation\n    types (not a \"clean\" class), oracle on clean inputs must force-assign a type,\n    yielding 91.0% (-1.4pp). This motivates confidence-gated routing: if PAR entropy\n    exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.8), suppress expert corrections and fall back to\n    frozen representations.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Clean LIBERO performance\n    content: '| Method | Clean LIBERO (%) | Δ vs Frozen |\n\n      |--------|-----------------|-------------|\n\n      | Frozen pi0 | 92.4 | — |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 91.6 | −0.8 |\n\n      | Universal r48 | 91.2 | −1.2 |\n\n      | Oracle routing | 91.0 | −1.4 |\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 90.4 | −2.0 |\n\n      '\n  - title: PAR router behavior on clean inputs\n    content: '| Metric | Value |\n\n      |--------|-------|\n\n      | Avg entropy | 0.88 |\n\n      | Max expert weight | 0.48 |\n\n      | w(LoRA-V) | 0.35 |\n\n      | w(LoRA-S) | 0.31 |\n\n      | w(LoRA-P) | 0.34 |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Routing entropy histogram: bimodal — perturbed inputs cluster at low entropy\n    (0.3-0.7), clean inputs cluster at high entropy (0.8-1.0). Clear separation supports\n    entropy-based gating.'\n  analysis: 'The -2.0pp clean degradation is notable but manageable. The entropy analysis\n    provides a clean solution: confidence-gated routing. Add to the paper as a practical\n    recommendation: \"For deployment with mixed clean/perturbed inputs, we recommend\n    entropy-gated routing — suppress corrections when PAR entropy exceeds 0.8 (indicating\n    no clear perturbation type). This recovers frozen performance on clean inputs\n    while preserving routing benefits on perturbed inputs.\" Include the entropy histogram\n    as evidence that the gating threshold is well-defined (bimodal distribution with\n    clear separation).\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-backbone validation — OpenVLA\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 (perturbation classification) + core Phase 1 pipeline\n    on OpenVLA to test generalizability of perturbation-diagnostic routing across\n    architectures.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    openvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.94\n    openvla_phase0_best_layer: layer_8\n    openvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.86\n    openvla_frozen_overall: 48.2\n    openvla_universal_r16_overall: 55.8\n    openvla_meta_brain_overall: 61.4\n    openvla_routing_benefit: 5.6\n    openvla_gain_over_frozen: 13.2\n  result_rationale: 'Phase 0 partially replicates on OpenVLA: 3-class F1=0.94 is lower\n    than pi0''s 0.993 because OpenVLA''s Llama backbone produces less cleanly separable\n    perturbation representations than PaliGemma (Llama processes visual tokens through\n    more language-oriented layers, partially mixing perturbation signals). Best layer\n    shifts from layer 6 (pi0/PaliGemma) to layer 8 (OpenVLA/Llama), consistent with\n    different architecture depths. K=1 F1 drops more sharply (0.86 vs 0.898) because\n    single-step features are noisier in OpenVLA. Phase 1 routing benefit on OpenVLA\n    (~5.6pp over universal r16) is comparable to pi0''s 5.6pp over r48, suggesting\n    the routing principle generalizes even if absolute performance differs (OpenVLA''s\n    frozen baseline is lower at 48.2%). The similar routing benefit across backbones\n    is the key generalizability result — perturbation- diagnostic routing is not pi0-specific.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Cross-backbone comparison\n    content: '| Metric | pi0 (PaliGemma) | OpenVLA (Llama) |\n\n      |--------|-----------------|-----------------|\n\n      | Phase 0 3-class F1 | 0.993 | 0.94 |\n\n      | Phase 0 K=1 F1 | 0.898 | 0.86 |\n\n      | Best classification layer | 6 | 8 |\n\n      | Frozen baseline | 53.6% | 48.2% |\n\n      | Universal r16 | 62.1% | 55.8% |\n\n      | Meta-Brain | 72.0% | 61.4% |\n\n      | Routing Δ (vs universal r16) | +9.9pp | +5.6pp |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side t-SNE plots: pi0 layer 6 activations (tight clusters) vs OpenVLA\n    layer 8 activations (looser clusters, partial overlap). Both show 3-class structure.'\n  analysis: 'Cross-backbone results support generalizability with caveats. The routing\n    principle works on OpenVLA but with lower absolute numbers and less clean separation.\n    Present as: \"perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes across VLA architectures\n    — the routing benefit (5-6pp over universal LoRA) is consistent despite different\n    backbone feature characteristics.\" Honestly note that OpenVLA''s weaker Phase\n    0 classification (F1=0.94) sets a lower ceiling for routing quality. This is a\n    P2 experiment — if time is short, Phase 0 replication alone is sufficient for\n    a generalizability claim.\n\n    '\n- name: LoRA layer placement ablation\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid\n    (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers (default). Test per-expert-type layer\n    placement hypothesis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    lora_v_early_visual: 76.8\n    lora_v_mid_visual: 73.2\n    lora_v_late_visual: 70.4\n    lora_v_all_visual: 75.5\n    lora_s_early_spatial: 64.6\n    lora_s_mid_spatial: 70.8\n    lora_s_late_spatial: 66.2\n    lora_s_all_spatial: 69.2\n    lora_p_early_physical: 57.4\n    lora_p_mid_physical: 59.2\n    lora_p_late_physical: 60.8\n    lora_p_all_physical: 61.0\n    optimal_v: early (2-6)\n    optimal_s: mid (6-12)\n    optimal_p: late (12-16) ≈ all layers\n  result_rationale: 'Layer placement shows a partial match to the V→early, S→mid,\n    P→late hypothesis, but with important nuances. LoRA-V is best at early layers\n    (76.8%) because visual perturbations corrupt early representations most — intervening\n    at layers 2-6 corrects corrupted visual features before they propagate to higher\n    layers. LoRA-V at all layers (75.5%) is slightly worse because later LoRA modules\n    introduce unnecessary corrections at layers where visual features are already\n    clean. LoRA-S is best at mid layers (70.8%) because spatial perturbations (layout\n    changes) affect mid-level spatial reasoning where object relationships are encoded\n    — early layers still process raw visual features correctly, and late layers have\n    already committed to action plans. LoRA-P shows the weakest layer preference:\n    late layers (60.8%) are marginally best but barely exceed all layers (61.0%),\n    confirming that physical perturbation correction is weak throughout the representation\n    hierarchy. The lack of strong layer preference for LoRA-P reinforces the median-tier\n    narrative that physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation\n    level. The layer placement analysis provides a concrete practical insight: type-specific\n    layer assignment (V→early, S→mid) could improve parameter efficiency by 40% (5\n    layers × 3 experts = 15 LoRA modules vs 18 layers × 3 experts = 54 modules in\n    the default config).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Layer placement ablation (per-type success rate with matched expert)\n    content: '| Expert | Early (2-6) | Mid (6-12) | Late (12-16) | All (default) |\n      Best |\n\n      |--------|-------------|------------|-------------|---------------|------|\n\n      | LoRA-V on Visual | **76.8** | 73.2 | 70.4 | 75.5 | Early |\n\n      | LoRA-S on Spatial | 64.6 | **70.8** | 66.2 | 69.2 | Mid |\n\n      | LoRA-P on Physical | 57.4 | 59.2 | 60.8 | **61.0** | All ≈ Late |\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - '3-panel line plot (one per expert type): x=layer range, y=success rate. LoRA-V\n    peaks early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P is nearly flat. Clear mechanistic alignment\n    for V and S; P shows no useful structure.'\n  analysis: 'Layer placement provides a practical insight for the paper''s \"layer-perturbation\n    mapping\" narrative. V→early and S→mid are clear wins that align with VLA processing\n    stages. P→late/all is ambiguous, reinforcing that physical perturbation correction\n    at the representation level is fundamentally limited. Present as: \"perturbation-type-to-layer\n    mapping reveals that visual perturbations are best corrected at early layers (pre-spatial-reasoning)\n    and spatial perturbations at mid layers (spatial-reasoning stage), suggesting\n    type-aware layer assignment as a parameter efficiency optimization.\" Note: all-layer\n    (default) is competitive for all types, so the default config is a reasonable\n    choice if practitioners don''t want to tune layer assignment.'\n","numLines":1048,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1048}}}
