{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QaArndFzM52nrWquVD5iUc","type":"tool_result","content":"# Review Form Analysis for CoRL 2024\n\nBased on the provided content, **no complete review form with scoring dimensions and rating scales is included** in this instruction document.\n\nThe page contains:\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria:** Papers evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact.\"\n\n**Desk Reject Criteria:** Three grounds for rejection—formatting issues, anonymity violations, or scope misalignment. The scope requirement states submissions must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through either explicit learning focus or physical robot testing.\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:** Reviewers should have expertise demonstrated by \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences\" or equivalent machine learning venues.\n\n## What IS NOT Provided:\n\n- Numeric rating scales or score ranges\n- Specific scoring dimensions (e.g., originality, clarity, significance scores)\n- Definitions of what each score means\n- Accept/reject decision thresholds\n- Complete review rubric or form template\n\nThe document appears to be instructional guidance rather than the actual peer review form reviewers would complete."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"26f12a20-020c-469a-b315-4da8d3064aad","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:17:40.381Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":133964,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Review Form Analysis for CoRL 2024\n\nBased on the provided content, **no complete review form with scoring dimensions and rating scales is included** in this instruction document.\n\nThe page contains:\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria:** Papers evaluated on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact.\"\n\n**Desk Reject Criteria:** Three grounds for rejection—formatting issues, anonymity violations, or scope misalignment. The scope requirement states submissions must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through either explicit learning focus or physical robot testing.\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:** Reviewers should have expertise demonstrated by \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences\" or equivalent machine learning venues.\n\n## What IS NOT Provided:\n\n- Numeric rating scales or score ranges\n- Specific scoring dimensions (e.g., originality, clarity, significance scores)\n- Definitions of what each score means\n- Accept/reject decision thresholds\n- Complete review rubric or form template\n\nThe document appears to be instructional guidance rather than the actual peer review form reviewers would complete.","durationMs":4151,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EEYfEGdbsT26bjJjUZEdPy","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, CoRL 2023 does **not provide a numerical scoring rubric or accept/reject decision criteria** in their reviewer guidelines.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation areas, but without numerical scales:\n\n1. **Originality**: \"Are the problems or methods new?\" Novel combinations of techniques are valued. Clarity on differentiation from prior work is essential.\n\n2. **Quality**: Technical soundness, evidence-based claims, appropriate methodology, completeness, and honest assessment of strengths and weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity**: Writing quality, organization, and reproducibility. \"A well written paper provides enough information for an expert reader to reproduce its results.\"\n\n4. **Significance**: Importance of results and potential impact on the field and practitioners.\n\n5. **Relevance**: \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning?\" and whether the CoRL audience would find it valuable.\n\n6. **Limitations**: Papers must include a limitations section. \"Treat the honestly reported limitations kindly,\" while unreported flaws should be heavily weighted in scoring.\n\n## Decision Framework\n\nRather than rigid numerical scales, the guidelines emphasize:\n- Being constructive, concrete, courteous, and substantive\n- Starting with positive aspects\n- Providing evidence for claims\n- Recognizing that novel perspectives matter alongside SOTA performance\n\nNo explicit accept/reject thresholds are documented."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"a0ec6e7f-355f-4925-accc-036eada548aa","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:17:42.399Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, CoRL 2023 does **not provide a numerical scoring rubric or accept/reject decision criteria** in their reviewer guidelines.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation areas, but without numerical scales:\n\n1. **Originality**: \"Are the problems or methods new?\" Novel combinations of techniques are valued. Clarity on differentiation from prior work is essential.\n\n2. **Quality**: Technical soundness, evidence-based claims, appropriate methodology, completeness, and honest assessment of strengths and weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity**: Writing quality, organization, and reproducibility. \"A well written paper provides enough information for an expert reader to reproduce its results.\"\n\n4. **Significance**: Importance of results and potential impact on the field and practitioners.\n\n5. **Relevance**: \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning?\" and whether the CoRL audience would find it valuable.\n\n6. **Limitations**: Papers must include a limitations section. \"Treat the honestly reported limitations kindly,\" while unreported flaws should be heavily weighted in scoring.\n\n## Decision Framework\n\nRather than rigid numerical scales, the guidelines emphasize:\n- Being constructive, concrete, courteous, and substantive\n- Starting with positive aspects\n- Providing evidence for claims\n- Recognizing that novel perspectives matter alongside SOTA performance\n\nNo explicit accept/reject thresholds are documented.","durationMs":5277,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
