{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NRjRUjDwiLEW7pGBTy1Wg9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginal above threshold, 5=marginal below, 4=below threshold, 3=clear reject)\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires: relevance to robot learning, learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots\n- Acceptance rate ~25-35%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea:** Frozen VLA (pi0) activations at a single layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996). Meta-Brain uses a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) — a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations — to produce soft routing weights over 3 type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus: 72.0% overall with ~12M params, +18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (p=0.008).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Meta-Brain framework: perturbation-diagnostic routing for VLA robustness (72.0% on LIBERO-Plus, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal)\n2. Comprehensive ablation: ablation ladder, parameter-matched baselines, oracle, rank sweep, layer placement, cross-backbone\n3. Practical insight: perturbation types map to correction layers (V→early, S→mid), routing benefit scales with capacity (inflection r≥32)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- VLA Robustness: LIBERO-Plus benchmark, VLA-GSE (MoE capacity routing, 81.2% with 114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B), RobustVLA (uniform adversarial training), SAFE (detection without correction), FTM (visual-only, 4K params)\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL (task-routing), LoRA-SP (rank-based). Claims Meta-Brain introduces new routing dimension (perturbation type)\n\n**Method:**\n- Phase 0: Linear probe shows frozen PaliGemma layer 6 activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.996)\n- PAR Router: 2-layer MLP on frozen activations → soft routing weights\n- 3 LoRA experts (rank 16, Q/K/V/O, all 18 layers) for Visual/Spatial/Physical\n- Soft blending: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h_frozen)\n- Two-stage training: (1) independent expert training, (2) router fine-tuning with frozen experts\n\n**Key results:**\n- 72.0% overall on LIBERO-Plus (+5.6pp over parameter-matched r48, p=0.008)\n- Routing benefit strongest on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp), weakest on Physical (+2.4pp)\n- Random routing < Universal r48 (proves routing quality matters)\n- Oracle gap 5.0pp (uniform across types)\n- Cross-backbone on OpenVLA: routing benefit ~5.6pp consistent\n- Clean LIBERO degradation: -2.0pp\n- FTM comparison: FTM 73.8% visual only, Meta-Brain comparable visual + multi-type coverage\n- VLA-GSE 81.2% with 114M params (different backbone, indirect comparison)\n\n**Key prior work to evaluate against:**\n- **MoE/Routing in general:** Mixture-of-Experts literature (Shazeer et al., Switch Transformer, etc.) — is routing by perturbation type genuinely novel vs. standard MoE?\n- **VLA-GSE:** Routes by per-token capacity, achieves higher absolute performance — how does Meta-Brain's perturbation-diagnostic routing differ architecturally? Is the difference fundamental or superficial?\n- **MoIRA, CORAL:** Task-routing LoRA — how is perturbation routing different from task routing conceptually?\n- **SAFE:** Detects perturbations for safety gating — how does Meta-Brain's Phase 0 differ from SAFE's detection?\n- **Adversarial robustness literature:** Domain adaptation, test-time adaptation — is the problem formulation novel?\n- **Conditional computation:** The soft routing over LoRA experts is essentially conditional computation — is this acknowledged?\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on NOVELTY/ORIGINALITY and RELATED WORK POSITIONING. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on novelty, conceptual contribution, positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on originality concerns, missing related work, overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Scoring** — provide individual scores for:\n   - Originality (1-10)\n   - Related work coverage (1-10)\n   - Significance of contribution (1-10)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is routing by perturbation type genuinely a new idea or a straightforward application of MoE to a new axis? Is the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) surprising or expected? How does this compare to domain-adaptive methods in computer vision? Is the positioning against VLA-GSE fair given different backbones? Are there important missing references (test-time adaptation, domain-specific adapters, conditional batch normalization, etc.)?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":923,"cache_read_input_tokens":69947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":923},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"b9c35555-3a00-41d7-916b-3af94378aba2"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a8bed8bbd0bc630ea","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginal above threshold, 5=marginal below, 4=below threshold, 3=clear reject)\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires: relevance to robot learning, learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots\n- Acceptance rate ~25-35%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea:** Frozen VLA (pi0) activations at a single layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996). Meta-Brain uses a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) — a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations — to produce soft routing weights over 3 type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus: 72.0% overall with ~12M params, +18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (p=0.008).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Meta-Brain framework: perturbation-diagnostic routing for VLA robustness (72.0% on LIBERO-Plus, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal)\n2. Comprehensive ablation: ablation ladder, parameter-matched baselines, oracle, rank sweep, layer placement, cross-backbone\n3. Practical insight: perturbation types map to correction layers (V→early, S→mid), routing benefit scales with capacity (inflection r≥32)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- VLA Robustness: LIBERO-Plus benchmark, VLA-GSE (MoE capacity routing, 81.2% with 114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B), RobustVLA (uniform adversarial training), SAFE (detection without correction), FTM (visual-only, 4K params)\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL (task-routing), LoRA-SP (rank-based). Claims Meta-Brain introduces new routing dimension (perturbation type)\n\n**Method:**\n- Phase 0: Linear probe shows frozen PaliGemma layer 6 activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.996)\n- PAR Router: 2-layer MLP on frozen activations → soft routing weights\n- 3 LoRA experts (rank 16, Q/K/V/O, all 18 layers) for Visual/Spatial/Physical\n- Soft blending: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h_frozen)\n- Two-stage training: (1) independent expert training, (2) router fine-tuning with frozen experts\n\n**Key results:**\n- 72.0% overall on LIBERO-Plus (+5.6pp over parameter-matched r48, p=0.008)\n- Routing benefit strongest on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp), weakest on Physical (+2.4pp)\n- Random routing < Universal r48 (proves routing quality matters)\n- Oracle gap 5.0pp (uniform across types)\n- Cross-backbone on OpenVLA: routing benefit ~5.6pp consistent\n- Clean LIBERO degradation: -2.0pp\n- FTM comparison: FTM 73.8% visual only, Meta-Brain comparable visual + multi-type coverage\n- VLA-GSE 81.2% with 114M params (different backbone, indirect comparison)\n\n**Key prior work to evaluate against:**\n- **MoE/Routing in general:** Mixture-of-Experts literature (Shazeer et al., Switch Transformer, etc.) — is routing by perturbation type genuinely novel vs. standard MoE?\n- **VLA-GSE:** Routes by per-token capacity, achieves higher absolute performance — how does Meta-Brain's perturbation-diagnostic routing differ architecturally? Is the difference fundamental or superficial?\n- **MoIRA, CORAL:** Task-routing LoRA — how is perturbation routing different from task routing conceptually?\n- **SAFE:** Detects perturbations for safety gating — how does Meta-Brain's Phase 0 differ from SAFE's detection?\n- **Adversarial robustness literature:** Domain adaptation, test-time adaptation — is the problem formulation novel?\n- **Conditional computation:** The soft routing over LoRA experts is essentially conditional computation — is this acknowledged?\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on NOVELTY/ORIGINALITY and RELATED WORK POSITIONING. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on novelty, conceptual contribution, positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on originality concerns, missing related work, overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Scoring** — provide individual scores for:\n   - Originality (1-10)\n   - Related work coverage (1-10)\n   - Significance of contribution (1-10)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is routing by perturbation type genuinely a new idea or a straightforward application of MoE to a new axis? Is the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) surprising or expected? How does this compare to domain-adaptive methods in computer vision? Is the positioning against VLA-GSE fair given different backbones? Are there important missing references (test-time adaptation, domain-specific adapters, conditional batch normalization, etc.)?","uuid":"780acff6-11cc-495f-9c7d-ebcac819686c","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginal above threshold, 5=marginal below, 4=below threshold, 3=clear reject)\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires: relevance to robot learning, learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots\n- Acceptance rate ~25-35%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea:** Frozen VLA (pi0) activations at a single layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996). Meta-Brain uses a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) — a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations — to produce soft routing weights over 3 type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus: 72.0% overall with ~12M params, +18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (p=0.008).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Meta-Brain framework: perturbation-diagnostic routing for VLA robustness (72.0% on LIBERO-Plus, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal)\n2. Comprehensive ablation: ablation ladder, parameter-matched baselines, oracle, rank sweep, layer placement, cross-backbone\n3. Practical insight: perturbation types map to correction layers (V→early, S→mid), routing benefit scales with capacity (inflection r≥32)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- VLA Robustness: LIBERO-Plus benchmark, VLA-GSE (MoE capacity routing, 81.2% with 114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B), RobustVLA (uniform adversarial training), SAFE (detection without correction), FTM (visual-only, 4K params)\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL (task-routing), LoRA-SP (rank-based). Claims Meta-Brain introduces new routing dimension (perturbation type)\n\n**Method:**\n- Phase 0: Linear probe shows frozen PaliGemma layer 6 activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.996)\n- PAR Router: 2-layer MLP on frozen activations → soft routing weights\n- 3 LoRA experts (rank 16, Q/K/V/O, all 18 layers) for Visual/Spatial/Physical\n- Soft blending: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h_frozen)\n- Two-stage training: (1) independent expert training, (2) router fine-tuning with frozen experts\n\n**Key results:**\n- 72.0% overall on LIBERO-Plus (+5.6pp over parameter-matched r48, p=0.008)\n- Routing benefit strongest on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp), weakest on Physical (+2.4pp)\n- Random routing < Universal r48 (proves routing quality matters)\n- Oracle gap 5.0pp (uniform across types)\n- Cross-backbone on OpenVLA: routing benefit ~5.6pp consistent\n- Clean LIBERO degradation: -2.0pp\n- FTM comparison: FTM 73.8% visual only, Meta-Brain comparable visual + multi-type coverage\n- VLA-GSE 81.2% with 114M params (different backbone, indirect comparison)\n\n**Key prior work to evaluate against:**\n- **MoE/Routing in general:** Mixture-of-Experts literature (Shazeer et al., Switch Transformer, etc.) — is routing by perturbation type genuinely novel vs. standard MoE?\n- **VLA-GSE:** Routes by per-token capacity, achieves higher absolute performance — how does Meta-Brain's perturbation-diagnostic routing differ architecturally? Is the difference fundamental or superficial?\n- **MoIRA, CORAL:** Task-routing LoRA — how is perturbation routing different from task routing conceptually?\n- **SAFE:** Detects perturbations for safety gating — how does Meta-Brain's Phase 0 differ from SAFE's detection?\n- **Adversarial robustness literature:** Domain adaptation, test-time adaptation — is the problem formulation novel?\n- **Conditional computation:** The soft routing over LoRA experts is essentially conditional computation — is this acknowledged?\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on NOVELTY/ORIGINALITY and RELATED WORK POSITIONING. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on novelty, conceptual contribution, positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on originality concerns, missing related work, overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Scoring** — provide individual scores for:\n   - Originality (1-10)\n   - Related work coverage (1-10)\n   - Significance of contribution (1-10)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is routing by perturbation type genuinely a new idea or a straightforward application of MoE to a new axis? Is the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) surprising or expected? How does this compare to domain-adaptive methods in computer vision? Is the positioning against VLA-GSE fair given different backbones? Are there important missing references (test-time adaptation, domain-specific adapters, conditional batch normalization, etc.)?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"cbc8d55e-8a61-4786-a2ce-305b302b44f8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:19:32.427Z"}
