{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014LfxbvAc3fB73Mj6YCEwRf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LQvaidSfR4w1sxXrcSLyot","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/best/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2582,"cache_read_input_tokens":27242,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2582,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2Ktz6qFgCU29M2M8WwFkW","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"e7e9785a-4a49-462a-afd5-91f07d99a9b6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQvaidSfR4w1sxXrcSLyot","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n11\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{times}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{Meta-Brain}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\liplus}{LIBERO-Plus}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{\\ours{}: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts\\\\for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\t  Anonymous Authors\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t%% ========================================\n30\t%% ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ========================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve strong performance on standard manipulation benchmarks but degrade sharply under environmental perturbations.\n34\tExisting robustness methods apply either single-modality correction or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation, ignoring a key signal: VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type.\n35\tWe introduce \\ours{}, a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts for targeted correction.\n36\tA lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) extracts a single-layer, single-token activation from the frozen backbone, classifies the perturbation type (visual, spatial, or physical), and soft-routes to the corresponding LoRA expert.\n37\tOn \\liplus{}, \\ours{} achieves 78.9\\% task success with ${\\sim}12$M trainable parameters, outperforming a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (65.1\\%) by $13.8\\pp$ ($p{<}0.001$).\n38\tRandom routing over the same expert bank yields only $65.8\\%$ ($+0.7\\pp$ over universal, $p{=}0.38$, n.s.), ruling out ensemble effects and confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing---not merely expert diversity---drives the gain.\n39\tThe oracle routing upper bound (80.7\\%) is only $1.8\\pp$ above \\ours{}, confirming near-optimal routing quality.\n40\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA shows consistent routing benefit ($+14.2\\pp$), and clean LIBERO performance is fully preserved ($+0.3\\pp$).\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%% ========================================\n44\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n45\t%% ========================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\\label{sec:intro}\n48\t\n49\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) such as pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0}, OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla}, and RT-2~\\cite{brohan2023rt2} achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks.\n50\tHowever, when deployed under environmental perturbations---camera viewpoint shifts, lighting changes, object layout randomization, or altered robot kinematics---performance collapses.\n51\tOn \\liplus{}~\\cite{liberoplus2026}, a recently proposed robustness benchmark extending LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023libero} with six perturbation types, a frozen pi0 model drops from 91.2\\% to 53.6\\%.\n52\t\n53\tDifferent perturbation types disrupt different processing stages of the VLA.\n54\tVisual perturbations (camera, lighting, background, noise) corrupt early-layer representations.\n55\tSpatial perturbations (object layout changes) invalidate mid-level trajectory planning.\n56\tPhysical perturbations (altered robot initial states) break late-layer action planning assumptions.\n57\tDespite this diversity, existing robustness methods either apply single-modality correction~\\cite{yang2025ftm,yang2025fla} that cannot generalize across perturbation types, or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation~\\cite{luo2025robustvla,chen2025srpo} that wastes capacity by applying identical correction regardless of failure mode.\n58\tCapacity-based mixture-of-experts approaches such as VLA-GSE~\\cite{vlagserouting2026} route by per-token computation needs but remain unaware of perturbation type---the same gating configuration handles camera jitter and layout randomization.\n59\t\n60\tWe make a key empirical observation: \\textbf{frozen VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy}.\n61\tProbing PaliGemma layer~6 first-token activations in a frozen pi0 model yields 3-class (visual/spatial/physical) $\\text{F1}{=}1.0$ with a single nearest-neighbor classifier at $K{=}1$.\n62\tThis diagnostic signal is available at zero computational overhead from a single forward pass, yet no existing method exploits it for correction routing.\n63\t\n64\tWe introduce \\ours{}, a modular framework that closes the diagnose-then-correct loop:\n65\t\\begin{enumerate}\n66\t    \\item A \\textbf{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)} extracts a frozen activation, classifies perturbation type via a lightweight 2-layer MLP, and produces soft routing weights.\n67\t    \\item An \\textbf{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB)} of three type-specialized experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P, each rank-16, ${\\sim}3.7$M params) applies the weighted correction: $h_{\\text{corrected}} = h_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_k p(\\text{type}_k) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(h_{\\text{frozen}})$.\n68\t\\end{enumerate}\n69\t\n70\t\\noindent\\textbf{Contributions.}\n71\t(1)~We demonstrate that frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-token granularity, enabling zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis.\n72\t(2)~We propose \\ours{}, which routes diagnosed perturbation types to specialized LoRA experts, achieving 78.9\\% on \\liplus{} with ${\\sim}12$M params---outperforming a parameter-matched universal LoRA by $13.8\\pp$.\n73\t(3)~We provide comprehensive ablations including cross-type mismatch analysis, rank scaling, layer placement, and cross-backbone validation, establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as a general design principle for VLA robustness.\n74\t\n75\t\n76\t%% ========================================\n77\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n78\t%% ========================================\n79\t\\section{Related Work}\n80\t\\label{sec:related}\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{VLA Foundations.}\n83\tRecent VLAs integrate vision encoders with language model backbones for end-to-end visuomotor control.\n84\tPi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} builds on PaliGemma for flow-matching action generation.\n85\tOpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} and SmolVLA~\\cite{smolvla2025} provide open-source alternatives at varying scales.\n86\tOcto~\\cite{octo2024} and RT-2~\\cite{brohan2023rt2} demonstrate generalization via language conditioning.\n87\tAll share a common vulnerability: environmental perturbations not seen during pretraining cause sharp performance drops.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n90\tVLA-GSE~\\cite{vlagserouting2026} adds mixture-of-experts gating to VLA layers, routing tokens by learned capacity needs (114M extra params, 81.2\\% on \\liplus{} with Qwen3-VL-4B backbone).\n91\tSAFE~\\cite{safe2026} detects perturbations via activation anomaly scores but applies no correction.\n92\tRobustVLA~\\cite{luo2025robustvla} uses adversarial training for uniform robustness.\n93\tFTM~\\cite{yang2025ftm} and FLA~\\cite{yang2025fla} achieve strong visual robustness via feature masking but cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n94\tSRPO~\\cite{chen2025srpo} and CorridorVLA~\\cite{corridorvla2026} train full models for robustness.\n95\tNone combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction routing.\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{Mixture-of-Experts Routing.}\n98\tSparse MoE architectures~\\cite{fedus2022switch,lepikhin2020gshard,zoph2022stmoe} route tokens to specialized sub-networks for capacity scaling.\n99\tV-MoE~\\cite{riquelme2021vmoe} adapts this to vision transformers.\n100\tThese methods route by computational demand or token similarity, not by diagnosed failure mode.\n101\t\\ours{}'s perturbation-diagnostic routing is orthogonal and complementary to capacity-based routing.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation.}\n104\tLoRA~\\cite{hu2021lora} enables efficient fine-tuning via low-rank weight updates.\n105\tExtensions include AdaLoRA~\\cite{zhang2023adalora} for rank allocation, DoRA~\\cite{liu2024dora} for weight decomposition, and LoRA-SP~\\cite{lorasp2025} for structure-preserving adaptation.\n106\tTask-conditioned LoRA routing appears in MoIRA~\\cite{moira2025} and CORAL~\\cite{coral2025}, which route by task identity.\n107\t\\ours{} routes by perturbation type rather than task identity---a finer-grained signal available from internal activations without explicit task labels.\n108\t\n109\t\n110\t%% ========================================\n111\t%% 3. METHOD\n112\t%% ========================================\n113\t\\section{Method}\n114\t\\label{sec:method}\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n117\t\\label{sec:problem}\n118\t\n119\tWe consider a frozen VLA backbone $f_\\theta$ (pi0 with PaliGemma vision encoder) that maps observations $o_t$ to actions $a_t$.\n120\tUnder environmental perturbation $\\delta \\in \\{\\text{visual}, \\text{spatial}, \\text{physical}\\}$, the perturbed observation $\\tilde{o}_t = o_t + \\delta$ causes performance degradation.\n121\tOur goal: restore performance by applying a small, type-specific correction to the frozen backbone's intermediate representations, without modifying $\\theta$.\n122\t\n123\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosability}\n124\t\\label{sec:phase0}\n125\t\n126\tWe first establish that frozen activations carry perturbation-type information.\n127\tLet $h_t^{(\\ell)} = f_\\theta^{(\\ell)}(\\tilde{o}_t)$ denote the hidden activation at layer $\\ell$ for perturbed input $\\tilde{o}_t$.\n128\tWe extract the first-token activation $h_t^{(\\ell)}[0]$ and train a $K$-nearest-neighbor classifier on episode-averaged features.\n129\t\n130\t\\textbf{Key finding}: At PaliGemma layer~6, a single nearest neighbor ($K{=}1$) achieves $\\text{F1}{=}1.000$ on 3-class classification (visual/spatial/physical) using first-token pooling.\n131\tThis indicates that perturbation-type information consolidates at an early-to-mid layer and is accessible from a single token at a single timestep---no temporal aggregation or multi-token pooling required.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{\\ours{} Architecture}\n134\t\\label{sec:architecture}\n135\t\n136\t\\ours{} comprises two modules operating on the frozen backbone.\n137\t\n138\t\\paragraph{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR).}\n139\tPAR extracts $h_t^{(6)}[0]$ (layer~6, first token) from the frozen PaliGemma forward pass and applies a 2-layer MLP:\n140\t\\begin{equation}\n141\t    \\mathbf{p} = \\text{softmax}\\!\\big(W_2 \\cdot \\text{ReLU}(W_1 \\cdot h_t^{(6)}[0] + b_1) + b_2\\big) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3\n142\t    \\label{eq:par}\n143\t\\end{equation}\n144\twhere $\\mathbf{p} = [p_V, p_S, p_P]$ are soft routing weights over the three perturbation types.\n145\tPAR adds 0.53M parameters and negligible latency (shared forward pass, single MLP evaluation).\n146\t\n147\t\\paragraph{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB).}\n148\tThree LoRA experts---LoRA-V (visual), LoRA-S (spatial), LoRA-P (physical)---each apply rank-16 low-rank updates to Q/K/V/O projections across all 18 PaliGemma layers (${\\sim}3.7$M params each).\n149\tThe corrected hidden state at each layer is:\n150\t\\begin{equation}\n151\t    h_{\\text{corrected}} = h_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_{k \\in \\{V,S,P\\}} p_k \\cdot \\Delta W_k \\cdot h_{\\text{frozen}}\n152\t    \\label{eq:correction}\n153\t\\end{equation}\n154\twhere $\\Delta W_k = B_k A_k$ is the LoRA update for expert $k$.\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Training Protocol.}\n157\tTraining proceeds in two stages:\n158\t(1)~\\textbf{Expert training}: each LoRA expert is trained independently on its matched perturbation type (LoRA-V on visual augmentations, LoRA-S on oracle spatial demos, LoRA-P on oracle physical demos) for 15 epochs.\n159\t(2)~\\textbf{Router fine-tuning}: LoRA parameters are frozen; only PAR weights are trained end-to-end on the full \\liplus{} mix for 5 epochs, optimizing task loss through the soft-routed correction.\n160\tTotal trainable parameters: $3 \\times 3.7\\text{M} + 0.5\\text{M} \\approx 11.6\\text{M}$.\n161\t\n162\t\n163\t%% ========================================\n164\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n165\t%% ========================================\n166\t\\section{Experiments}\n167\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n168\t\n169\t\\subsection{Setup}\n170\t\\label{sec:setup}\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{Benchmark.}\n173\tWe evaluate on \\liplus{}~\\cite{liberoplus2026}, which extends LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023libero} with six perturbation types organized into three categories: \\textit{visual} (camera jitter, lighting change, background swap, Gaussian noise), \\textit{spatial} (object layout randomization), and \\textit{physical} (robot initial state variation).\n174\tWe report task success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 seeds unless stated otherwise.\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Backbone.}\n177\tOur primary backbone is pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} with PaliGemma vision encoder (18 transformer layers).\n178\tCross-backbone validation uses SmolVLA~\\cite{smolvla2025} (${\\sim}12$ layers).\n179\t\n180\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n181\t(1)~\\textbf{Frozen pi0}: no adaptation.\n182\t(2)~\\textbf{Universal LoRA r16}: single LoRA (rank 16, 3.7M) trained on mixed perturbation data.\n183\t(3)~\\textbf{Universal LoRA r48}: single LoRA (rank 48, ${\\sim}12$M) parameter-matched to \\ours{}.\n184\t(4)~\\textbf{Random Routing}: 3 specialized experts with uniform random routing weights (${\\sim}12$M).\n185\t(5)~\\textbf{Oracle Routing}: ground-truth perturbation labels for hard one-hot routing (${\\sim}12$M).\n186\t(6)~\\textbf{FTM}~\\cite{yang2025ftm}: feature masking baseline (4K params, visual-only).\n187\t\n188\t\n189\t\\subsection{Main Results: Ablation Ladder}\n190\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n191\t\n192\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder} presents the systematic ablation ladder on \\liplus{}.\n193\tThe progression is strictly monotonic: Frozen (53.6\\%) $<$ Universal-r16 (62.4\\%) $<$ Universal-r48 (65.1\\%) $<$ Random (65.8\\%) $<$ \\ours{} (78.9\\%) $<$ Oracle (80.7\\%).\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[t]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\caption{Ablation ladder on \\liplus{} (5 seeds). \\ours{} outperforms the parameter-matched universal LoRA (r48) by $13.8\\pp$ ($p{<}0.001$). Random routing provides negligible gain over universal-r48 ($+0.7\\pp$, $p{=}0.38$, n.s.), ruling out ensemble effects.}\n198\t\\label{tab:ablation_ladder}\n199\t\\small\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tMethod & Params & Overall (\\%) & Visual & Spatial & Physical \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 53.6 $\\pm$ 0.8 & 50.8 & 57.5 & 56.2 \\\\\n205\tUniversal r16 & 3.7M & 62.4 $\\pm$ 1.4 & 64.8 & 60.5 & 58.2 \\\\\n206\tUniversal r48 & 12M & 65.1 $\\pm$ 1.3 & 67.5 & 63.2 & 60.8 \\\\\n207\tRandom Routing & 12M & 65.8 $\\pm$ 2.1 & 66.8 & 65.5 & 63.2 \\\\\n208\t\\textbf{\\ours{} (ours)} & \\textbf{12M} & \\textbf{78.9 $\\pm$ 1.2} & \\textbf{81.2} & \\textbf{77.5} & \\textbf{74.8} \\\\\n209\tOracle Routing & 12M & 80.7 $\\pm$ 0.9 & 83.0 & 79.2 & 76.5 \\\\\n210\t\\bottomrule\n211\t\\end{tabular}\n212\t\\end{table}\n213\t\n214\tThree key comparisons isolate the source of \\ours{}'s gains:\n215\t\n216\t\\textit{Routing vs.\\ capacity scaling.}\n217\tUniversal-r48 (12M) improves only $+2.7\\pp$ over Universal-r16 (3.7M), while \\ours{} (12M) improves $+13.8\\pp$ over Universal-r48 at the same parameter count.\n218\tThe 3.2$\\times$ parameter increase from r16 to r48 yields diminishing returns because the single adapter must compromise across three conflicting correction directions.\n219\t\n220\t\\textit{Routing vs.\\ ensemble.}\n221\tRandom routing ($65.8\\%$) gains only $+0.7\\pp$ over Universal-r48 ($p{=}0.38$, not significant).\n222\tSince random routing uses the same three specialized experts as \\ours{} but without perturbation-aware selection, this null result rules out the alternative hypothesis that expert diversity alone (ensemble effect) explains \\ours{}'s performance.\n223\t\n224\t\\textit{Routing quality.}\n225\tThe oracle gap is only $1.8\\pp$ ($p{=}0.03$), confirming that PAR achieves near-optimal routing with 96.8\\% routing accuracy.\n226\t\n227\t\\begin{table}[t]\n228\t\\centering\n229\t\\caption{Statistical significance (paired $t$-test, 5 seeds).}\n230\t\\label{tab:significance}\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n233\t\\toprule\n234\tComparison & Gap (pp) & $p$-value & Significant? \\\\\n235\t\\midrule\n236\t\\ours{} vs.\\ Universal r48 & 13.8 & ${<}0.001$ & Yes (***) \\\\\n237\t\\ours{} vs.\\ Random & 13.1 & ${<}0.001$ & Yes (***) \\\\\n238\tRandom vs.\\ Universal r48 & 0.7 & 0.38 & No (n.s.) \\\\\n239\tOracle vs.\\ \\ours{} & 1.8 & 0.03 & Yes (*) \\\\\n240\t\\bottomrule\n241\t\\end{tabular}\n242\t\\end{table}\n243\t\n244\t\n245\t\\subsection{Per-Perturbation Breakdown}\n246\t\\label{sec:per_perturbation}\n247\t\n248\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_perturbation} reports \\ours{}'s per-perturbation performance.\n249\tAll six perturbation types show large improvements over frozen pi0 ($+18.6$ to $+31.3\\pp$).\n250\tVisual perturbations benefit most ($+28.9$ to $+31.3\\pp$) because low-rank visual correction is the most regular transformation.\n251\tThe PAR routing weights are near-diagonal: on visual perturbations, LoRA-V receives weight $0.89$--$0.93$; on spatial, LoRA-S receives $0.88$; on physical, LoRA-P receives $0.86$.\n252\tRouting entropy is low across all types ($0.12$--$0.18$ vs.\\ maximum $\\log 3 = 1.10$), indicating confident, near-deterministic routing.\n253\t\n254\t\\begin{table}[t]\n255\t\\centering\n256\t\\caption{\\ours{} per-perturbation breakdown on \\liplus{}.}\n257\t\\label{tab:per_perturbation}\n258\t\\small\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n260\t\\toprule\n261\tPerturbation & Type & Success (\\%) & $\\Delta$ Frozen & Top Routing Wt. \\\\\n262\t\\midrule\n263\tCamera    & Visual   & 78.5 & +30.3 & LoRA-V: 0.92 \\\\\n264\tLight     & Visual   & 83.8 & +31.3 & LoRA-V: 0.93 \\\\\n265\tBackground& Visual   & 80.2 & +29.4 & LoRA-V: 0.90 \\\\\n266\tNoise     & Visual   & 82.1 & +28.9 & LoRA-V: 0.89 \\\\\n267\tLayout    & Spatial  & 77.5 & +20.0 & LoRA-S: 0.88 \\\\\n268\tRobot     & Physical & 74.8 & +18.6 & LoRA-P: 0.86 \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tOverall   & ---      & 78.9 & +25.3 & --- \\\\\n271\t\\bottomrule\n272\t\\end{tabular}\n273\t\\end{table}\n274\t\n275\t\n276\t\\subsection{Expert Specialization: Cross-Type Analysis}\n277\t\\label{sec:cross_type}\n278\t\n279\tTo validate that expert specialization is essential (not merely beneficial), we evaluate each LoRA expert on all three perturbation types (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_type}).\n280\tDiagonal entries (matched expert--type) substantially outperform off-diagonal entries, with an average cross-type drop of $17.4\\pp$.\n281\t\n282\tLoRA-V achieves 80.2\\% on visual perturbations but only 62.0\\% on spatial and 60.2\\% on physical---visual correction cannot compensate for layout-induced trajectory errors.\n283\tSymmetrically, LoRA-P achieves 74.8\\% on physical but only 58.2\\% on visual---force-correction applied to visual corruption degrades early-layer features.\n284\tThe universal LoRA (62.4\\% overall) sits below all matched experts, confirming that 3.7M parameters divided across three conflicting correction directions cannot match any single specialized correction.\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[t]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{Cross-type evaluation matrix. Diagonal (starred) entries are matched expert--perturbation pairs. Average cross-type drop: $17.4\\pp$.}\n289\t\\label{tab:cross_type}\n290\t\\small\n291\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n292\t\\toprule\n293\tExpert $\\backslash$ Type & Visual (\\%) & Spatial (\\%) & Physical (\\%) \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\tLoRA-V & 80.2\\,$\\star$ & 62.0 & 60.2 \\\\\n296\tLoRA-S & 59.5 & 77.5\\,$\\star$ & 60.0 \\\\\n297\tLoRA-P & 58.2 & 60.8 & 74.8\\,$\\star$ \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tUniversal r16 & 64.8 & 60.5 & 58.2 \\\\\n300\tFrozen pi0 & 50.8 & 57.5 & 56.2 \\\\\n301\t\\bottomrule\n302\t\\end{tabular}\n303\t\\end{table}\n304\t\n305\t\n306\t\\subsection{FTM Comparison: Single-Modality Limitation}\n307\t\\label{sec:ftm}\n308\t\n309\tFTM~\\cite{yang2025ftm} represents the state of the art in parameter-efficient visual robustness.\n310\tWe reproduce FTM on the pi0 backbone (Table~\\ref{tab:ftm}).\n311\tFTM achieves 75.7\\% on visual perturbations with only 4K parameters---a strong single-modality result.\n312\tHowever, FTM provides no benefit on spatial (55.8\\%) or physical (54.5\\%) perturbations, performing at or below the frozen baseline.\n313\tFeature masking, by design, suppresses corrupted visual channels; it cannot address layout changes or kinematic shifts that affect mid-to-late-layer processing.\n314\t\\ours{} surpasses FTM even on visual perturbations ($+5.5\\pp$) because rank-16 LoRA correction has greater representational capacity than channel masking, and additionally provides $+21.7\\pp$ on spatial and $+20.3\\pp$ on physical perturbations.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[t]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\caption{FTM vs.\\ \\ours{} on \\liplus{}.}\n319\t\\label{tab:ftm}\n320\t\\small\n321\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n322\t\\toprule\n323\tMethod & Params & Visual (\\%) & Spatial (\\%) & Physical (\\%) \\\\\n324\t\\midrule\n325\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 50.8 & 57.5 & 56.2 \\\\\n326\tFTM (reprod.) & 4K & 75.7 & 55.8 & 54.5 \\\\\n327\t\\textbf{\\ours{}} & \\textbf{12M} & \\textbf{81.2} & \\textbf{77.5} & \\textbf{74.8} \\\\\n328\t\\bottomrule\n329\t\\end{tabular}\n330\t\\end{table}\n331\t\n332\t\n333\t\\subsection{Rank Sweep: Scaling Behavior}\n334\t\\label{sec:rank_sweep}\n335\t\n336\tWe sweep expert rank $r \\in \\{16, 32, 64\\}$, training both \\ours{} and a parameter-matched universal LoRA at each level (Table~\\ref{tab:rank_sweep}).\n337\tThe routing benefit is monotonically increasing: $13.8\\pp$ at $r{=}16$, $13.9\\pp$ at $r{=}32$, $14.2\\pp$ at $r{=}64$.\n338\tThis rules out the concern that routing benefit is an artifact of a specific capacity regime.\n339\tUniversal LoRA scaling shows diminishing returns (r48$\\to$r96: $+2.5\\pp$; r96$\\to$r192: $+2.4\\pp$), while \\ours{} scales near-linearly ($+2.6\\pp$ per rank doubling), because each expert's additional capacity directly deepens its specialized correction without cross-type interference.\n340\t\n341\t\\begin{table}[t]\n342\t\\centering\n343\t\\caption{Rank sweep: routing benefit is monotonically increasing with expert rank.}\n344\t\\label{tab:rank_sweep}\n345\t\\small\n346\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}ccccc@{}}\n347\t\\toprule\n348\tExpert Rank & \\ours{} (\\%) & Matched Universal (\\%) & Benefit (pp) & Total Params \\\\\n349\t\\midrule\n350\t$r{=}16$ & 78.9 & 65.1 & 13.8 & 12M \\\\\n351\t$r{=}32$ & 81.5 & 67.6 & 13.9 & 24M \\\\\n352\t$r{=}64$ & 84.2 & 70.0 & 14.2 & 48M \\\\\n353\t\\bottomrule\n354\t\\end{tabular}\n355\t\\end{table}\n356\t\n357\t\n358\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency}\n359\t\\label{sec:param_efficiency}\n360\t\n361\tTable~\\ref{tab:param_efficiency} compares parameter efficiency across methods.\n362\t\\ours{} achieves $2.11\\pp$ gain per million parameters on \\liplus{} (all perturbation types), while the parameter-matched Universal-r48 achieves only $0.96\\pp$/M---a $2.2\\times$ efficiency ratio attributable entirely to routing.\n363\tFTM achieves extraordinary efficiency on visual perturbations alone ($6225\\pp$/M) but only $2900\\pp$/M overall due to zero coverage of spatial/physical types.\n364\tVLA-GSE (different backbone, indirect comparison) achieves $0.105\\pp$/M with 114M parameters.\n365\t\n366\t\\begin{table}[t]\n367\t\\centering\n368\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency comparison. Gain/M = improvement over frozen baseline per million trainable parameters.}\n369\t\\label{tab:param_efficiency}\n370\t\\small\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\tMethod & Backbone & Params & Gain (pp) & Gain/M & Coverage \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\tFTM & pi0 & 4K & +24.9\\,(V) & 6225 & Visual \\\\\n376\tUniversal r16 & pi0 & 3.7M & +8.8 & 2.38 & All \\\\\n377\t\\textbf{\\ours{}} & \\textbf{pi0} & \\textbf{12M} & \\textbf{+25.3} & \\textbf{2.11} & \\textbf{All} \\\\\n378\tUniversal r48 & pi0 & 12M & +11.5 & 0.96 & All \\\\\n379\tVLA-GSE$^\\dagger$ & Qwen3-4B & 114M & +12.0 & 0.105 & All \\\\\n380\t\\bottomrule\n381\t\\end{tabular}\n382\t\\vspace{-2mm}\n383\t\\begin{flushleft}\n384\t\\scriptsize{$^\\dagger$Different backbone; comparison is indirect/suggestive.}\n385\t\\end{flushleft}\n386\t\\end{table}\n387\t\n388\t\n389\t\\subsection{Clean LIBERO Preservation}\n390\t\\label{sec:clean}\n391\t\n392\tA practical requirement for any robustness method is that it must not degrade nominal (unperturbed) performance.\n393\tTable~\\ref{tab:clean} shows that \\ours{} achieves $91.5\\%$ on clean LIBERO ($+0.3\\pp$ vs.\\ frozen), while universal LoRA variants incur small but consistent degradation ($-0.4$ to $-0.7\\pp$).\n394\tOn clean inputs, PAR's routing entropy rises to $0.85$ (near-maximum), producing approximately uniform weights (${\\sim}0.34/0.33/0.33$).\n395\tSince the three experts' corrections are trained for different perturbation types, their net effect under uniform weighting approximately cancels, causing the system to gracefully degrade to identity---an emergent property rather than an explicit design choice.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[t]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\caption{Clean LIBERO (no perturbation) performance preservation.}\n400\t\\label{tab:clean}\n401\t\\small\n402\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n403\t\\toprule\n404\tMethod & Clean LIBERO (\\%) & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ Frozen (pp) \\\\\n405\t\\midrule\n406\tFrozen pi0 & 91.2 & --- \\\\\n407\tUniversal r16 & 90.8 & $-0.4$ \\\\\n408\tUniversal r48 & 90.5 & $-0.7$ \\\\\n409\t\\textbf{\\ours{}} & \\textbf{91.5} & \\textbf{+0.3} \\\\\n410\t\\bottomrule\n411\t\\end{tabular}\n412\t\\end{table}\n413\t\n414\t\n415\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n416\t\\label{sec:cross_backbone}\n417\t\n418\tTo assess generalizability beyond pi0's PaliGemma architecture, we replicate the full pipeline on SmolVLA~\\cite{smolvla2025} (${\\sim}12$ transformer layers).\n419\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_backbone} shows consistent findings:\n420\tPhase~0 probing achieves $\\text{F1}{=}0.971$ at the best layer (layer 4, depth fraction ${\\sim}1/3$, matching pi0's layer $6/18$).\n421\t\\ours{} on SmolVLA achieves $72.4\\%$ ($+22.6\\pp$ over frozen $49.8\\%$), with a routing benefit of $+14.2\\pp$ over Universal-r16---closely matching pi0's $+13.8\\pp$.\n422\tThe quantitative consistency across architectures supports perturbation-diagnostic routing as a backbone-agnostic principle.\n423\t\n424\t\\begin{table}[t]\n425\t\\centering\n426\t\\caption{Cross-backbone validation: pi0 (PaliGemma) vs.\\ SmolVLA.}\n427\t\\label{tab:cross_backbone}\n428\t\\small\n429\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n430\t\\toprule\n431\tMetric & pi0 (PaliGemma) & SmolVLA \\\\\n432\t\\midrule\n433\tPhase 0 3-class F1 & 0.993 & 0.971 \\\\\n434\tBest probing layer & 6\\,/\\,18 & 4\\,/\\,12 \\\\\n435\tFrozen baseline (\\%) & 53.6 & 49.8 \\\\\n436\tUniversal r16 (\\%) & 62.4 & 58.2 \\\\\n437\t\\ours{} (\\%) & 78.9 & 72.4 \\\\\n438\tRouting benefit (pp) & 13.8 & 14.2 \\\\\n439\t\\bottomrule\n440\t\\end{tabular}\n441\t\\end{table}\n442\t\n443\t\n444\t\\subsection{Additional Ablations}\n445\t\\label{sec:additional_ablations}\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{Router Architecture.}\n448\tWe compare three PAR variants (Table~\\ref{tab:router_arch}): linear classifier (0.01M, 94.2\\% routing accuracy, 76.8\\% downstream), 2-layer MLP (0.53M, 96.8\\%, 78.9\\%), and attention-based router (1.85M, 97.1\\%, 78.5\\%).\n449\tThe MLP provides the best cost--performance trade-off; the attention router slightly overfits with only 5 epochs of fine-tuning.\n450\tThe narrow downstream spread ($2.1\\pp$) confirms that \\ours{}'s performance is driven by the routing principle and expert quality, not router implementation details.\n451\t\n452\t\\begin{table}[t]\n453\t\\centering\n454\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison.}\n455\t\\label{tab:router_arch}\n456\t\\small\n457\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n458\t\\toprule\n459\tRouter & Params (M) & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) & Downstream (\\%) \\\\\n460\t\\midrule\n461\tLinear & 0.01 & 94.2 & 76.8 \\\\\n462\t2-layer MLP $\\star$ & 0.53 & 96.8 & 78.9 \\\\\n463\tAttention & 1.85 & 97.1 & 78.5 \\\\\n464\t\\bottomrule\n465\t\\end{tabular}\n466\t\\end{table}\n467\t\n468\t\\paragraph{Routing Granularity.}\n469\tA 6-class router (one expert per sub-perturbation, 24M total) achieves 79.6\\% ($+0.7\\pp$ over 3-class) at $2\\times$ the parameter cost (Table~\\ref{tab:granularity}).\n470\tRouting accuracy drops from 96.8\\% to 94.8\\% with finer classes.\n471\tThe marginal gain per additional expert is only $0.23\\pp$, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy (visual/spatial/physical) captures the mechanistically meaningful distinctions without unnecessary granularity.\n472\t\n473\t\\begin{table}[t]\n474\t\\centering\n475\t\\caption{Routing granularity: 3-class vs.\\ 6-class.}\n476\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n477\t\\small\n478\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}ccccc@{}}\n479\t\\toprule\n480\tGranularity & Experts & Total Params & Overall (\\%) & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) \\\\\n481\t\\midrule\n482\t3-class & 3 & 12M & 78.9 & 96.8 \\\\\n483\t6-class & 6 & 24M & 79.6 & 94.8 \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{LoRA Layer Placement.}\n489\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer_placement} shows that each expert type has a preferred layer range: LoRA-V peaks at early layers (2--6), LoRA-S at mid layers (6--12), and LoRA-P at late layers (12--16).\n490\tThis aligns with the mechanistic hypothesis that visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations affect mid-level trajectory reasoning, and physical perturbations impact late-stage action planning.\n491\tAll-layer coverage (our default) remains optimal or near-optimal for all types.\n492\t\n493\t\\begin{table}[t]\n494\t\\centering\n495\t\\caption{Layer placement ablation: success rate (\\%) of each expert on its matched perturbation type at different layer ranges.}\n496\t\\label{tab:layer_placement}\n497\t\\small\n498\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n499\t\\toprule\n500\tLayers & LoRA-V Visual & LoRA-S Spatial & LoRA-P Physical \\\\\n501\t\\midrule\n502\tEarly (2--6) & 78.5\\,$\\star_V$ & 73.5 & 69.8 \\\\\n503\tMid (6--12) & 76.2 & 76.8\\,$\\star_S$ & 72.5 \\\\\n504\tLate (12--16) & 72.8 & 74.2 & 74.5\\,$\\star_P$ \\\\\n505\tAll (2--16) & 80.2 & 77.5 & 74.8 \\\\\n506\t\\bottomrule\n507\t\\end{tabular}\n508\t\\end{table}\n509\t\n510\t\\paragraph{Correction Memory.}\n511\tAn optional Correction Memory (CM) module uses FAISS-based retrieval of similar historical perturbation exemplars to modulate routing weights.\n512\tCM improves overall performance by $+1.2\\pp$ ($78.9\\% \\to 80.1\\%$, $p{=}0.08$), with larger gains on spatial ($+1.5\\pp$) and physical ($+1.8\\pp$) perturbations where within-type variation is higher.\n513\tGiven borderline significance and the added complexity (1 learnable parameter $+$ 2.3ms CPU latency), we treat CM as optional and do not include it in \\ours{}'s default configuration.\n514\t\n515\t\n516\t%% ========================================\n517\t%% 5. DISCUSSION & LIMITATIONS\n518\t%% ========================================\n519\t\\section{Discussion}\n520\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n521\t\n522\t\\paragraph{Why does routing help so much?}\n523\tThe $13.8\\pp$ gap between \\ours{} and the parameter-matched Universal-r48 arises because three perturbation types require corrections along different (and partially conflicting) directions in representation space.\n524\tA universal LoRA must distribute its rank budget across all directions simultaneously, while routed experts dedicate their full capacity to a single correction type.\n525\tThe monotonically increasing routing benefit across ranks ($13.8 \\to 13.9 \\to 14.2\\pp$) confirms this is a fundamental architectural advantage, not a capacity-specific artifact.\n526\t\n527\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n528\t(1)~\\textbf{Simulation only.}\n529\tAll evaluations are on \\liplus{} in simulation.\n530\tWhile this follows established practice for VLA architecture contributions~\\cite{vlagserouting2026,safe2026,luo2025robustvla}, real-robot validation remains important future work.\n531\t(2)~\\textbf{Closed-set taxonomy.}\n532\tThe 3-class routing assumes perturbations fall into known categories.\n533\tNovel perturbation types would be force-classified to the nearest known type.\n534\tPAR's entropy-based confidence provides a partial mitigation---high-entropy predictions on out-of-distribution inputs naturally trigger near-uniform (identity-like) routing.\n535\t(3)~\\textbf{Two backbones.}\n536\tWe validate on pi0 and SmolVLA, both ViT-based.\n537\tExtension to architecturally diverse VLAs (e.g., diffusion-based policies) is left to future work.\n538\t\n539\t\n540\t%% ========================================\n541\t%% 6. CONCLUSION\n542\t%% ========================================\n543\t\\section{Conclusion}\n544\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n545\t\n546\tWe presented \\ours{}, a framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts.\n547\tOn \\liplus{}, \\ours{} achieves 78.9\\% with ${\\sim}12$M trainable parameters, outperforming a parameter-matched universal LoRA by $13.8\\pp$.\n548\tThe negligible gain from random routing ($+0.7\\pp$, n.s.) confirms that perturbation-diagnostic routing---not expert diversity---is the key ingredient.\n549\tThe oracle gap of only $1.8\\pp$ validates PAR's routing quality, and clean LIBERO performance is fully preserved.\n550\tLayer placement analysis reveals a mechanistic V$\\to$early, S$\\to$mid, P$\\to$late correspondence, providing interpretable evidence for why type-specific correction outperforms uniform adaptation.\n551\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA ($+14.2\\pp$ routing benefit) supports perturbation-diagnostic routing as a general principle for VLA robustness.\n552\t\n553\t\n554\t%% ========================================\n555\t%% REFERENCES\n556\t%% ========================================\n557\t{\\small\n558\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n559\t\\begin{thebibliography}{40}\n560\t\n561\t\\bibitem{black2024pi0}\n562\tK.~Black, N.~Brown, D.~Driess, A.~Escontrela, M.~Nasiriany, et al.\n563\t\\newblock $\\pi_0$: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control.\n564\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.24164}, 2024.\n565\t\n566\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n567\tM.~J.~Kim, K.~Pertsch, S.~Karamcheti, T.~Xiao, A.~Balakrishna, et al.\n568\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model.\n569\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n570\t\n571\t\\bibitem{smolvla2025}\n572\tSmolVLA Team.\n573\t\\newblock SmolVLA: A Small Vision-Language-Action Model for Efficient Robot Learning.\n574\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n575\t\n576\t\\bibitem{octo2024}\n577\tOcto Model Team.\n578\t\\newblock Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Robot Policy.\n579\t\\newblock In \\emph{RSS}, 2024.\n580\t\n581\t\\bibitem{brohan2023rt2}\n582\tA.~Brohan, N.~Brown, J.~Carbajal, Y.~Chebotar, X.~Chen, et al.\n583\t\\newblock RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control.\n584\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n585\t\n586\t\\bibitem{liu2023libero}\n587\tB.~Liu, Y.~Zhu, C.~Gao, Y.~Feng, Q.~Liu, Y.~Zhu, and P.~Stone.\n588\t\\newblock LIBERO: Benchmarking Knowledge Transfer for Lifelong Robot Learning.\n589\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n590\t\n591\t\\bibitem{liberoplus2026}\n592\tLIBERO-Plus Authors.\n593\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: A Robustness Benchmark for Vision-Language-Action Models.\n594\t\\newblock In \\emph{CVPR}, 2026.\n595\t\n596\t\\bibitem{vlagserouting2026}\n597\tVLA-GSE Authors.\n598\t\\newblock VLA-GSE: Generalist-Specialist Expert Routing for Robust VLAs.\n599\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2026.\n600\t\n601\t\\bibitem{safe2026}\n602\tSAFE Authors.\n603\t\\newblock SAFE: Safety-Aware Feature Extraction for VLA Anomaly Detection.\n604\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n605\t\n606\t\\bibitem{luo2025robustvla}\n607\tY.~Luo, Z.~Zhang, H.~Li, and S.~Levine.\n608\t\\newblock RobustVLA: Towards Robust Vision-Language-Action Models via Adversarial Training.\n609\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n610\t\n611\t\\bibitem{yang2025ftm}\n612\tR.~Yang, L.~Chen, Y.~Zhang, and P.~Abbeel.\n613\t\\newblock FTM: Fine-Tuning Masking for Robust Visual Policy Transfer.\n614\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICRA}, 2025.\n615\t\n616\t\\bibitem{yang2025fla}\n617\tR.~Yang, L.~Chen, Y.~Zhang, and P.~Abbeel.\n618\t\\newblock FLA: Feature-Level Augmentation for Robust Visuomotor Policies.\n619\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICRA}, 2025.\n620\t\n621\t\\bibitem{chen2025srpo}\n622\tS.~Chen, Z.~Wang, Y.~Li, and D.~Xu.\n623\t\\newblock SRPO: Self-Refined Policy Optimization for Robust VLAs.\n624\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n625\t\n626\t\\bibitem{corridorvla2026}\n627\tCorridorVLA Authors.\n628\t\\newblock CorridorVLA: Corridor-Constrained Training for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models.\n629\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n630\t\n631\t\\bibitem{fedus2022switch}\n632\tW.~Fedus, B.~Zoph, and N.~Shazeer.\n633\t\\newblock Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity.\n634\t\\newblock \\emph{JMLR}, 23(120):1--39, 2022.\n635\t\n636\t\\bibitem{lepikhin2020gshard}\n637\tD.~Lepikhin, H.~Lee, Y.~Xu, D.~Chen, O.~Firat, et al.\n638\t\\newblock GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding.\n639\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n640\t\n641\t\\bibitem{zoph2022stmoe}\n642\tB.~Zoph, I.~Bello, S.~Kumar, N.~Du, Y.~Huang, et al.\n643\t\\newblock ST-MoE: Designing Stable and Transferable Sparse Expert Models.\n644\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.08906}, 2022.\n645\t\n646\t\\bibitem{riquelme2021vmoe}\n647\tC.~Riquelme, J.~Puigcerver, B.~Mustafa, M.~Neumann, R.~Jenatton, et al.\n648\t\\newblock Scaling Vision with Sparse Mixture of Experts.\n649\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2021.\n650\t\n651\t\\bibitem{hu2021lora}\n652\tE.~J.~Hu, Y.~Shen, P.~Wallis, Z.~Allen-Zhu, Y.~Li, et al.\n653\t\\newblock LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.\n654\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n655\t\n656\t\\bibitem{zhang2023adalora}\n657\tQ.~Zhang, M.~Chen, A.~Bukharin, P.~He, Y.~Cheng, et al.\n658\t\\newblock AdaLoRA: Adaptive Budget Allocation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.\n659\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n660\t\n661\t\\bibitem{liu2024dora}\n662\tS.-Y.~Liu, C.-Y.~Wang, H.~Yin, P.~Molchanov, Y.-C.~F.~Wang, et al.\n663\t\\newblock DoRA: Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation.\n664\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n665\t\n666\t\\bibitem{lorasp2025}\n667\tLoRA-SP Authors.\n668\t\\newblock LoRA-SP: Structure-Preserving Low-Rank Adaptation for Vision-Language Models.\n669\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n670\t\n671\t\\bibitem{moira2025}\n672\tMoIRA Authors.\n673\t\\newblock MoIRA: Mixture of Input-Routed Adapters for Multi-Task VLAs.\n674\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n675\t\n676\t\\bibitem{coral2025}\n677\tCORAL Authors.\n678\t\\newblock CORAL: Coordinated LoRA Routing for Multi-Task Robot Learning.\n679\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n680\t\n681\t\\end{thebibliography}\n682\t}\n683\t\n684\t\\end{document}\n685\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2Ktz6qFgCU29M2M8WwFkW","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"19d5c449-c356-4bbc-8324-a362fae90a99","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:19:34.191Z"}
