{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017RiufK5LmuEvZmsh3kCV8p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019FjiEUkYEHW92MC5yh3qqa","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/median/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":361,"cache_read_input_tokens":49908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":361,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"1c4978e6-4de5-4f3b-b818-382a9803e760"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019FjiEUkYEHW92MC5yh3qqa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n12\t\\setlist{nosep}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts\\\\for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{\n17\t  Anonymous Authors\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%=============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve over 90\\% success on standard manipulation benchmarks but collapse under environmental perturbations---pi0 drops to 53.6\\% on LIBERO-Plus.\n27\tExisting robustness methods apply either single-modality correction (visual only) or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation, ignoring the diagnostic information already present in VLA internal representations.\n28\tWe show that frozen VLA activations at a single layer and single timestep encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class F1$=$0.996), enabling zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n29\tBuilding on this finding, we propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen activations via a lightweight \\emph{Perturbation-Aware Router} (PAR) and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts for targeted correction.\n30\tOn LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 72.0\\% overall success with ${\\sim}$12M trainable parameters, improving +18.4 percentage points (pp) over frozen pi0 and +5.6pp over a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA, with statistical significance across five seeds ($p{=}0.008$).\n31\tThe routing advantage is strongest for perturbation types requiring qualitatively different representation-level corrections: +5.4pp on Visual and +4.9pp on Spatial, but only +2.4pp on Physical perturbations where representation-level adaptation is inherently limited.\n32\tA rank sweep reveals that routing benefit scales with expert capacity (5.6pp at rank 16, 7.8pp at rank 64), and cross-backbone evaluation on OpenVLA confirms the generality of perturbation-diagnostic routing.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t%=============================================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\\label{sec:intro}\n38\t\n39\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have emerged as a dominant paradigm for robotic manipulation, achieving strong performance on standard benchmarks through large-scale pretraining on vision-language and robot trajectory data~\\cite{black2024pi0,kim2024openvla}.\n40\tHowever, deployment environments inevitably differ from training conditions---lighting changes, camera shifts, object rearrangements, and robot calibration drift all degrade performance.\n41\tOn LIBERO-Plus, a recently proposed robustness benchmark spanning six perturbation types across visual, spatial, and physical categories, pi0's success rate drops from 92.4\\% to 53.6\\%.\n42\t\n43\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into three categories.\n44\t\\emph{Visual-only methods} such as Fine-Tuned Transforms (FTM) achieve strong visual robustness with minimal parameters (${\\sim}$4K) but cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n45\t\\emph{Uniform adaptation methods} such as RobustVLA apply perturbation-agnostic training, improving robustness uniformly but inefficiently---the same correction is applied regardless of the perturbation type.\n46\t\\emph{Capacity-based routing} such as VLA-GSE routes by per-token capacity needs using Mixture-of-Experts, achieving 81.2\\% on LIBERO-Plus but requiring 114M trainable parameters on a larger backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B).\n47\tNone of these methods combine perturbation \\emph{diagnosis} with type-specific \\emph{correction}.\n48\t\n49\tWe make a key empirical observation: frozen VLA internal representations already encode perturbation-type-specific information.\n50\tA linear probe on PaliGemma layer~6 activations (first token, single timestep) achieves F1$=$0.996 for 3-class perturbation classification (Visual/Spatial/Physical) and F1$=$1.000 with first-token pooling."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"5bd68bb8-b027-4ff8-b292-92de04b797ed","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:20:11.744Z"}
