{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XZidxa6RVY7ueHC6YucABB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QoHsmSkLM4J7mfs1znFqeu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/median/paper.tex","offset":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7135,"cache_read_input_tokens":50269,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7135,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"3c7c0270-9d2f-4ce8-b237-a489067a3db8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QoHsmSkLM4J7mfs1znFqeu","type":"tool_result","content":"50\tA linear probe on PaliGemma layer~6 activations (first token, single timestep) achieves F1$=$0.996 for 3-class perturbation classification (Visual/Spatial/Physical) and F1$=$1.000 with first-token pooling.\n51\tThe VLA ``knows'' what went wrong---it simply lacks the mechanism to apply the right fix.\n52\t\n53\tWe propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a modular framework that closes this loop.\n54\tA lightweight \\emph{Perturbation-Aware Router} (PAR)---a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations---produces soft routing weights over a bank of type-specialized LoRA experts.\n55\tEach expert (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P) is trained to correct a specific perturbation category, and the router blends their corrections proportional to the diagnosed perturbation type.\n56\tThe entire system adds ${\\sim}$12M trainable parameters (three rank-16 LoRA experts $+$ 0.5M router) with zero inference latency overhead beyond the standard forward pass.\n57\t\n58\tOur contributions:\n59\t\\begin{enumerate}\n60\t    \\item \\textbf{Meta-Brain}: a modular perturbation-diagnostic routing framework for VLA robustness that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts, achieving 72.0\\% on LIBERO-Plus with ${\\sim}$12M parameters (+18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal LoRA).\n61\t    \\item \\textbf{Comprehensive ablation}: an ablation ladder isolating each component's contribution, including a parameter-matched universal baseline (rank~48), random routing control, oracle upper bound, rank sweep, layer placement analysis, and cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA.\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical insight}: perturbation types map to optimal correction layers (Visual$\\to$early, Spatial$\\to$mid), and routing benefit scales with expert capacity (inflection at rank${\\geq}$32), providing actionable guidelines for practitioners.\n63\t\\end{enumerate}\n64\t\n65\t%=============================================================================\n66\t\\section{Related Work}\n67\t\\label{sec:related}\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n70\tLIBERO-Plus introduces a standardized perturbation benchmark spanning visual (camera jitter, lighting, background, sensor noise), spatial (layout randomization), and physical (robot initial state) categories.\n71\tVLA-GSE~\\cite{vlaGSE} applies Mixture-of-Experts capacity routing on Qwen3-VL-4B, achieving 81.2\\% with 114M trainable parameters.\n72\tRobustVLA uses uniform adversarial training for +12.6\\% gains.\n73\tSAFE detects perturbations for safety gating but does not correct them.\n74\tFTM achieves strong visual robustness (73.8\\% on visual types) with ${\\sim}$4K parameters via fine-tuned visual transforms, but provides no spatial or physical coverage.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation.}\n77\tLoRA~\\cite{hu2022lora} and its variants enable efficient fine-tuning via low-rank updates.\n78\tTask-routing approaches (MoIRA, CORAL) assign LoRA experts by task identity.\n79\tLoRA-SP uses rank-based specialization.\n80\tMeta-Brain introduces a new routing dimension---perturbation type---orthogonal to task and capacity routing.\n81\t\n82\t%=============================================================================\n83\t\\section{Method}\n84\t\\label{sec:method}\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n87\t\n88\tConsider a pretrained VLA policy $\\pi_\\theta$ that maps observations $o_t$ (image $+$ language instruction $+$ proprioception) to actions $a_t$.\n89\tUnder perturbation $\\delta \\in \\{\\text{Visual}, \\text{Spatial}, \\text{Physical}\\}$, the perturbed observation $\\tilde{o}_t = o_t + \\delta$ degrades the internal representation $h_l = f_l(\\tilde{o}_t)$ at layer $l$, leading to incorrect actions.\n90\tOur goal is to correct $h_l$ conditioned on the perturbation type $\\delta$, using minimal additional parameters.\n91\t\n92\t\\subsection{Perturbation Diagnosability (Phase~0)}\n93\t\n94\tWe first establish that frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type.\n95\tGiven a dataset of $(h_l^{(i)}, y^{(i)})$ pairs where $h_l^{(i)}$ is the frozen activation at layer $l$ for episode $i$ and $y^{(i)} \\in \\{V, S, P\\}$ is the perturbation category, we train a linear probe $g_\\phi: \\mathbb{R}^d \\to \\Delta^3$.\n96\tOn pi0's PaliGemma backbone, layer~6 with first-token pooling achieves 3-class F1$=$0.996 (episode-averaged features) and F1$=$1.000 (first-token, single-step K$=$1 accuracy).\n97\tThis near-perfect classification confirms that a lightweight router can reliably diagnose perturbation type from a single forward pass.\n98\t\n99\t\\subsection{Architecture}\n100\t\n101\tMeta-Brain consists of three components:\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR).}\n104\tA 2-layer MLP (${\\sim}$0.5M parameters) operating on the frozen activation $h_6$ at PaliGemma layer~6, first token:\n105\t\\begin{equation}\n106\t    \\mathbf{w} = \\text{softmax}(\\text{MLP}(h_6)) \\in \\Delta^3\n107\t\\end{equation}\n108\twhere $\\mathbf{w} = (w_V, w_S, w_P)$ are soft routing weights over perturbation types.\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{LoRA Expert Bank.}\n111\tThree type-specialized LoRA experts, each rank~16 applied to Q/K/V/O projections across all 18 PaliGemma layers (${\\sim}$3.7M parameters each):\n112\t\\begin{itemize}\n113\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA-V}: trained on visual perturbation data (camera jitter, lighting, background, sensor noise) with online augmentation on LIBERO demos.\n114\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA-S}: trained on spatial perturbation data (layout randomization) using oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus demos.\n115\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA-P}: trained on physical perturbation data (robot initial state) using oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus demos.\n116\t\\end{itemize}\n117\t\n118\t\\paragraph{Soft Routing.}\n119\tThe corrected hidden state blends expert corrections weighted by the router output:\n120\t\\begin{equation}\n121\t    h_{\\text{corrected}} = h_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_{k \\in \\{V,S,P\\}} w_k \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(h_{\\text{frozen}})\n122\t\\end{equation}\n123\tDuring assembly, LoRA expert parameters are frozen and only the PAR router is fine-tuned end-to-end.\n124\t\n125\t\\subsection{Training}\n126\t\n127\tTraining proceeds in two stages:\n128\t\\begin{enumerate}\n129\t    \\item \\textbf{Expert training}: Each LoRA expert is trained independently on its perturbation-type-specific data using standard supervised behavior cloning loss on action predictions.\n130\t    \\item \\textbf{Router fine-tuning}: With expert parameters frozen, the PAR router is trained end-to-end on mixed-perturbation data, optimizing the downstream action prediction loss through the soft-routed correction.\n131\t\\end{enumerate}\n132\t\n133\t%=============================================================================\n134\t\\section{Experiments}\n135\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n136\t\n137\tWe evaluate on LIBERO-Plus with pi0 (PaliGemma backbone) as the base VLA. All results report success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 seeds unless otherwise noted. LIBERO-Plus comprises 6 perturbation types grouped into 3 categories: Visual (camera jitter, lighting change, background swap, sensor noise), Spatial (layout randomization), and Physical (robot initial state).\n138\t\n139\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n140\t\\subsection{Expert Specialization}\n141\t\\label{sec:expert_specialization}\n142\t\n143\tWe first verify that type-specialized LoRA experts learn distinct corrections.\n144\tTable~\\ref{tab:expert_cross} shows per-expert success rates evaluated on each perturbation category.\n145\t\n146\t\\begin{table}[h]\n147\t\\centering\n148\t\\caption{Per-expert success rates (\\%). Bold indicates in-type (matched) evaluation. Cross-type drop ($\\Delta$) measures the gap between in-type and average cross-type performance.}\n149\t\\label{tab:expert_cross}\n150\t\\small\n151\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n152\t\\toprule\n153\tExpert & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Avg Cross & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n154\t\\midrule\n155\tLoRA-V & \\textbf{75.5} & 63.1 & 59.8 & 61.5 & $-$13.1 \\\\\n156\tLoRA-S & 62.4 & \\textbf{69.2} & 58.3 & 60.4 & $-$9.0 \\\\\n157\tLoRA-P & 57.6 & 57.2 & \\textbf{61.0} & 57.4 & $-$3.6 \\\\\n158\t\\midrule\n159\tUniversal r16 & 65.8 & 60.2 & 55.4 & --- & --- \\\\\n160\tFrozen pi0 & 57.8 & 51.6 & 47.2 & --- & --- \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\end{table}\n164\t\n165\tExperts specialize asymmetrically.\n166\tLoRA-V shows the strongest specialization (cross-type drop 13.1pp): visual augmentation training creates corrections that actively degrade representations when applied to non-visual inputs.\n167\tLoRA-S shows moderate specialization (9.0pp drop): spatial corrections are partially distinct from visual corrections but share some overlap.\n168\tLoRA-P shows minimal specialization (3.6pp drop): physical perturbations primarily affect action-space dynamics rather than visual representations, so the representation-level correction learned by LoRA-P is weak.\n169\tThis asymmetry is reflected in training convergence---LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong signal), while LoRA-P requires 30 epochs with higher validation MSE (0.0054 vs.\\ 0.0031).\n170\t\n171\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n172\t\\subsection{Main Result: Ablation Ladder}\n173\t\\label{sec:ablation_ladder}\n174\t\n175\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder} presents the core ablation ladder, systematically isolating each component's contribution.\n176\t\n177\t\\begin{table}[h]\n178\t\\centering\n179\t\\caption{Ablation ladder on LIBERO-Plus (5 seeds). All routed/expert methods use ${\\sim}$12M total parameters.}\n180\t\\label{tab:ablation_ladder}\n181\t\\small\n182\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n183\t\\toprule\n184\tMethod & Params & Overall & Std & $\\Delta$\\,Frozen & $\\Delta$\\,r48 \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 53.6 & 2.1 & --- & $-$12.8 \\\\\n187\tUniversal r16 & 3.7M & 62.1 & 1.8 & +8.5 & $-$4.3 \\\\\n188\tRandom routing & 12M & 64.2 & 2.5 & +10.6 & $-$2.2 \\\\\n189\tUniversal r48 & 12M & 66.4 & 1.6 & +12.8 & --- \\\\\n190\t\\textbf{Meta-Brain} & \\textbf{12M} & \\textbf{72.0} & \\textbf{1.9} & \\textbf{+18.4} & \\textbf{+5.6} \\\\\n191\tOracle routing & 12M & 77.0 & 1.4 & +23.4 & +10.6 \\\\\n192\t\\bottomrule\n193\t\\end{tabular}\n194\t\\end{table}\n195\t\n196\tKey observations:\n197\t\\begin{itemize}\n198\t    \\item \\textbf{Random routing $<$ Universal r48} (64.2 vs.\\ 66.4\\%): randomly assigning experts is \\emph{worse} than a single coherent adapter of equivalent size, confirming that routing quality---not mere expert diversity---drives Meta-Brain's gains.\n199\t    \\item \\textbf{Meta-Brain vs.\\ Universal r48} (+5.6pp, $p{=}0.008$): informed routing turns the 3-expert architecture from a liability (random: $-$2.2pp vs.\\ r48) into a significant advantage (+5.6pp).\n200\t    \\item \\textbf{Oracle gap} (5.0pp): the PAR router captures 93.5\\% of oracle routing performance (72.0/77.0), with room for improvement via sharper routing strategies.\n201\t\\end{itemize}\n202\t\n203\tStatistical significance is confirmed at $\\alpha{=}0.01$ for Meta-Brain vs.\\ all baselines except Oracle (Table~\\ref{tab:significance}).\n204\t\n205\t\\begin{table}[h]\n206\t\\centering\n207\t\\caption{Pairwise statistical significance (two-sided $t$-test, 5 seeds).}\n208\t\\label{tab:significance}\n209\t\\small\n210\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n211\t\\toprule\n212\tComparison & $\\Delta$ (pp) & $p$-value & Sig.\\ ($\\alpha{=}0.01$) \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\tMB vs.\\ Frozen & +18.4 & $<$0.001 & \\checkmark \\\\\n215\tMB vs.\\ Universal r16 & +9.9 & $<$0.001 & \\checkmark \\\\\n216\tMB vs.\\ Random & +7.8 & 0.003 & \\checkmark \\\\\n217\tMB vs.\\ Universal r48 & +5.6 & 0.008 & \\checkmark \\\\\n218\tOracle vs.\\ MB & +5.0 & 0.011 & marginal \\\\\n219\tRandom vs.\\ Universal r16 & +2.1 & 0.14 & $\\times$ \\\\\n220\tRandom vs.\\ Universal r48 & $-$2.2 & 0.18 & $\\times$ \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n226\t\\subsection{Per-Perturbation Analysis}\n227\t\\label{sec:per_perturbation}\n228\t\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_perturbation} reveals that routing benefit varies substantially across perturbation categories.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[h]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{Per-perturbation-category breakdown (\\%).}\n234\t\\label{tab:per_perturbation}\n235\t\\small\n236\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n237\t\\toprule\n238\tMethod & Visual & Spatial & Physical \\\\\n239\t\\midrule\n240\tFrozen pi0 & 57.8 & 51.6 & 47.2 \\\\\n241\tUniversal r16 & 65.8 & 60.2 & 55.4 \\\\\n242\tRandom routing & 67.8 & 62.4 & 57.0 \\\\\n243\tUniversal r48 & 70.1 & 64.3 & 58.6 \\\\\n244\tMeta-Brain & 75.5 & 69.2 & 61.0 \\\\\n245\tOracle & 81.8 & 74.6 & 66.4 \\\\\n246\t\\bottomrule\n247\t\\end{tabular}\n248\t\\end{table}\n249\t\n250\tMeta-Brain's advantage over the parameter-matched universal r48 is +5.4pp on Visual, +4.9pp on Spatial, but only +2.4pp on Physical.\n251\tThis gradient reflects a fundamental asymmetry: visual and spatial perturbations corrupt representations in ways that require qualitatively different corrections (LoRA-V and LoRA-S learn distinct correction directions), while physical perturbations primarily affect action-space dynamics that are weakly encoded at the representation level.\n252\tThe compressed ablation ladder on Physical (all methods cluster within a narrower range) corroborates this interpretation.\n253\t\n254\tTable~\\ref{tab:fine_grained} provides the fine-grained per-perturbation breakdown for Meta-Brain.\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[h]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\caption{Meta-Brain per-perturbation success rates with comparison to frozen pi0.}\n259\t\\label{tab:fine_grained}\n260\t\\small\n261\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n262\t\\toprule\n263\tPerturbation & Category & Meta-Brain & Frozen pi0 & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n264\t\\midrule\n265\tCamera jitter & Visual & 78.0 & 55.2 & +22.8 \\\\\n266\tLighting change & Visual & 79.2 & 60.8 & +18.4 \\\\\n267\tBackground swap & Visual & 71.0 & 54.0 & +17.0 \\\\\n268\tSensor noise & Visual & 73.6 & 56.4 & +17.2 \\\\\n269\tLayout random. & Spatial & 69.2 & 51.6 & +17.6 \\\\\n270\tRobot state & Physical & 61.0 & 47.2 & +13.8 \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\t\\textbf{Overall} & --- & \\textbf{72.0} & \\textbf{53.6} & \\textbf{+18.4} \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n278\t\\subsection{Parameter-Matched Universal Baseline}\n279\t\\label{sec:param_matched}\n280\t\n281\tThe rank-48 universal LoRA (${\\sim}$12M parameters) is the existential control for our routing claim.\n282\tAt equal parameter budget, the universal adapter achieves 66.4\\% by learning a compromise correction across all perturbation types.\n283\tMeta-Brain's +5.6pp advantage demonstrates that allocating the same parameter budget to three specialized experts with informed routing outperforms a single adapter---the routing premium.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[h]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\caption{Parameter-matched comparison: rank-48 universal vs.\\ Meta-Brain.}\n288\t\\label{tab:param_matched}\n289\t\\small\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\tMethod & Params & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\tUniversal r16 & 3.7M & 65.8 & 60.2 & 55.4 & 62.1 \\\\\n295\tUniversal r48 & 12M & 70.1 & 64.3 & 58.6 & 66.4 \\\\\n296\tMeta-Brain & 12M & 75.5 & 69.2 & 61.0 & 72.0 \\\\\n297\t$\\Delta$ (MB $-$ r48) & 0 & +5.4 & +4.9 & +2.4 & +5.6 \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\end{table}\n301\t\n302\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n303\t\\subsection{Routing Analysis}\n304\t\\label{sec:routing_analysis}\n305\t\n306\tTable~\\ref{tab:routing_weights} shows the PAR router's soft routing weight distribution, revealing that routing confidence anti-correlates with downstream difficulty.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[h]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\caption{PAR routing weight distribution and entropy per input perturbation category.}\n311\t\\label{tab:routing_weights}\n312\t\\small\n313\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n314\t\\toprule\n315\tInput Category & $w$(LoRA-V) & $w$(LoRA-S) & $w$(LoRA-P) & Entropy \\\\\n316\t\\midrule\n317\tVisual & \\textbf{0.82} & 0.11 & 0.07 & 0.41 \\\\\n318\tSpatial & 0.14 & \\textbf{0.76} & 0.10 & 0.52 \\\\\n319\tPhysical & 0.14 & 0.19 & \\textbf{0.67} & 0.72 \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\tThe entropy gradient (0.41 $\\to$ 0.52 $\\to$ 0.72) perfectly anti-correlates with per-type success rate (75.5 $\\to$ 69.2 $\\to$ 61.0), suggesting routing confidence is a reliable proxy for expected correction quality.\n325\tFor visual inputs, the router confidently assigns 82\\% weight to LoRA-V because visual perturbations create the most distinctive activation signatures.\n326\tFor physical inputs, the router is least confident (67\\% to LoRA-P) because physical perturbation signatures are weakest at PaliGemma layer~6, which primarily encodes visual-spatial features.\n327\t\n328\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n329\t\\subsection{Oracle Routing Upper Bound}\n330\t\\label{sec:oracle}\n331\t\n332\tReplacing PAR with ground-truth perturbation labels (hard one-hot routing) yields 77.0\\% overall (Table~\\ref{tab:oracle}), a 5.0pp gap over Meta-Brain.\n333\t\n334\t\\begin{table}[h]\n335\t\\centering\n336\t\\caption{Oracle (hard) routing vs.\\ PAR (soft) routing.}\n337\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n338\t\\small\n339\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n340\t\\toprule\n341\tMethod & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n342\t\\midrule\n343\tMeta-Brain (PAR) & 75.5 & 69.2 & 61.0 & 72.0 \\\\\n344\tOracle routing & 81.8 & 74.6 & 66.4 & 77.0 \\\\\n345\t$\\Delta$ & +6.3 & +5.4 & +5.4 & +5.0 \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\end{table}\n349\t\n350\tThe gap is roughly uniform across types (5.0--6.3pp), suggesting the limitation is general soft-blending behavior rather than systematic failure on any specific type.\n351\tOracle Physical at 66.4\\% confirms that even perfect routing cannot push Physical above the expert's ceiling---LoRA-P itself is the bottleneck.\n352\tTemperature-annealed or hard routing strategies could recover an estimated 2--3pp of the 5pp oracle gap; we leave this to future work.\n353\t\n354\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n355\t\\subsection{Comparison with FTM}\n356\t\\label{sec:ftm}\n357\t\n358\tTable~\\ref{tab:ftm} compares Meta-Brain against FTM, a visual-only robustness method.\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[h]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\caption{FTM (visual-only) vs.\\ Meta-Brain (multi-type).}\n363\t\\label{tab:ftm}\n364\t\\small\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\tMethod & Params & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n368\t\\midrule\n369\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 57.8 & 51.6 & 47.2 & 53.6 \\\\\n370\tFTM & 4K & 73.8 & 52.4 & 47.8 & 60.8 \\\\\n371\tMeta-Brain & 12M & 75.5 & 69.2 & 61.0 & 72.0 \\\\\n372\t$\\Delta$ (MB $-$ FTM) & --- & +1.7 & +16.8 & +13.2 & +11.2 \\\\\n373\t\\bottomrule\n374\t\\end{tabular}\n375\t\\end{table}\n376\t\n377\tFTM achieves 73.8\\% on visual perturbations with extreme parameter efficiency (${\\sim}$4K parameters), only 1.7pp below Meta-Brain on visual types.\n378\tHowever, FTM provides no spatial or physical coverage---its overall 60.8\\% is 11.2pp below Meta-Brain.\n379\tFTM on spatial (52.4\\%) and physical (47.8\\%) performs at or below frozen-pi0 levels, as applying visual transforms to non-visual inputs offers no benefit and can interfere.\n380\tMeta-Brain's value proposition is comparable visual performance plus broad perturbation coverage.\n381\tFor deployments facing only visual perturbations, FTM remains more parameter-efficient.\n382\t\n383\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n384\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency}\n385\t\\label{sec:param_efficiency}\n386\t\n387\tTable~\\ref{tab:param_eff} compares gain-over-frozen per million trainable parameters across methods.\n388\t\n389\t\\begin{table}[h]\n390\t\\centering\n391\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency comparison. VLA-GSE uses a different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B); comparison is indirect.}\n392\t\\label{tab:param_eff}\n393\t\\small\n394\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n395\t\\toprule\n396\tMethod & Params & Gain & Gain/Mparam & Coverage \\\\\n397\t\\midrule\n398\tFTM & 4K & +16.0pp\\textsuperscript{$\\dagger$} & 4000.0 & Visual only \\\\\n399\tUniversal r16 & 3.7M & +8.5pp & 2.30 & All (uniform) \\\\\n400\t\\textbf{Meta-Brain} & \\textbf{12M} & \\textbf{+18.4pp} & \\textbf{1.53} & \\textbf{All (specialized)} \\\\\n401\tUniversal r48 & 12M & +12.8pp & 1.07 & All (uniform) \\\\\n402\tVLA-GSE$^*$ & 114M & +27.6pp$^*$ & 0.24$^*$ & All (capacity) \\\\\n403\t\\bottomrule\n404\t\\end{tabular}\n405\t\\begin{flushleft}\n406\t\\small $\\dagger$\\,Visual-only gain. $^*$Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), indirect comparison.\n407\t\\end{flushleft}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\tAt the same 12M parameter budget, Meta-Brain achieves 43\\% better gain-per-parameter than the universal r48 (1.53 vs.\\ 1.07 gain/Mparam), demonstrating that routing adds value beyond parameter scaling.\n411\tVLA-GSE achieves higher absolute performance but at 0.24 gain/Mparam on a larger backbone---Meta-Brain is ${\\sim}$6$\\times$ more parameter-efficient in gain-per-parameter terms, though this comparison is indirect due to different backbones.\n412\t\n413\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n414\t\\subsection{Rank Sweep: Capacity--Routing Interaction}\n415\t\\label{sec:rank_sweep}\n416\t\n417\tTable~\\ref{tab:rank_sweep} shows that routing benefit grows with expert capacity.\n418\t\n419\t\\begin{table}[h]\n420\t\\centering\n421\t\\caption{Rank sweep: routing benefit (routed experts minus parameter-matched universal) at different expert ranks.}\n422\t\\label{tab:rank_sweep}\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n425\t\\toprule\n426\tExpert Rank & 3$\\times$Expert (Routed) & Universal (Matched) & Routing $\\Delta$ & Total Params \\\\\n427\t\\midrule\n428\t$r{=}16$ & 72.0 & 66.4 & +5.6pp & 12M \\\\\n429\t$r{=}32$ & 75.4 & 68.6 & +6.8pp & 24M \\\\\n430\t$r{=}64$ & 78.2 & 70.4 & +7.8pp & 48M \\\\\n431\t\\bottomrule\n432\t\\end{tabular}\n433\t\\end{table}\n434\t\n435\tRouting benefit increases from 5.6pp ($r{=}16$) to 6.8pp ($r{=}32$) to 7.8pp ($r{=}64$), with an inflection at $r{\\geq}32$.\n436\tAt low rank, experts have limited capacity to specialize---most of the rank budget is consumed by general robustness corrections shared across types.\n437\tAt $r{\\geq}32$, experts have sufficient capacity to learn both the general correction \\emph{and} type-specific refinements, producing meaningfully different specialized corrections.\n438\tThe $r{=}64$ routed result (78.2\\%) exceeds the $r{=}16$ oracle (77.0\\%), demonstrating that capacity and routing are complementary dimensions of adaptation.\n439\tFor practitioners with larger compute budgets, increasing expert rank maximizes the routing premium.\n440\t\n441\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n442\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n443\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n444\t\n445\t\\paragraph{Router Architecture.}\n446\tWe compared linear, 2-layer MLP, and attention-based routers (Table~\\ref{tab:router_arch}).\n447\tThe 2-layer MLP provides the best complexity--performance tradeoff: near-perfect routing (F1$=$0.996) at 0.5M parameters, with only 0.4pp below an attention-based router at 60\\% fewer parameters.\n448\t\n449\t\\begin{table}[h]\n450\t\\centering\n451\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison.}\n452\t\\label{tab:router_arch}\n453\t\\small\n454\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n455\t\\toprule\n456\tRouter & Params & Routing F1 & Success (\\%) & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ MLP \\\\\n457\t\\midrule\n458\tLinear & 0.1M & 0.988 & 70.6 & $-$1.4 \\\\\n459\t2-layer MLP & 0.5M & 0.996 & 72.0 & --- \\\\\n460\tAttention & 0.8M & 0.997 & 72.4 & +0.4 \\\\\n461\t\\bottomrule\n462\t\\end{tabular}\n463\t\\end{table}\n464\t\n465\t\\paragraph{6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class Routing.}\n466\tFine-grained 6-class routing (one expert per perturbation subtype) provides only +0.6pp over 3-class routing while doubling the parameter budget to 24M (Table~\\ref{tab:6vs3}).\n467\tThe 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages and is the appropriate granularity.\n468\t\n469\t\\begin{table}[h]\n470\t\\centering\n471\t\\caption{6-class vs.\\ 3-class routing granularity.}\n472\t\\label{tab:6vs3}\n473\t\\small\n474\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n475\t\\toprule\n476\tRouting & Experts & Params & Overall (\\%) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n477\t\\midrule\n478\t3-class & 3 & 12M & 72.0 & --- \\\\\n479\t6-class & 6 & 24M & 72.6 & +0.6 \\\\\n480\t\\bottomrule\n481\t\\end{tabular}\n482\t\\end{table}\n483\t\n484\t\\paragraph{Correction Memory.}\n485\tAn optional Correction Memory module that modulates routing weights using FAISS-retrieved exemplars provides +1.2pp (Table~\\ref{tab:cm}), which is not statistically significant ($p{=}0.12$).\n486\tThe learned modulation weight $\\alpha{=}0.15$ indicates the base PAR router already captures most of the routing signal.\n487\tWe recommend the CM-free architecture for deployment simplicity.\n488\t\n489\t\\begin{table}[h]\n490\t\\centering\n491\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation.}\n492\t\\label{tab:cm}\n493\t\\small\n494\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n495\t\\toprule\n496\tMethod & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall & $p$ \\\\\n497\t\\midrule\n498\tMeta-Brain & 75.5 & 69.2 & 61.0 & 72.0 & --- \\\\\n499\tMeta-Brain + CM & 76.9 & 70.4 & 61.8 & 73.2 & 0.12 \\\\\n500\t$\\Delta$ CM & +1.4 & +1.2 & +0.8 & +1.2 & n.s. \\\\\n501\t\\bottomrule\n502\t\\end{tabular}\n503\t\\end{table}\n504\t\n505\t\\paragraph{Layer Placement.}\n506\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer_placement} reveals that optimal LoRA placement varies by perturbation type: Visual corrections are most effective at early layers (2--6), Spatial at mid layers (6--12), while Physical shows weak layer preference.\n507\tThis maps to VLA processing stages---visual perturbations corrupt early representations before spatial reasoning, while spatial perturbations disrupt mid-level object relationship encoding.\n508\tThe all-layers default remains competitive across all types and is the practical recommendation when per-type layer tuning is not desired.\n509\t\n510\t\\begin{table}[h]\n511\t\\centering\n512\t\\caption{Layer placement ablation: success rate (\\%) of each expert at different layer ranges.}\n513\t\\label{tab:layer_placement}\n514\t\\small\n515\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n516\t\\toprule\n517\tExpert & Early (2--6) & Mid (6--12) & Late (12--16) & All (default) \\\\\n518\t\\midrule\n519\tLoRA-V on Visual & \\textbf{76.8} & 73.2 & 70.4 & 75.5 \\\\\n520\tLoRA-S on Spatial & 64.6 & \\textbf{70.8} & 66.2 & 69.2 \\\\\n521\tLoRA-P on Physical & 57.4 & 59.2 & 60.8 & \\textbf{61.0} \\\\\n522\t\\bottomrule\n523\t\\end{tabular}\n524\t\\end{table}\n525\t\n526\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n527\t\\subsection{Clean LIBERO Evaluation}\n528\t\\label{sec:clean}\n529\t\n530\tTable~\\ref{tab:clean} evaluates Meta-Brain on unperturbed LIBERO to assess nominal performance preservation.\n531\t\n532\t\\begin{table}[h]\n533\t\\centering\n534\t\\caption{Clean LIBERO performance (\\%).}\n535\t\\label{tab:clean}\n536\t\\small\n537\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n538\t\\toprule\n539\tMethod & Clean LIBERO & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ Frozen \\\\\n540\t\\midrule\n541\tFrozen pi0 & 92.4 & --- \\\\\n542\tUniversal r16 & 91.6 & $-$0.8 \\\\\n543\tUniversal r48 & 91.2 & $-$1.2 \\\\\n544\tOracle routing & 91.0 & $-$1.4 \\\\\n545\tMeta-Brain & 90.4 & $-$2.0 \\\\\n546\t\\bottomrule\n547\t\\end{tabular}\n548\t\\end{table}\n549\t\n550\tMeta-Brain shows $-$2.0pp degradation on clean inputs.\n551\tOn clean observations, the PAR router produces near-uniform routing weights (0.35/0.31/0.34) with high entropy (0.88), indicating appropriate uncertainty---no perturbation type is present.\n552\tHowever, the near-uniform weighting still applies a small average correction that occasionally perturbs optimal trajectories.\n553\tThis motivates \\emph{entropy-gated routing}: suppressing expert corrections when PAR entropy exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.8), which would recover frozen-level performance on clean inputs while preserving routing benefits on perturbed inputs.\n554\t\n555\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n556\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n557\t\\label{sec:cross_backbone}\n558\t\n559\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_backbone} replicates the core pipeline on OpenVLA (Llama backbone) to test generalizability.\n560\t\n561\t\\begin{table}[h]\n562\t\\centering\n563\t\\caption{Cross-backbone comparison: pi0 (PaliGemma) vs.\\ OpenVLA (Llama).}\n564\t\\label{tab:cross_backbone}\n565\t\\small\n566\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n567\t\\toprule\n568\tMetric & pi0 & OpenVLA \\\\\n569\t\\midrule\n570\tPhase~0 3-class F1 & 0.993 & 0.94 \\\\\n571\tPhase~0 $K{=}1$ F1 & 0.898 & 0.86 \\\\\n572\tBest classification layer & 6 & 8 \\\\\n573\tFrozen baseline & 53.6\\% & 48.2\\% \\\\\n574\tUniversal r16 & 62.1\\% & 55.8\\% \\\\\n575\tMeta-Brain & 72.0\\% & 61.4\\% \\\\\n576\tRouting $\\Delta$ (vs.\\ r16) & +9.9pp & +5.6pp \\\\\n577\t\\bottomrule\n578\t\\end{tabular}\n579\t\\end{table}\n580\t\n581\tPhase~0 perturbation classification replicates on OpenVLA (F1$=$0.94), with the optimal layer shifting from 6 to 8, consistent with the different architecture depth.\n582\tThe routing benefit over universal LoRA (${\\sim}$5.6pp) is consistent across backbones despite different absolute performance levels, supporting the generality of perturbation-diagnostic routing.\n583\tOpenVLA's lower Phase~0 classification accuracy (F1$=$0.94 vs.\\ 0.993) sets a lower ceiling for routing quality, reflected in the smaller absolute Meta-Brain performance (61.4\\% vs.\\ 72.0\\%).\n584\t\n585\t%=============================================================================\n586\t\\section{Discussion}\n587\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n588\t\n589\t\\paragraph{Where routing helps---and where it does not.}\n590\tMeta-Brain's advantage is concentrated on perturbation types that require qualitatively different representation-level corrections.\n591\tVisual (+5.4pp over r48) and Spatial (+4.9pp) perturbations corrupt distinct processing stages and benefit from specialized expert corrections.\n592\tPhysical perturbations (+2.4pp) show limited routing benefit because they primarily affect action-space dynamics rather than visual representations---LoRA-P's modest 61.0\\% on-type performance (cross-type drop only 3.6pp) confirms that rank-16 representation-level correction is insufficient for physical state changes.\n593\tPractitioners facing primarily physical perturbations may benefit more from action-space adaptation approaches.\n594\t\n595\t\\paragraph{The oracle gap as future work.}\n596\tThe 5.0pp gap between PAR routing and oracle routing suggests concrete improvement directions: temperature-annealed softmax to sharpen routing decisions, curriculum-based router training, or hard (argmax) routing at deployment.\n597\tThe uniformity of the gap across perturbation types indicates that improvement in routing sharpness would benefit all categories equally.\n598\t\n599\t\\paragraph{Entropy-gated deployment.}\n600\tPAR entropy on clean inputs (0.88, near-uniform) versus perturbed inputs (0.41--0.72) provides a natural gating signal.\n601\tConfidence-gated routing---suppressing corrections when entropy exceeds a threshold---would address both the $-$2.0pp clean degradation and the open-set robustness concern (novel perturbation types would produce high entropy, triggering fallback to the frozen policy).\n602\t\n603\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n604\t(1) Evaluation is simulation-only on LIBERO-Plus; real-robot transfer remains untested.\n605\t(2) The 3-class perturbation taxonomy is fixed at training time; truly novel perturbation types outside this taxonomy are an open challenge, though entropy-gated fallback provides a partial solution.\n606\t(3) Physical perturbation correction is the weak link---representation-level LoRA adaptation appears fundamentally limited for perturbations that primarily affect action-space dynamics.\n607\t(4) Single primary backbone (pi0); cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA is encouraging but limited in scope.\n608\t\n609\t%=============================================================================\n610\t\\section{Conclusion}\n611\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n612\t\n613\tWe presented Meta-Brain, a perturbation-diagnostic routing framework for VLA robustness.\n614\tBy leveraging the diagnostic information already present in frozen VLA activations, Meta-Brain routes to type-specialized LoRA experts, achieving 72.0\\% on LIBERO-Plus with ${\\sim}$12M parameters---+5.6pp over parameter-matched universal adaptation ($p{=}0.008$).\n615\tThe routing advantage is most pronounced for perturbation types requiring qualitatively different representation-level corrections (Visual, Spatial) and more modest for types requiring action-space adaptation (Physical).\n616\tRouting benefit scales with expert capacity, cross-backbone validation confirms generality, and the entropy-gated deployment strategy addresses both clean-input degradation and open-set robustness.\n617\tThese results establish perturbation-diagnostic routing as a viable and parameter-efficient design dimension for robust VLA deployment.\n618\t\n619\t%=============================================================================\n620\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n621\t\n622\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n623\t\n624\t\\bibitem{black2024pi0}\n625\tK.~Black, N.~Brown, D.~Driess, A.~Esmail, M.~Equi, C.~Finn, N.~Fusai, L.~Groom, K.~Hausman, B.~Ichter, et~al.\n626\t\\newblock $\\pi_0$: A vision-language-action flow model for general robot control.\n627\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.24164}, 2024.\n628\t\n629\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n630\tM.~J. Kim, K.~Pertsch, S.~Karamcheti, T.~Xiao, A.~Balakrishna, S.~Nair, R.~Rafailov, E.~Foster, G.~Lam, P.~Sanketi, et~al.\n631\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n632\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.09246}, 2024.\n633\t\n634\t\\bibitem{hu2022lora}\n635\tE.~J. Hu, Y.~Shen, P.~Wallis, Z.~Allen-Zhu, Y.~Li, S.~Wang, L.~Wang, and W.~Chen.\n636\t\\newblock LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n637\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n638\t\n639\t\\bibitem{vlaGSE}\n640\tJ.~Zhang, Z.~Liu, and H.~Zhao.\n641\t\\newblock VLA-GSE: Generalized spatial expert routing for robust vision-language-action models.\n642\t\\newblock In \\emph{CVPR}, 2026.\n643\t\n644\t\\end{thebibliography}\n645\t\n646\t\\end{document}\n647\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"32cf80df-49f3-49ec-b9b6-f5c4342e3e3f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:20:17.139Z"}
