{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2Ktz6qFgCU29M2M8WwFkW","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize my review as Reviewer R1 focusing on Methods and Experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Meta-Brain — Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nMeta-Brain proposes a two-stage framework for VLA robustness: (1) a lightweight MLP router (PAR) that classifies perturbation type from frozen PaliGemma layer-6 activations, and (2) three type-specialized rank-16 LoRA experts soft-routed by the diagnosed type. On LIBERO-Plus, the system achieves 78.9% with ~12M params, outperforming a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA by 13.8pp (p<0.001). The random routing null result (+0.7pp, p=0.38) cleanly isolates routing as the source of gain rather than ensemble effects.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Exemplary ablation ladder with the right controls.** The six-point progression (Frozen 53.6 -> r16 62.4 -> r48 65.1 -> Random 65.8 -> Meta-Brain 78.9 -> Oracle 80.7) is strictly monotonic, and each step isolates exactly one factor. The rank-48 parameter-matched baseline is the existential control for the paper's core claim, and it is present. The random routing null result (p=0.38) is the single most important experiment in the paper -- it cleanly falsifies the ensemble alternative hypothesis. Many CoRL papers lack either of these controls; this paper includes both.\n\n**S2. Statistical rigor above venue average.** Five-seed evaluation with paired t-tests, exact p-values (including the non-significant random routing result and the borderline Correction Memory p=0.08), and standard deviations reported throughout. The honest treatment of non-significant and borderline results is commendable and sets a good standard.\n\n**S3. Cross-type evaluation matrix demonstrates non-interchangeable corrections.** The 17.4pp average cross-type performance drop (Table 4) is the strongest single piece of evidence that expert specialization is necessary, not merely helpful. The asymmetry is informative: LoRA-V drops 18.2pp on spatial but LoRA-P drops only 16.6pp on visual, suggesting physical corrections are slightly more transferable -- a detail consistent with the broader representational story.\n\n**S4. Comprehensive experiment coverage.** Twelve experiments spanning ablation ladder, statistical significance, per-perturbation breakdown, cross-type matrix, FTM comparison, rank sweep, parameter efficiency, clean LIBERO preservation, cross-backbone validation, router architecture, routing granularity, layer placement, and Correction Memory ablation. This is unusually thorough for a single paper and each experiment serves a clear purpose.\n\n**S5. Cross-backbone consistency is convincing.** Routing benefit of +14.2pp on SmolVLA vs. +13.8pp on pi0 is remarkably consistent. The Phase 0 probing F1=0.971 at layer 4/12 (matching pi0's depth fraction of 6/18 ~ 1/3) suggests the phenomenon generalizes beyond PaliGemma.\n\n**S6. Clean LIBERO preservation is practically important.** Meta-Brain achieves +0.3pp on unperturbed tasks while universal LoRA variants degrade (-0.4 to -0.7pp). The emergent high-entropy fallback on clean inputs (routing weights ~ 0.34/0.33/0.33 producing near-identity correction) is an appealing property for deployment.\n\n**S7. Layer placement analysis provides mechanistic grounding.** The V->early, S->mid, P->late correspondence (Table 12) aligns with the computational stages of a vision-language-action pipeline and provides interpretable evidence for why type-specific correction outperforms uniform adaptation.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) All results are imagined -- no real experiments have been run.** The project's own ROADMAP.md (dated 2026-05-15) states the project is in \"Phase 1 LoRA Training\" with only Phase 0 (activation collection/classification) completed. The report.md shows only one experiment (\"phase0_activation_collection\") as completed. The paper.tex, review_context.yaml, and all \"experimental results\" come from a dream simulation pipeline that generates imagined results according to best/median/worst outcome tiers. Every number in Tables 1-12 is a fabrication designed to make the narrative work. The paper cannot be evaluated as presenting real experimental evidence because none exists beyond the Phase 0 linear probe. This is the single most critical issue: the review is of a *hypothetical* paper, not a real one.\n\n**W2. (Major) Closed-set 3-class taxonomy with no principled derivation.** The Visual/Spatial/Physical taxonomy is inherited directly from LIBERO-Plus's perturbation categories rather than derived from representational analysis (e.g., unsupervised clustering on activations, representational similarity analysis). No compound perturbation evaluation is presented -- real deployment involves co-occurring perturbations (e.g., lighting change + object displacement). The near-one-hot routing weights (0.86-0.93) suggest the system cannot effectively blend corrections for compound scenarios. No open-set evaluation tests what happens with genuinely novel perturbation types that require correction but don't match any known category. For a robotics venue where deployment conditions are unpredictable, this is a serious practical limitation.\n\n**W3. (Major) Simulation-only evaluation on a single benchmark.** All claims rest on LIBERO-Plus where perturbations are discrete, non-overlapping, and synthetically applied. The near-perfect Phase 0 classification (F1=1.0) may reflect the categorical distinctness of synthetic perturbations rather than a deep representational property. The gap between this evaluation setting and the claimed contribution (\"a general design principle for VLA robustness\") is substantial.\n\n**W4. (Moderate) Training data asymmetry confound.** LoRA-V trains on 4 visual subtypes (camera, lighting, background, noise); LoRA-S and LoRA-P each train on 1 subtype. The V>S>P performance gradient (81.2 > 77.5 > 74.8) may partly reflect data diversity rather than intrinsic perturbation difficulty or correction quality. This confound is not discussed, controlled, or acknowledged. A data-volume-matched ablation (e.g., training LoRA-V on only 1 visual subtype) would isolate this factor.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) No latency/throughput analysis.** Soft routing requires computing all 3 LoRA expert outputs at every timestep (3x LoRA compute vs. a single universal LoRA). For real-time robot control at 10+ Hz, this overhead matters. The paper claims \"negligible latency\" but provides no wall-clock measurements. The claim should be quantified.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) Episode counts not disclosed.** The number of evaluation episodes per perturbation type per seed is never stated. This makes it impossible to assess statistical power or success rate resolution. If evaluation uses 50 episodes per condition, the resolution is 2pp, which would mean many of the reported differences fall within measurement granularity.\n\n**W7. (Moderate) Rank sweep trend claim is statistically unsupported.** Three data points spanning a 0.4pp total range (13.8, 13.9, 14.2) are described as \"monotonically increasing.\" No trend significance test is provided. This near-constant offset is equally consistent with no trend at all. The claim that routing benefit increases with rank is not supported by the data presented.\n\n**W8. (Moderate) \"Overall\" metric biased toward visual perturbations.** 4 of 6 subtypes are visual, so the headline 78.9% is upward-biased by visual gains (which are the largest). A category-balanced average (V:81.2, S:77.5, P:74.8 -> balanced 77.8%) should be reported alongside the subtype-averaged metric. The difference is small here but the bias should be transparent.\n\n**W9. (Minor) Phase 0 finding framed as contribution rather than prerequisite.** Different perturbation types produce categorically different observation changes; high linear separability in intermediate representations is expected rather than surprising, especially given that SAFE already demonstrated VLA activations encode execution-relevant state. This should be presented as a prerequisite observation that motivates the architecture, not as a standalone contribution.\n\n**W10. (Minor) \"Orthogonal design dimension\" claim unsupported.** The paper claims perturbation-diagnostic routing is \"orthogonal and complementary\" to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE) but provides no composability experiment (e.g., Meta-Brain + VLA-GSE). This claim should be removed or supported with evidence.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Episode counts and resolution:** How many rollout episodes per perturbation type per seed? What is the success rate measurement granularity?\n\n2. **Compound perturbations:** What is Meta-Brain's behavior when visual and spatial perturbations co-occur simultaneously? Does the near-one-hot routing prevent effective correction blending?\n\n3. **Open-set robustness:** Has the high-entropy fallback been tested on genuinely novel perturbation types (not in the taxonomy) that *do* require correction? What is the router output on such inputs?\n\n4. **Training data asymmetry:** LoRA-V sees 4x the perturbation diversity of LoRA-S or LoRA-P. Have you controlled for data volume per expert? What happens if LoRA-V is trained on only 1 visual subtype?\n\n5. **Latency overhead:** What is the wall-clock latency of Meta-Brain (3 LoRA forward passes + router) vs. a single universal LoRA forward pass? Is this compatible with real-time control at 10+ Hz?\n\n6. **MoE-LoRA differentiation:** The routing formula h_corrected = h_frozen + sum_k p_k * DeltaW_k * h_frozen is architecturally identical to LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA. How does Meta-Brain differ beyond the choice of routing signal? Why is this a methodological contribution rather than an application variation?\n\n7. **Taxonomy derivation:** Have you performed unsupervised clustering on layer-6 activations to verify that the 3-class structure emerges naturally, rather than being imposed by LIBERO-Plus's design?\n\n8. **Rank sweep significance:** Are the differences in routing benefit across ranks (13.8, 13.9, 14.2pp) statistically significant, or are they within noise?\n\n9. **SAFE extension baseline:** Could SAFE's anomaly detection features directly gate LoRA experts, producing a functionally equivalent architecture without a separate PAR module? What is the incremental contribution over \"SAFE + LoRA bank\"?\n\n10. **Clean LIBERO mechanism:** You attribute the +0.3pp clean improvement to expert corrections canceling under uniform routing. Have you verified this by examining the L2 norm of the net correction vector on clean inputs vs. perturbed inputs?\n\n### 5. Scoring\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | **6/10** | Method is technically correct. Two-phase training is well-motivated. The soft routing formulation is sound. However, the closed-set taxonomy lacks principled derivation, the training data asymmetry confound is uncontrolled, and the \"orthogonal design dimension\" claim is unsupported. All experimental results appear to be imagined rather than from actual runs, which fundamentally undermines soundness assessment. |\n| **Empirical evaluation** | **5/10** | The *design* of the evaluation is excellent: ablation ladder with parameter-matched control, random routing null result, cross-backbone validation, 5-seed evaluation with appropriate statistics. However, all results are from a dream simulation pipeline rather than actual experiments. Assuming the results are real, the score would be 7-8/10, but the single-benchmark limitation, missing episode counts, missing latency analysis, no compound perturbation testing, and training data asymmetry confound are genuine gaps. |\n| **Clarity of method** | **7/10** | The method is clearly described with explicit equations. The two-phase training protocol (expert training then router fine-tuning) is well-specified. The Phase 0 motivation is clearly presented. Minor issues: the E2E training objective for router fine-tuning could be more explicit about gradient flow through soft routing weights, and the Correction Memory description is cursory. |\n| **Overall recommendation** | **6/10** | Marginally above acceptance threshold. The experimental *design* is exemplary -- if the results hold up in actual experiments, this would be a solid empirical contribution. The random routing null result and parameter-matched baseline are exactly the right controls. The cross-backbone consistency is convincing. However, limited methodological novelty (standard MoE-LoRA architecture with a different routing signal), single simulated benchmark, closed-set taxonomy without principled derivation, missing latency analysis, and the training data asymmetry confound prevent a confident accept. The paper's fate hinges on whether actual experimental results match the imagined ones, particularly the 13.8pp gap over rank-48 universal LoRA. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I have carefully reviewed the method, all experimental tables, the project's internal documentation (ROADMAP, decisions, memory, paper framing), and both prior dream review reports. My main uncertainty is whether the imagined results will hold in actual experiments. |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4265dcf694fbe04c (use SendMessage with to: 'a4265dcf694fbe04c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78390\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 125078</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"aef05eb0-6177-4205-bf0b-00ae66442a7d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:21:12.251Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginal above threshold, 5=marginal below, 4=below threshold, 3=clear reject)\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires: relevance to robot learning, learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots\n- Acceptance rate ~25-35%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea:** Frozen VLA (pi0) activations at a single layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996). Meta-Brain uses a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) — a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations — to produce soft routing weights over 3 type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus: 72.0% overall with ~12M params, +18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (p=0.008).\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- Phase 0: Linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 activations → F1=0.996 for 3-class classification\n- PAR Router: 2-layer MLP (~0.5M params) on frozen h6, produces soft weights w=(wV, wS, wP)\n- LoRA Expert Bank: 3 rank-16 LoRA experts (Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers, ~3.7M each)\n- Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h_frozen)\n- Training: (1) experts trained independently on type-specific data, (2) router fine-tuned end-to-end with frozen experts\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- Table 1: Expert specialization — LoRA-V cross-drop 13.1pp, LoRA-S 9.0pp, LoRA-P only 3.6pp\n- Table 2: Ablation ladder — Frozen 53.6 → r16 62.1 → Random 64.2 → r48 66.4 → Meta-Brain 72.0 → Oracle 77.0\n- Table 3: Statistical significance — MB vs r48 p=0.008, MB vs Random p=0.003\n- Table 4: Per-category — MB vs r48: Visual +5.4, Spatial +4.9, Physical +2.4\n- Table 7: Routing weights — Visual entropy 0.41, Spatial 0.52, Physical 0.72 (anti-correlates with success)\n- Table 8: Oracle gap 5.0pp uniform across types\n- Table 9: FTM comparison — FTM 73.8% visual-only, MB 75.5% visual + covers spatial/physical\n- Table 10: Parameter efficiency — MB 1.53 gain/Mparam vs r48 1.07, but FTM 4000 gain/Mparam (visual only)\n- Table 11: Rank sweep — routing benefit 5.6pp (r16) → 6.8pp (r32) → 7.8pp (r64)\n- Table 12: Router architecture — linear 70.6, MLP 72.0, attention 72.4\n- Table 13: 6-class vs 3-class — only +0.6pp at 2x params\n- Table 14: Correction Memory — +1.2pp, not significant (p=0.12)\n- Table 15: Layer placement — Visual best early (76.8), Spatial mid (70.8), Physical weak preference\n- Table 16: Clean LIBERO — Meta-Brain -2.0pp degradation\n- Table 17: Cross-backbone (OpenVLA) — routing benefit ~5.6pp, F1=0.94\n\n**Experiment design reference (review_context.yaml) lists these imagined experiments:**\nAll experiments listed above are present in the paper. Key coverage notes:\n- All 12 planned experiments appear reflected in the paper's tables\n- The CM ablation honestly shows non-significant results\n- VLA-GSE comparison is indirect (different backbone) — acknowledged\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on METHODS and EXPERIMENTS. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on method design and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on method limitations and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Scoring** — provide individual scores for:\n   - Soundness (1-10)\n   - Empirical evaluation (1-10)\n   - Clarity of method (1-10)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. A CoRL paper should demonstrate relevance to robot learning with strong experimental evaluation. Consider: Is the method technically novel? Are baselines fair? Are claims supported? Is the evaluation sufficient for a robotics venue (simulation-only is a limitation)? Are there missing experiments or controls?","agentId":"a4265dcf694fbe04c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize my review as Reviewer R1 focusing on Methods and Experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Meta-Brain — Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nMeta-Brain proposes a two-stage framework for VLA robustness: (1) a lightweight MLP router (PAR) that classifies perturbation type from frozen PaliGemma layer-6 activations, and (2) three type-specialized rank-16 LoRA experts soft-routed by the diagnosed type. On LIBERO-Plus, the system achieves 78.9% with ~12M params, outperforming a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA by 13.8pp (p<0.001). The random routing null result (+0.7pp, p=0.38) cleanly isolates routing as the source of gain rather than ensemble effects.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Exemplary ablation ladder with the right controls.** The six-point progression (Frozen 53.6 -> r16 62.4 -> r48 65.1 -> Random 65.8 -> Meta-Brain 78.9 -> Oracle 80.7) is strictly monotonic, and each step isolates exactly one factor. The rank-48 parameter-matched baseline is the existential control for the paper's core claim, and it is present. The random routing null result (p=0.38) is the single most important experiment in the paper -- it cleanly falsifies the ensemble alternative hypothesis. Many CoRL papers lack either of these controls; this paper includes both.\n\n**S2. Statistical rigor above venue average.** Five-seed evaluation with paired t-tests, exact p-values (including the non-significant random routing result and the borderline Correction Memory p=0.08), and standard deviations reported throughout. The honest treatment of non-significant and borderline results is commendable and sets a good standard.\n\n**S3. Cross-type evaluation matrix demonstrates non-interchangeable corrections.** The 17.4pp average cross-type performance drop (Table 4) is the strongest single piece of evidence that expert specialization is necessary, not merely helpful. The asymmetry is informative: LoRA-V drops 18.2pp on spatial but LoRA-P drops only 16.6pp on visual, suggesting physical corrections are slightly more transferable -- a detail consistent with the broader representational story.\n\n**S4. Comprehensive experiment coverage.** Twelve experiments spanning ablation ladder, statistical significance, per-perturbation breakdown, cross-type matrix, FTM comparison, rank sweep, parameter efficiency, clean LIBERO preservation, cross-backbone validation, router architecture, routing granularity, layer placement, and Correction Memory ablation. This is unusually thorough for a single paper and each experiment serves a clear purpose.\n\n**S5. Cross-backbone consistency is convincing.** Routing benefit of +14.2pp on SmolVLA vs. +13.8pp on pi0 is remarkably consistent. The Phase 0 probing F1=0.971 at layer 4/12 (matching pi0's depth fraction of 6/18 ~ 1/3) suggests the phenomenon generalizes beyond PaliGemma.\n\n**S6. Clean LIBERO preservation is practically important.** Meta-Brain achieves +0.3pp on unperturbed tasks while universal LoRA variants degrade (-0.4 to -0.7pp). The emergent high-entropy fallback on clean inputs (routing weights ~ 0.34/0.33/0.33 producing near-identity correction) is an appealing property for deployment.\n\n**S7. Layer placement analysis provides mechanistic grounding.** The V->early, S->mid, P->late correspondence (Table 12) aligns with the computational stages of a vision-language-action pipeline and provides interpretable evidence for why type-specific correction outperforms uniform adaptation.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) All results are imagined -- no real experiments have been run.** The project's own ROADMAP.md (dated 2026-05-15) states the project is in \"Phase 1 LoRA Training\" with only Phase 0 (activation collection/classification) completed. The report.md shows only one experiment (\"phase0_activation_collection\") as completed. The paper.tex, review_context.yaml, and all \"experimental results\" come from a dream simulation pipeline that generates imagined results according to best/median/worst outcome tiers. Every number in Tables 1-12 is a fabrication designed to make the narrative work. The paper cannot be evaluated as presenting real experimental evidence because none exists beyond the Phase 0 linear probe. This is the single most critical issue: the review is of a *hypothetical* paper, not a real one.\n\n**W2. (Major) Closed-set 3-class taxonomy with no principled derivation.** The Visual/Spatial/Physical taxonomy is inherited directly from LIBERO-Plus's perturbation categories rather than derived from representational analysis (e.g., unsupervised clustering on activations, representational similarity analysis). No compound perturbation evaluation is presented -- real deployment involves co-occurring perturbations (e.g., lighting change + object displacement). The near-one-hot routing weights (0.86-0.93) suggest the system cannot effectively blend corrections for compound scenarios. No open-set evaluation tests what happens with genuinely novel perturbation types that require correction but don't match any known category. For a robotics venue where deployment conditions are unpredictable, this is a serious practical limitation.\n\n**W3. (Major) Simulation-only evaluation on a single benchmark.** All claims rest on LIBERO-Plus where perturbations are discrete, non-overlapping, and synthetically applied. The near-perfect Phase 0 classification (F1=1.0) may reflect the categorical distinctness of synthetic perturbations rather than a deep representational property. The gap between this evaluation setting and the claimed contribution (\"a general design principle for VLA robustness\") is substantial.\n\n**W4. (Moderate) Training data asymmetry confound.** LoRA-V trains on 4 visual subtypes (camera, lighting, background, noise); LoRA-S and LoRA-P each train on 1 subtype. The V>S>P performance gradient (81.2 > 77.5 > 74.8) may partly reflect data diversity rather than intrinsic perturbation difficulty or correction quality. This confound is not discussed, controlled, or acknowledged. A data-volume-matched ablation (e.g., training LoRA-V on only 1 visual subtype) would isolate this factor.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) No latency/throughput analysis.** Soft routing requires computing all 3 LoRA expert outputs at every timestep (3x LoRA compute vs. a single universal LoRA). For real-time robot control at 10+ Hz, this overhead matters. The paper claims \"negligible latency\" but provides no wall-clock measurements. The claim should be quantified.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) Episode counts not disclosed.** The number of evaluation episodes per perturbation type per seed is never stated. This makes it impossible to assess statistical power or success rate resolution. If evaluation uses 50 episodes per condition, the resolution is 2pp, which would mean many of the reported differences fall within measurement granularity.\n\n**W7. (Moderate) Rank sweep trend claim is statistically unsupported.** Three data points spanning a 0.4pp total range (13.8, 13.9, 14.2) are described as \"monotonically increasing.\" No trend significance test is provided. This near-constant offset is equally consistent with no trend at all. The claim that routing benefit increases with rank is not supported by the data presented.\n\n**W8. (Moderate) \"Overall\" metric biased toward visual perturbations.** 4 of 6 subtypes are visual, so the headline 78.9% is upward-biased by visual gains (which are the largest). A category-balanced average (V:81.2, S:77.5, P:74.8 -> balanced 77.8%) should be reported alongside the subtype-averaged metric. The difference is small here but the bias should be transparent.\n\n**W9. (Minor) Phase 0 finding framed as contribution rather than prerequisite.** Different perturbation types produce categorically different observation changes; high linear separability in intermediate representations is expected rather than surprising, especially given that SAFE already demonstrated VLA activations encode execution-relevant state. This should be presented as a prerequisite observation that motivates the architecture, not as a standalone contribution.\n\n**W10. (Minor) \"Orthogonal design dimension\" claim unsupported.** The paper claims perturbation-diagnostic routing is \"orthogonal and complementary\" to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE) but provides no composability experiment (e.g., Meta-Brain + VLA-GSE). This claim should be removed or supported with evidence.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Episode counts and resolution:** How many rollout episodes per perturbation type per seed? What is the success rate measurement granularity?\n\n2. **Compound perturbations:** What is Meta-Brain's behavior when visual and spatial perturbations co-occur simultaneously? Does the near-one-hot routing prevent effective correction blending?\n\n3. **Open-set robustness:** Has the high-entropy fallback been tested on genuinely novel perturbation types (not in the taxonomy) that *do* require correction? What is the router output on such inputs?\n\n4. **Training data asymmetry:** LoRA-V sees 4x the perturbation diversity of LoRA-S or LoRA-P. Have you controlled for data volume per expert? What happens if LoRA-V is trained on only 1 visual subtype?\n\n5. **Latency overhead:** What is the wall-clock latency of Meta-Brain (3 LoRA forward passes + router) vs. a single universal LoRA forward pass? Is this compatible with real-time control at 10+ Hz?\n\n6. **MoE-LoRA differentiation:** The routing formula h_corrected = h_frozen + sum_k p_k * DeltaW_k * h_frozen is architecturally identical to LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA. How does Meta-Brain differ beyond the choice of routing signal? Why is this a methodological contribution rather than an application variation?\n\n7. **Taxonomy derivation:** Have you performed unsupervised clustering on layer-6 activations to verify that the 3-class structure emerges naturally, rather than being imposed by LIBERO-Plus's design?\n\n8. **Rank sweep significance:** Are the differences in routing benefit across ranks (13.8, 13.9, 14.2pp) statistically significant, or are they within noise?\n\n9. **SAFE extension baseline:** Could SAFE's anomaly detection features directly gate LoRA experts, producing a functionally equivalent architecture without a separate PAR module? What is the incremental contribution over \"SAFE + LoRA bank\"?\n\n10. **Clean LIBERO mechanism:** You attribute the +0.3pp clean improvement to expert corrections canceling under uniform routing. Have you verified this by examining the L2 norm of the net correction vector on clean inputs vs. perturbed inputs?\n\n### 5. Scoring\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | **6/10** | Method is technically correct. Two-phase training is well-motivated. The soft routing formulation is sound. However, the closed-set taxonomy lacks principled derivation, the training data asymmetry confound is uncontrolled, and the \"orthogonal design dimension\" claim is unsupported. All experimental results appear to be imagined rather than from actual runs, which fundamentally undermines soundness assessment. |\n| **Empirical evaluation** | **5/10** | The *design* of the evaluation is excellent: ablation ladder with parameter-matched control, random routing null result, cross-backbone validation, 5-seed evaluation with appropriate statistics. However, all results are from a dream simulation pipeline rather than actual experiments. Assuming the results are real, the score would be 7-8/10, but the single-benchmark limitation, missing episode counts, missing latency analysis, no compound perturbation testing, and training data asymmetry confound are genuine gaps. |\n| **Clarity of method** | **7/10** | The method is clearly described with explicit equations. The two-phase training protocol (expert training then router fine-tuning) is well-specified. The Phase 0 motivation is clearly presented. Minor issues: the E2E training objective for router fine-tuning could be more explicit about gradient flow through soft routing weights, and the Correction Memory description is cursory. |\n| **Overall recommendation** | **6/10** | Marginally above acceptance threshold. The experimental *design* is exemplary -- if the results hold up in actual experiments, this would be a solid empirical contribution. The random routing null result and parameter-matched baseline are exactly the right controls. The cross-backbone consistency is convincing. However, limited methodological novelty (standard MoE-LoRA architecture with a different routing signal), single simulated benchmark, closed-set taxonomy without principled derivation, missing latency analysis, and the training data asymmetry confound prevent a confident accept. The paper's fate hinges on whether actual experimental results match the imagined ones, particularly the 13.8pp gap over rank-48 universal LoRA. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I have carefully reviewed the method, all experimental tables, the project's internal documentation (ROADMAP, decisions, memory, paper framing), and both prior dream review reports. My main uncertainty is whether the imagined results will hold in actual experiments. |"}],"totalDurationMs":125078,"totalTokens":78390,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_read_input_tokens":74469,"output_tokens":3214,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":704},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3214,"cache_read_input_tokens":74469,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":704,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":14}}}
