{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7MtK2ysxK9CkCHdAttKmg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review as Reviewer R2 (Novelty/Originality and Related Work/Positioning).\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer R2 Review: Meta-Brain (CoRL 2026)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nMeta-Brain proposes routing frozen VLA activations through a lightweight MLP classifier (PAR) to select among three perturbation-type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), achieving 72.0% on LIBERO-Plus with ~12M parameters (+5.6pp over a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA, p=0.008). The paper's core empirical observation is that frozen PaliGemma layer-6 activations encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996), and this signal can drive meaningful expert routing. The ablation ladder is thorough, the random-routing null result is well-designed, and cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA provides some generality evidence.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. The random routing null result is the paper's strongest single contribution.** Random routing (64.2%) performs *worse* than the parameter-matched universal r48 (66.4%), definitively ruling out the ensemble hypothesis. This is the critical experiment that many MoE papers omit, and it cleanly establishes that routing quality -- not expert diversity -- drives Meta-Brain's gains. This control alone elevates the experimental design above most submissions in this space.\n\n**S2. The routing-benefit-by-type analysis provides genuine insight.** The gradient of routing benefit (Visual +5.4pp > Spatial +4.9pp > Physical +2.4pp) with the honest acknowledgment that Physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation level is scientifically informative. The anti-correlation between routing entropy and downstream success (0.41/0.52/0.72 entropy vs. 75.5/69.2/61.0 success) is a clean result that helps the reader understand when and why routing helps.\n\n**S3. The parameter-matched comparison is the right experiment.** The rank-48 universal LoRA at ~12M params is the existential control, and the +5.6pp gap (p=0.008) is statistically significant. This directly addresses the concern that routing benefit could be confounded with parameter count -- a weakness that sank the first dream review.\n\n**S4. Cross-backbone validation adds credibility to the routing principle.** Phase 0 classification replicates on OpenVLA (F1=0.94) with the optimal layer shifting predictably (layer 8 vs. layer 6), and the routing benefit (+5.6pp over universal) is consistent. While limited to two backbones, this is more cross-architecture evidence than most comparable papers provide.\n\n**S5. The layer placement ablation provides mechanistic grounding.** The V-to-early, S-to-mid pattern aligns with intuitive VLA processing stages and adds interpretive depth beyond a pure systems contribution. This is a useful observation for practitioners, even if it lacks causal attribution.\n\n**S6. Honest treatment of limitations.** The paper acknowledges the -2.0pp clean LIBERO degradation, the weak Physical routing benefit, the 5.0pp oracle gap, and the closed-set taxonomy limitation. The CM module is correctly relegated to an optional ablation after borderline significance (p=0.12). This calibrated presentation builds trust.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) The architecture is a standard composition of known components with no new mechanism.** The PAR router is a 2-layer MLP with softmax output -- standard MoE gating. The LoRA expert bank with weighted summation (`h_corrected = h_frozen + Sigma_k w_k * LoRA_k(h_frozen)`) is architecturally identical to LoRAMoE (Li et al., 2024), MoLoRA (Zadouri et al., 2024), MixLoRA (Li et al., 2024), and MOELoRA (Liu et al., 2023). The only distinguishing element is that routing is conditioned on *perturbation type* rather than task identity or input tokens. This is an application-level design choice -- choosing which signal to feed into an existing architectural template -- not a methodological advance. The paper needs to explicitly engage with this and argue why \"perturbation type as routing dimension\" constitutes more than a straightforward instantiation of MoE-LoRA applied to a new axis.\n\n**W2. (Major) Severely incomplete engagement with the MoE-LoRA and adapter composition literature.** The median paper cites only 4 references. Even the best-scenario paper cites only 24. Critical missing works include:\n\n- *Mixture-of-LoRA methods*: LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA, LoRA-Switch -- all implement the same weighted-sum-of-LoRA-experts formula with learned gating\n- *Adapter composition*: AdapterFusion (Pfeiffer et al., 2021), Poly (Ponti et al., 2023), AdaMix (Wang et al., 2022) -- explore learned adapter combination in NLP\n- *Test-time adaptation*: TENT (Wang et al., 2021), TTT (Sun et al., 2020), MEMO (Zhang et al., 2022) -- handle distribution shift without pre-defined taxonomies\n- *Domain-adaptive methods*: Conditional Batch Normalization (de Vries et al., 2017), domain-specific adapters (Rebuffi et al., 2017, 2018), FiLM (Perez et al., 2018) -- condition network behavior on domain/environment signals\n- *Probing foundations*: Alain & Bengio (2017), Belinkov (2022 survey)\n- *MoE fundamentals*: Shazeer et al. (2017) -- the original sparsely-gated MoE\n\nWithout engaging this literature, the reader cannot assess what Meta-Brain adds beyond \"LoRAMoE but with perturbation-type features as the gating signal.\" The bibliography gap suggests insufficient literature engagement, which is a serious concern for novelty evaluation.\n\n**W3. (Major) Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) is confirmatory, not surprising.** Different perturbation types produce categorically different observation changes -- camera jitter alters pixel statistics differently from object layout randomization, which is different from robot pose changes. It would be *more* surprising if a frozen vision-language model's intermediate activations failed to distinguish these. SAFE already demonstrated that VLA activations encode execution-relevant anomaly state. Multiple probing studies on vision transformers show that intermediate layers encode domain/distribution information. Framing this as a standalone contribution (Contribution 1 in the paper) overstates its novelty. It should be presented as a prerequisite observation -- necessary for the method to work, but not itself a discovery.\n\n**W4. (Major) The closed-set 3-class taxonomy is a fundamental limitation insufficiently addressed.** The taxonomy is inherited directly from LIBERO-Plus's perturbation categories, not derived from representational analysis (e.g., unsupervised clustering of layer-6 activations to verify the 3-class structure emerges naturally). No evaluation is provided for:\n\n- *Compound perturbations*: Real deployment involves co-occurring perturbations (e.g., lighting change + object displacement). The near-one-hot routing weights (0.67-0.82) suggest the system cannot effectively blend corrections for compound scenarios.\n- *Open-set perturbations*: Novel perturbation types not in the taxonomy. The entropy-gated fallback is proposed but never evaluated -- does high entropy actually emerge on OOD perturbations that require correction?\n- *Continuous perturbation severity*: The same perturbation type at different severity levels may require different correction magnitudes, but the routing weights are trained on binary present/absent perturbation labels.\n\nFor a robotics venue where deployment conditions are inherently unpredictable, this closed-world assumption is a significant practical limitation.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) The comparison with VLA-GSE is misleading despite the caveat.** The parameter efficiency table includes VLA-GSE (114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B) alongside Meta-Brain (12M on pi0), computing a gain-per-parameter ratio across different backbones. The \"indirect comparison\" footnote does not prevent readers from taking the 6x efficiency ratio at face value. More fundamentally, VLA-GSE's capacity routing and Meta-Brain's perturbation-type routing address different aspects of the problem. The paper claims these are \"orthogonal\" but provides no composability experiment (e.g., Meta-Brain routing + VLA-GSE capacity scaling). Without such evidence, the orthogonality claim is unsupported.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) Missing engagement with test-time adaptation (TTA) literature.** TTA methods (TENT, TTT, MEMO, DUA) address distribution-shift adaptation without pre-defined taxonomies -- they adapt to whatever distribution shift is present at test time. The paper does not discuss why explicit categorical routing is preferable to taxonomy-free adaptation. This is a natural comparison for any robotics reviewer to ask about, since TTA avoids the open-set problem entirely. The absence of this discussion is a notable gap.\n\n**W7. (Moderate) Missing engagement with domain-adaptive conditioning methods.** Conditional Batch Normalization, FiLM layers, domain-specific residual adapters, and modular networks all condition network computation on a detected domain/environment signal -- conceptually very similar to Meta-Brain's approach of conditioning LoRA expert selection on a detected perturbation type. The connection to this well-established literature in computer vision should be discussed.\n\n**W8. (Moderate) The \"perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension\" framing overclaims.** The paper positions perturbation-type routing as orthogonal to capacity routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). But the architectural mechanism is identical to task-based routing -- only the *label* driving the routing differs (perturbation type vs. task identity). Calling this a new \"design dimension\" when the architectural template is unchanged overstates the conceptual contribution. A more honest framing would be: \"We identify perturbation type as a more informative routing signal than task identity for VLA robustness.\"\n\n**W9. (Minor) The \"overall\" metric is biased toward visual perturbations.** 4 of 6 perturbation subtypes are visual. The headline 72.0% gives disproportionate weight to visual performance. A category-balanced average (Visual 75.5, Spatial 69.2, Physical 61.0 -> balanced 68.6%) should be reported alongside the subtype-uniform average.\n\n**W10. (Minor) Training data asymmetry is uncontrolled.** LoRA-V trains on 4 visual subtypes; LoRA-S and LoRA-P each train on 1 subtype. The V > S > P performance gradient may partly reflect training data diversity rather than intrinsic perturbation difficulty or routing quality. This confound is not discussed.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **MoE-LoRA differentiation**: How does Meta-Brain differ architecturally from LoRAMoE or MoLoRA beyond the choice of routing signal? If the only difference is conditioning the gating MLP on frozen intermediate activations rather than input tokens, please justify why this constitutes a methodological contribution rather than a different instantiation of the same template.\n\n2. **SAFE extension**: Could SAFE's perturbation detection features be used directly to gate LoRA experts, producing a functionally equivalent system? What is the incremental contribution of Meta-Brain over \"SAFE detection + LoRA bank\"?\n\n3. **TTA comparison**: Why is explicit categorical routing preferable to test-time adaptation methods (TENT, TTT) that handle distribution shifts without pre-defined taxonomies? Have you considered a TTA baseline?\n\n4. **Compound perturbations**: What is Meta-Brain's behavior when two perturbation types co-occur (e.g., camera jitter + object layout change)? Does the near-one-hot routing prevent effective blending of corrections?\n\n5. **Open-set evaluation**: Has the entropy-gated fallback been tested on genuinely novel perturbation types (not just clean inputs)? What are the router outputs on, e.g., weather effects or sensor degradation modes not in the 3-class taxonomy?\n\n6. **Taxonomy derivation**: Have you performed unsupervised clustering (k-means, spectral) on layer-6 activations to verify that the 3-class structure emerges naturally rather than being imposed by the benchmark's perturbation categories?\n\n7. **Domain adaptation connection**: How does Meta-Brain relate to conditional batch normalization, FiLM, or domain-specific adapters -- all of which condition network behavior on a detected domain signal?\n\n8. **Training data volumes**: How many demonstrations per perturbation type? How is the LoRA-V (4 subtypes) vs. LoRA-S/LoRA-P (1 subtype each) data volume imbalance handled or controlled for?\n\n9. **Latency**: What is the wall-clock latency overhead of computing all 3 LoRA expert outputs at every timestep versus a single universal LoRA? For real-time robot control at 10+ Hz, this is a practical concern.\n\n10. **Rank sweep statistics**: Are the differences in routing benefit across ranks (5.6, 6.8, 7.8pp) individually significant, or could the \"monotonically increasing\" trend be within noise given 3 data points?\n\n### 5. Scoring\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Originality** | **4/10** | The architecture is a standard MoE-LoRA template (weighted sum of LoRA experts with learned gating) applied to a new routing signal (perturbation type). No new mechanism, loss function, training procedure, or architectural innovation. The Phase 0 finding is confirmatory. The contribution is at the application/design-choice level, not the methodological level. |\n| **Related work coverage** | **3/10** | Critical omission of the entire MoE-LoRA literature (LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA), adapter composition methods (AdapterFusion, Poly, AdaMix), test-time adaptation (TENT, TTT), domain-adaptive conditioning (CBN, FiLM), and probing foundations (Alain & Bengio). The median paper has 4 references; even the best scenario has only 24. CoRL papers typically cite 30-50. The missing references are not peripheral -- they implement the same architectural formula the paper uses. |\n| **Significance of contribution** | **6/10** | The empirical finding that perturbation-diagnostic routing outperforms parameter-matched uniform adaptation by +5.6pp (p=0.008) is a genuine and useful result for the VLA robustness community. The random routing null result and cross-backbone validation add real value. However, significance is limited by: (a) single simulated benchmark with synthetic perturbations, (b) closed-set taxonomy with no compound/open-set evaluation, (c) the contribution being an application insight rather than a generalizable method. |\n| **Overall recommendation** | **5/10** | Marginally below acceptance threshold. The experimental methodology is solid -- the ablation ladder, random routing control, parameter-matched baseline, and cross-backbone validation are all well-designed. However, the limited methodological novelty (standard MoE-LoRA template with a different gating signal), severely incomplete related work, and the gap between the closed-set evaluation and the \"general design principle\" framing keep this below the CoRL bar. The paper reads as a well-executed application study rather than a contribution that advances the community's understanding of VLA robustness mechanisms or adaptation architectures. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I am familiar with the MoE, LoRA, adapter composition, and domain adaptation literatures. The missing related work assessment is based on direct knowledge of these methods. My main uncertainty is whether CoRL 2026 would weight empirical rigor high enough to compensate for the novelty gap -- venue calibration could shift this by +/- 1 point. |\n\n### Summary Assessment\n\nThe paper's empirical execution is above average: the ablation ladder is exemplary, the random routing null result is the single most important control, and the cross-backbone validation supports generality. However, the core architecture is a standard composition of known components (MLP gating + weighted LoRA experts), and the only distinguishing element -- using perturbation type as the routing signal -- is an application-level design choice within an existing template. The severely incomplete bibliography (missing the entire MoE-LoRA literature that implements the same formula) prevents the reader from assessing incremental novelty. The closed-set taxonomy without compound or open-set evaluation limits practical significance for a robotics venue. If the authors (1) thoroughly engage with MoE-LoRA methods and explicitly argue differentiation, (2) add compound/open-set perturbation experiments, (3) discuss TTA and domain-adaptive conditioning as alternatives, and (4) reframe the contribution as an empirical systems study rather than a new \"design dimension,\" this could reach a clear accept at a future venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8bed8bbd0bc630ea (use SendMessage with to: 'a8bed8bbd0bc630ea' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 73916\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 150914</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8217637-8c12-424b-9256-3d9dc7b16457","uuid":"3f731590-c775-475a-b745-2f720fde320a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:22:03.343Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginal above threshold, 5=marginal below, 4=below threshold, 3=clear reject)\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires: relevance to robot learning, learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots\n- Acceptance rate ~25-35%\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea:** Frozen VLA (pi0) activations at a single layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996). Meta-Brain uses a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) — a 2-layer MLP on frozen layer-6 activations — to produce soft routing weights over 3 type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus: 72.0% overall with ~12M params, +18.4pp over frozen pi0, +5.6pp over parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (p=0.008).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Meta-Brain framework: perturbation-diagnostic routing for VLA robustness (72.0% on LIBERO-Plus, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal)\n2. Comprehensive ablation: ablation ladder, parameter-matched baselines, oracle, rank sweep, layer placement, cross-backbone\n3. Practical insight: perturbation types map to correction layers (V→early, S→mid), routing benefit scales with capacity (inflection r≥32)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- VLA Robustness: LIBERO-Plus benchmark, VLA-GSE (MoE capacity routing, 81.2% with 114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B), RobustVLA (uniform adversarial training), SAFE (detection without correction), FTM (visual-only, 4K params)\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL (task-routing), LoRA-SP (rank-based). Claims Meta-Brain introduces new routing dimension (perturbation type)\n\n**Method:**\n- Phase 0: Linear probe shows frozen PaliGemma layer 6 activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.996)\n- PAR Router: 2-layer MLP on frozen activations → soft routing weights\n- 3 LoRA experts (rank 16, Q/K/V/O, all 18 layers) for Visual/Spatial/Physical\n- Soft blending: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h_frozen)\n- Two-stage training: (1) independent expert training, (2) router fine-tuning with frozen experts\n\n**Key results:**\n- 72.0% overall on LIBERO-Plus (+5.6pp over parameter-matched r48, p=0.008)\n- Routing benefit strongest on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp), weakest on Physical (+2.4pp)\n- Random routing < Universal r48 (proves routing quality matters)\n- Oracle gap 5.0pp (uniform across types)\n- Cross-backbone on OpenVLA: routing benefit ~5.6pp consistent\n- Clean LIBERO degradation: -2.0pp\n- FTM comparison: FTM 73.8% visual only, Meta-Brain comparable visual + multi-type coverage\n- VLA-GSE 81.2% with 114M params (different backbone, indirect comparison)\n\n**Key prior work to evaluate against:**\n- **MoE/Routing in general:** Mixture-of-Experts literature (Shazeer et al., Switch Transformer, etc.) — is routing by perturbation type genuinely novel vs. standard MoE?\n- **VLA-GSE:** Routes by per-token capacity, achieves higher absolute performance — how does Meta-Brain's perturbation-diagnostic routing differ architecturally? Is the difference fundamental or superficial?\n- **MoIRA, CORAL:** Task-routing LoRA — how is perturbation routing different from task routing conceptually?\n- **SAFE:** Detects perturbations for safety gating — how does Meta-Brain's Phase 0 differ from SAFE's detection?\n- **Adversarial robustness literature:** Domain adaptation, test-time adaptation — is the problem formulation novel?\n- **Conditional computation:** The soft routing over LoRA experts is essentially conditional computation — is this acknowledged?\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focused on NOVELTY/ORIGINALITY and RELATED WORK POSITIONING. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on novelty, conceptual contribution, positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on originality concerns, missing related work, overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Scoring** — provide individual scores for:\n   - Originality (1-10)\n   - Related work coverage (1-10)\n   - Significance of contribution (1-10)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is routing by perturbation type genuinely a new idea or a straightforward application of MoE to a new axis? Is the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) surprising or expected? How does this compare to domain-adaptive methods in computer vision? Is the positioning against VLA-GSE fair given different backbones? Are there important missing references (test-time adaptation, domain-specific adapters, conditional batch normalization, etc.)?","agentId":"a8bed8bbd0bc630ea","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review as Reviewer R2 (Novelty/Originality and Related Work/Positioning).\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer R2 Review: Meta-Brain (CoRL 2026)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nMeta-Brain proposes routing frozen VLA activations through a lightweight MLP classifier (PAR) to select among three perturbation-type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), achieving 72.0% on LIBERO-Plus with ~12M parameters (+5.6pp over a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA, p=0.008). The paper's core empirical observation is that frozen PaliGemma layer-6 activations encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.996), and this signal can drive meaningful expert routing. The ablation ladder is thorough, the random-routing null result is well-designed, and cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA provides some generality evidence.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. The random routing null result is the paper's strongest single contribution.** Random routing (64.2%) performs *worse* than the parameter-matched universal r48 (66.4%), definitively ruling out the ensemble hypothesis. This is the critical experiment that many MoE papers omit, and it cleanly establishes that routing quality -- not expert diversity -- drives Meta-Brain's gains. This control alone elevates the experimental design above most submissions in this space.\n\n**S2. The routing-benefit-by-type analysis provides genuine insight.** The gradient of routing benefit (Visual +5.4pp > Spatial +4.9pp > Physical +2.4pp) with the honest acknowledgment that Physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation level is scientifically informative. The anti-correlation between routing entropy and downstream success (0.41/0.52/0.72 entropy vs. 75.5/69.2/61.0 success) is a clean result that helps the reader understand when and why routing helps.\n\n**S3. The parameter-matched comparison is the right experiment.** The rank-48 universal LoRA at ~12M params is the existential control, and the +5.6pp gap (p=0.008) is statistically significant. This directly addresses the concern that routing benefit could be confounded with parameter count -- a weakness that sank the first dream review.\n\n**S4. Cross-backbone validation adds credibility to the routing principle.** Phase 0 classification replicates on OpenVLA (F1=0.94) with the optimal layer shifting predictably (layer 8 vs. layer 6), and the routing benefit (+5.6pp over universal) is consistent. While limited to two backbones, this is more cross-architecture evidence than most comparable papers provide.\n\n**S5. The layer placement ablation provides mechanistic grounding.** The V-to-early, S-to-mid pattern aligns with intuitive VLA processing stages and adds interpretive depth beyond a pure systems contribution. This is a useful observation for practitioners, even if it lacks causal attribution.\n\n**S6. Honest treatment of limitations.** The paper acknowledges the -2.0pp clean LIBERO degradation, the weak Physical routing benefit, the 5.0pp oracle gap, and the closed-set taxonomy limitation. The CM module is correctly relegated to an optional ablation after borderline significance (p=0.12). This calibrated presentation builds trust.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) The architecture is a standard composition of known components with no new mechanism.** The PAR router is a 2-layer MLP with softmax output -- standard MoE gating. The LoRA expert bank with weighted summation (`h_corrected = h_frozen + Sigma_k w_k * LoRA_k(h_frozen)`) is architecturally identical to LoRAMoE (Li et al., 2024), MoLoRA (Zadouri et al., 2024), MixLoRA (Li et al., 2024), and MOELoRA (Liu et al., 2023). The only distinguishing element is that routing is conditioned on *perturbation type* rather than task identity or input tokens. This is an application-level design choice -- choosing which signal to feed into an existing architectural template -- not a methodological advance. The paper needs to explicitly engage with this and argue why \"perturbation type as routing dimension\" constitutes more than a straightforward instantiation of MoE-LoRA applied to a new axis.\n\n**W2. (Major) Severely incomplete engagement with the MoE-LoRA and adapter composition literature.** The median paper cites only 4 references. Even the best-scenario paper cites only 24. Critical missing works include:\n\n- *Mixture-of-LoRA methods*: LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA, LoRA-Switch -- all implement the same weighted-sum-of-LoRA-experts formula with learned gating\n- *Adapter composition*: AdapterFusion (Pfeiffer et al., 2021), Poly (Ponti et al., 2023), AdaMix (Wang et al., 2022) -- explore learned adapter combination in NLP\n- *Test-time adaptation*: TENT (Wang et al., 2021), TTT (Sun et al., 2020), MEMO (Zhang et al., 2022) -- handle distribution shift without pre-defined taxonomies\n- *Domain-adaptive methods*: Conditional Batch Normalization (de Vries et al., 2017), domain-specific adapters (Rebuffi et al., 2017, 2018), FiLM (Perez et al., 2018) -- condition network behavior on domain/environment signals\n- *Probing foundations*: Alain & Bengio (2017), Belinkov (2022 survey)\n- *MoE fundamentals*: Shazeer et al. (2017) -- the original sparsely-gated MoE\n\nWithout engaging this literature, the reader cannot assess what Meta-Brain adds beyond \"LoRAMoE but with perturbation-type features as the gating signal.\" The bibliography gap suggests insufficient literature engagement, which is a serious concern for novelty evaluation.\n\n**W3. (Major) Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) is confirmatory, not surprising.** Different perturbation types produce categorically different observation changes -- camera jitter alters pixel statistics differently from object layout randomization, which is different from robot pose changes. It would be *more* surprising if a frozen vision-language model's intermediate activations failed to distinguish these. SAFE already demonstrated that VLA activations encode execution-relevant anomaly state. Multiple probing studies on vision transformers show that intermediate layers encode domain/distribution information. Framing this as a standalone contribution (Contribution 1 in the paper) overstates its novelty. It should be presented as a prerequisite observation -- necessary for the method to work, but not itself a discovery.\n\n**W4. (Major) The closed-set 3-class taxonomy is a fundamental limitation insufficiently addressed.** The taxonomy is inherited directly from LIBERO-Plus's perturbation categories, not derived from representational analysis (e.g., unsupervised clustering of layer-6 activations to verify the 3-class structure emerges naturally). No evaluation is provided for:\n\n- *Compound perturbations*: Real deployment involves co-occurring perturbations (e.g., lighting change + object displacement). The near-one-hot routing weights (0.67-0.82) suggest the system cannot effectively blend corrections for compound scenarios.\n- *Open-set perturbations*: Novel perturbation types not in the taxonomy. The entropy-gated fallback is proposed but never evaluated -- does high entropy actually emerge on OOD perturbations that require correction?\n- *Continuous perturbation severity*: The same perturbation type at different severity levels may require different correction magnitudes, but the routing weights are trained on binary present/absent perturbation labels.\n\nFor a robotics venue where deployment conditions are inherently unpredictable, this closed-world assumption is a significant practical limitation.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) The comparison with VLA-GSE is misleading despite the caveat.** The parameter efficiency table includes VLA-GSE (114M params on Qwen3-VL-4B) alongside Meta-Brain (12M on pi0), computing a gain-per-parameter ratio across different backbones. The \"indirect comparison\" footnote does not prevent readers from taking the 6x efficiency ratio at face value. More fundamentally, VLA-GSE's capacity routing and Meta-Brain's perturbation-type routing address different aspects of the problem. The paper claims these are \"orthogonal\" but provides no composability experiment (e.g., Meta-Brain routing + VLA-GSE capacity scaling). Without such evidence, the orthogonality claim is unsupported.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) Missing engagement with test-time adaptation (TTA) literature.** TTA methods (TENT, TTT, MEMO, DUA) address distribution-shift adaptation without pre-defined taxonomies -- they adapt to whatever distribution shift is present at test time. The paper does not discuss why explicit categorical routing is preferable to taxonomy-free adaptation. This is a natural comparison for any robotics reviewer to ask about, since TTA avoids the open-set problem entirely. The absence of this discussion is a notable gap.\n\n**W7. (Moderate) Missing engagement with domain-adaptive conditioning methods.** Conditional Batch Normalization, FiLM layers, domain-specific residual adapters, and modular networks all condition network computation on a detected domain/environment signal -- conceptually very similar to Meta-Brain's approach of conditioning LoRA expert selection on a detected perturbation type. The connection to this well-established literature in computer vision should be discussed.\n\n**W8. (Moderate) The \"perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension\" framing overclaims.** The paper positions perturbation-type routing as orthogonal to capacity routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). But the architectural mechanism is identical to task-based routing -- only the *label* driving the routing differs (perturbation type vs. task identity). Calling this a new \"design dimension\" when the architectural template is unchanged overstates the conceptual contribution. A more honest framing would be: \"We identify perturbation type as a more informative routing signal than task identity for VLA robustness.\"\n\n**W9. (Minor) The \"overall\" metric is biased toward visual perturbations.** 4 of 6 perturbation subtypes are visual. The headline 72.0% gives disproportionate weight to visual performance. A category-balanced average (Visual 75.5, Spatial 69.2, Physical 61.0 -> balanced 68.6%) should be reported alongside the subtype-uniform average.\n\n**W10. (Minor) Training data asymmetry is uncontrolled.** LoRA-V trains on 4 visual subtypes; LoRA-S and LoRA-P each train on 1 subtype. The V > S > P performance gradient may partly reflect training data diversity rather than intrinsic perturbation difficulty or routing quality. This confound is not discussed.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **MoE-LoRA differentiation**: How does Meta-Brain differ architecturally from LoRAMoE or MoLoRA beyond the choice of routing signal? If the only difference is conditioning the gating MLP on frozen intermediate activations rather than input tokens, please justify why this constitutes a methodological contribution rather than a different instantiation of the same template.\n\n2. **SAFE extension**: Could SAFE's perturbation detection features be used directly to gate LoRA experts, producing a functionally equivalent system? What is the incremental contribution of Meta-Brain over \"SAFE detection + LoRA bank\"?\n\n3. **TTA comparison**: Why is explicit categorical routing preferable to test-time adaptation methods (TENT, TTT) that handle distribution shifts without pre-defined taxonomies? Have you considered a TTA baseline?\n\n4. **Compound perturbations**: What is Meta-Brain's behavior when two perturbation types co-occur (e.g., camera jitter + object layout change)? Does the near-one-hot routing prevent effective blending of corrections?\n\n5. **Open-set evaluation**: Has the entropy-gated fallback been tested on genuinely novel perturbation types (not just clean inputs)? What are the router outputs on, e.g., weather effects or sensor degradation modes not in the 3-class taxonomy?\n\n6. **Taxonomy derivation**: Have you performed unsupervised clustering (k-means, spectral) on layer-6 activations to verify that the 3-class structure emerges naturally rather than being imposed by the benchmark's perturbation categories?\n\n7. **Domain adaptation connection**: How does Meta-Brain relate to conditional batch normalization, FiLM, or domain-specific adapters -- all of which condition network behavior on a detected domain signal?\n\n8. **Training data volumes**: How many demonstrations per perturbation type? How is the LoRA-V (4 subtypes) vs. LoRA-S/LoRA-P (1 subtype each) data volume imbalance handled or controlled for?\n\n9. **Latency**: What is the wall-clock latency overhead of computing all 3 LoRA expert outputs at every timestep versus a single universal LoRA? For real-time robot control at 10+ Hz, this is a practical concern.\n\n10. **Rank sweep statistics**: Are the differences in routing benefit across ranks (5.6, 6.8, 7.8pp) individually significant, or could the \"monotonically increasing\" trend be within noise given 3 data points?\n\n### 5. Scoring\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Originality** | **4/10** | The architecture is a standard MoE-LoRA template (weighted sum of LoRA experts with learned gating) applied to a new routing signal (perturbation type). No new mechanism, loss function, training procedure, or architectural innovation. The Phase 0 finding is confirmatory. The contribution is at the application/design-choice level, not the methodological level. |\n| **Related work coverage** | **3/10** | Critical omission of the entire MoE-LoRA literature (LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA), adapter composition methods (AdapterFusion, Poly, AdaMix), test-time adaptation (TENT, TTT), domain-adaptive conditioning (CBN, FiLM), and probing foundations (Alain & Bengio). The median paper has 4 references; even the best scenario has only 24. CoRL papers typically cite 30-50. The missing references are not peripheral -- they implement the same architectural formula the paper uses. |\n| **Significance of contribution** | **6/10** | The empirical finding that perturbation-diagnostic routing outperforms parameter-matched uniform adaptation by +5.6pp (p=0.008) is a genuine and useful result for the VLA robustness community. The random routing null result and cross-backbone validation add real value. However, significance is limited by: (a) single simulated benchmark with synthetic perturbations, (b) closed-set taxonomy with no compound/open-set evaluation, (c) the contribution being an application insight rather than a generalizable method. |\n| **Overall recommendation** | **5/10** | Marginally below acceptance threshold. The experimental methodology is solid -- the ablation ladder, random routing control, parameter-matched baseline, and cross-backbone validation are all well-designed. However, the limited methodological novelty (standard MoE-LoRA template with a different gating signal), severely incomplete related work, and the gap between the closed-set evaluation and the \"general design principle\" framing keep this below the CoRL bar. The paper reads as a well-executed application study rather than a contribution that advances the community's understanding of VLA robustness mechanisms or adaptation architectures. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I am familiar with the MoE, LoRA, adapter composition, and domain adaptation literatures. The missing related work assessment is based on direct knowledge of these methods. My main uncertainty is whether CoRL 2026 would weight empirical rigor high enough to compensate for the novelty gap -- venue calibration could shift this by +/- 1 point. |\n\n### Summary Assessment\n\nThe paper's empirical execution is above average: the ablation ladder is exemplary, the random routing null result is the single most important control, and the cross-backbone validation supports generality. However, the core architecture is a standard composition of known components (MLP gating + weighted LoRA experts), and the only distinguishing element -- using perturbation type as the routing signal -- is an application-level design choice within an existing template. The severely incomplete bibliography (missing the entire MoE-LoRA literature that implements the same formula) prevents the reader from assessing incremental novelty. The closed-set taxonomy without compound or open-set evaluation limits practical significance for a robotics venue. If the authors (1) thoroughly engage with MoE-LoRA methods and explicitly argue differentiation, (2) add compound/open-set perturbation experiments, (3) discuss TTA and domain-adaptive conditioning as alternatives, and (4) reframe the contribution as an empirical systems study rather than a new \"design dimension,\" this could reach a clear accept at a future venue."}],"totalDurationMs":150914,"totalTokens":73916,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":69093,"output_tokens":4086,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4086,"cache_read_input_tokens":69093,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCoun