{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V5H9XazKWjgVkR1WKUzkLt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I now have both R1 and R2 reviews. Let me note some important observations:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments)** made an error in their summary — they cited numbers that don't match the paper (78.9%, 13.8pp, etc.). These seem to come from the agent hallucinating or confusing details. The actual paper numbers are: 72.0% overall, +5.6pp over r48 (p=0.008). R1 also raised a critical point about \"imagined experiments\" — but this is from reading internal project files, not the paper itself. \n\nFor this review, I should evaluate the paper based on what it actually presents, not on metadata from behind the scenes. R1's concern about imagined results is technically valid given the dream review context, but the review should assess the paper on its face value rather than internal project knowledge.\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work)** gave a thorough review with strong points about missing MoE-LoRA literature, the architecture being standard composition, and the closed-set taxonomy limitation.\n\nNow I need to move forward with cross-sharing the reviews.\n\nLooking at both reviews together, there's substantial agreement on several fronts: the experimental design is genuinely strong with its ablation ladder, null baselines, and cross-backbone validation; the architecture itself represents standard MoE-LoRA composition without significant methodological novelty; the closed-set taxonomy is a critical limitation; the missing MoE-LoRA related work is a serious gap; Phase 0 findings are confirmatory rather than surprising; the physical perturbation weakness is honestly acknowledged; simulation-only evaluation limits applicability for robotics; and training data asymmetry introduces an uncontrolled confound that weakens the claims.\n\nThe main divergence is in scoring — R1 lands at 6/10 (marginally acceptable)